(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e4a.30d22d8028.js"]={"37":[{"id":"sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the anatomy of the olfactory epithelium, olfactory receptor neurons, cribriform plate, olfactory bulb, glomeruli, mitral cells, tufted cells, and olfactory tract.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell type of the olfactory epithelium serves as the mitotically active progenitor that continuously regenerates olfactory receptor neurons?","choices":{"A":"Sustentacular cells, supporting cell type","B":"Bipolar cells, primary olfactory receptor neurons","C":"Basal cells, deep epithelial layer","D":"Microvillar cells, minor receptor cell type","E":"Bowman gland cells, mucus-secreting type"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Basal cells are undifferentiated stem cells at the base of the olfactory epithelium that undergo mitotic division to replace olfactory receptor neurons, which turn over every 30-60 days. Sustentacular cells provide structural support rather than regenerative capacity, bipolar cells are the postmitotic receptor neurons themselves, microvillar cells are a minor, poorly understood cell type, and Bowman gland cells secrete mucus rather than regenerating neurons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: olfactory receptor neurons are unique among CNS neurons for their continuous lifelong turnover from basal cell progenitors. COMLEX: this regenerative capacity is why partial post-viral or post-traumatic anosmia can sometimes improve over months.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — the cutaneous mechanoreceptor/proprioceptor classification framework, now paralleled by the distinct cell types of the olfactory epithelium and bulb","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the anatomy of the olfactory epithelium, olfactory receptor neurons, cribriform plate, olfactory bulb, glomeruli, mitral cells, tufted cells, and olfactory tract.","batch":1,"stem":"The unmyelinated axons of olfactory receptor neurons bundle into olfactory fila and pass through which skull structure to reach the olfactory bulb?","choices":{"A":"Foramen ovale of the sphenoid bone","B":"Internal acoustic meatus of the temporal bone","C":"Cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone","D":"Jugular foramen of the occipital bone","E":"Optic canal of the sphenoid bone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Olfactory fila, bundles of unmyelinated receptor neuron axons, pass through perforations in the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone to synapse in the overlying olfactory bulb. Foramen ovale transmits the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve, the internal acoustic meatus transmits CN VII and VIII, the jugular foramen transmits CN IX, X, and XI, and the optic canal transmits CN II, none of which are relevant to olfactory transmission.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cribriform plate is the anatomic bottleneck for olfactory fila, making it uniquely vulnerable to shearing injury in anterior skull base trauma. COMLEX: anterior skull base fractures through this region carry a risk of both anosmia and CSF rhinorrhea from dural tearing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — the cutaneous mechanoreceptor/proprioceptor classification framework, now paralleled by the distinct cell types of the olfactory epithelium and bulb","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the anatomy of the olfactory epithelium, olfactory receptor neurons, cribriform plate, olfactory bulb, glomeruli, mitral cells, tufted cells, and olfactory tract.","batch":1,"stem":"Within the olfactory bulb, axons of olfactory receptor neurons terminate exclusively within which spherical synaptic structures?","choices":{"A":"Mitral cell bodies of the mitral cell layer","B":"Granule cell bodies of the granule cell layer","C":"Periglomerular cell clusters of the outer layer","D":"Olfactory glomeruli of the glomerular layer","E":"Tufted cell bodies of the external plexiform layer"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Olfactory receptor neuron axons terminate exclusively within olfactory glomeruli, where they synapse onto the apical dendrites of mitral and tufted cells, with each glomerulus receiving input from neurons expressing a single receptor subtype. Mitral and tufted cell bodies reside in deeper layers and receive rather than provide this synaptic input, granule cells are interneurons without direct receptor neuron synapses, and periglomerular cells are adjacent interneurons rather than the primary axon termination site.","boardPrep":"Step 1: glomeruli are the single most distinctive feature of the olfactory bulb, functioning as the first site of synaptic convergence and receptor-subtype-specific organization. COMLEX: this glomerular convergence underlies the odor \"map\" that allows fine discrimination among thousands of distinct odorants.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — the cutaneous mechanoreceptor/proprioceptor classification framework, now paralleled by the distinct cell types of the olfactory epithelium and bulb","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace olfactory information from odorant binding to primary olfactory cortex and limbic-associated targets.","batch":1,"stem":"Axons of mitral and tufted cells exit the olfactory bulb and travel via which structure to reach primary olfactory cortex?","choices":{"A":"Medial olfactory tract, minor efferent bulb pathway","B":"Anterior commissure, interhemispheric pathway","C":"Fornix, hippocampal output pathway","D":"Stria terminalis, amygdalar output pathway","E":"Lateral olfactory tract, efferent bulb pathway"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The lateral olfactory tract carries the efferent axons of mitral and tufted cells caudally to terminate in the olfactory cortex, including the piriform cortex, olfactory tubercle, and amygdaloid structures. A discrete medial olfactory tract of this type does not carry the bulk of this efferent output, the anterior commissure carries interhemispheric olfactory fibers rather than the primary efferent pathway, and the fornix and stria terminalis are hippocampal and amygdalar output tracts unrelated to bulb efferents.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lateral olfactory tract is the sole efferent pathway carrying mitral/tufted cell output to olfactory cortex, analogous to the optic tract or medial lemniscus in other systems. COMLEX: lesions of the olfactory bulb or tract, as from an olfactory groove meningioma, can produce unilateral anosmia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 7 — hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate/parahippocampal regions in memory and emotion, now reached directly by olfactory tract projections without cortical relay","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace olfactory information from odorant binding to primary olfactory cortex and limbic-associated targets.","batch":1,"stem":"Which region is the principal primary olfactory cortex target receiving direct lateral olfactory tract input?","choices":{"A":"Piriform cortex, primary olfactory cortex","B":"Cingulate cortex, limbic association cortex","C":"Entorhinal cortex, hippocampal input cortex","D":"Orbitofrontal cortex, secondary olfactory cortex","E":"Insular cortex, gustatory association cortex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The piriform cortex is the largest and most direct target of lateral olfactory tract fibers and is considered the primary olfactory cortex, a form of three-layered paleocortex. The cingulate cortex is limbic association cortex rather than primary olfactory cortex, the entorhinal cortex is a secondary target more associated with the hippocampal memory pathway, the orbitofrontal cortex receives olfactory information secondarily via a thalamic relay, and the insular cortex is primarily gustatory association cortex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: piriform cortex is the primary olfactory cortex, the direct target of the lateral olfactory tract without any thalamic relay. COMLEX: lesions of piriform and adjacent structures can produce uncinate fits with olfactory auras in temporal lobe epilepsy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 7 — hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate/parahippocampal regions in memory and emotion, now reached directly by olfactory tract projections without cortical relay","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace olfactory information from odorant binding to primary olfactory cortex and limbic-associated targets.","batch":1,"stem":"Olfactory tract fibers project directly to which paired limbic structures important for emotional and memory associations with smell?","choices":{"A":"Thalamus and hypothalamus, diencephalic targets","B":"Caudate and putamen, striatal targets","C":"Amygdala and entorhinal cortex, limbic targets","D":"Globus pallidus and substantia nigra, pallidal targets","E":"Cerebellum and pons, infratentorial targets"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lateral olfactory tract fibers project directly to the amygdala, via the cortical and periamygdaloid nuclei, and directly to the entorhinal cortex, which then projects onward to the hippocampus, together providing the anatomic substrate for the strong emotional and memory associations characteristic of smell. The thalamus is notably bypassed by the primary olfactory pathway, the caudate and putamen are striatal motor structures, the globus pallidus and substantia nigra are basal nuclei output structures, and the cerebellum and pons are infratentorial structures unrelated to this limbic projection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: direct amygdalar and entorhinal cortical olfactory input, with the entorhinal cortex relaying onward to the hippocampus, explains why smells trigger unusually vivid emotional memories compared to other sensory modalities. COMLEX: this same anatomic substrate underlies olfactory auras and hallucinations in mesial temporal lobe epilepsy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 7 — hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate/parahippocampal regions in memory and emotion, now reached directly by olfactory tract projections without cortical relay","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The thalamus is notably bypass"}},{"id":"sdl37_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why olfaction is unique among sensory systems with respect to thalamic relay.","batch":1,"stem":"Unlike every other sensory modality, olfactory information reaches primary cortex without first relaying through which structure?","choices":{"A":"Hypothalamus, the autonomic relay structure","B":"Midbrain, the reflex relay structure","C":"Medulla, the visceral relay structure","D":"Cerebellum, the coordination relay structure","E":"Thalamus, the sensory relay structure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Olfaction is the sole sensory modality in which primary sensory information reaches cortex, the piriform cortex, without first passing through the thalamus, unlike vision, hearing, and somatosensation, which all obligately relay through a dedicated thalamic nucleus. The hypothalamus, midbrain, medulla, and cerebellum are not the thalamic relay bypassed by this rule and are not obligatory relay stations for other sensory pathways either.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"the only cranial nerve without thalamic relay to cortex\" is a First Aid-highlighted, frequently tested single fact about CN I. COMLEX: this direct route is why olfactory stimuli can trigger such immediate, unfiltered emotional and autonomic responses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — the rule that nearly every sensory pathway relays through the thalamus before cortex, now applied to olfaction as the sole exception","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The hypothalamus, midbrain, me","B":"The hypothalamus, midbrain, me","C":"The hypothalamus, midbrain, me","D":"The hypothalamus, midbrain, me"}},{"id":"sdl37_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why olfaction is unique among sensory systems with respect to thalamic relay.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cortical structure receives olfactory bulb output directly, without an obligatory thalamic relay?","choices":{"A":"Primary somatosensory cortex, parietal neocortex","B":"Primary visual cortex, occipital neocortex","C":"Piriform cortex, paleocortical target","D":"Primary auditory cortex, temporal neocortex","E":"Primary motor cortex, frontal neocortex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The piriform cortex receives direct lateral olfactory tract input from the olfactory bulb without any intervening thalamic synapse, the defining exception among primary sensory cortices. Primary somatosensory, visual, and auditory cortices all require an obligatory thalamic relay, through the VPL/VPM, lateral geniculate, and medial geniculate nuclei respectively, and primary motor cortex is not a sensory cortex at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: piriform cortex is the direct-access primary olfactory cortex, the anatomic basis for the \"no thalamic relay\" rule. COMLEX: this fact is frequently tested as a single best-answer discriminator among sensory pathway questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — the rule that nearly every sensory pathway relays through the thalamus before cortex, now applied to olfaction as the sole exception","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why olfaction is unique among sensory systems with respect to thalamic relay.","batch":1,"stem":"A secondary olfactory pathway that supports conscious discrimination and identification of odors does relay through which thalamic nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Ventral posterolateral nucleus of the thalamus","B":"Medial geniculate nucleus of the thalamus","C":"Ventral posteromedial nucleus of the thalamus","D":"Lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus","E":"Dorsomedial nucleus of the thalamus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Although the primary olfactory pathway bypasses the thalamus entirely, a secondary pathway from olfactory cortex does relay through the dorsomedial thalamic nucleus en route to orbitofrontal and insular cortex, supporting conscious discrimination and identification of odors. The ventral posterolateral and posteromedial nuclei relay body and facial somatosensory information respectively, the lateral geniculate nucleus relays vision, and the medial geniculate nucleus relays hearing, none of which are involved in this secondary olfactory pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the dorsomedial nucleus is the exception to the exception, the one thalamic nucleus that does carry olfactory information, specifically for discrimination rather than basic detection. COMLEX: lesions of this secondary pathway, as from a pituitary or hypothalamic mass, can impair odor identification while sparing odor detection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — the rule that nearly every sensory pathway relays through the thalamus before cortex, now applied to olfaction as the sole exception","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify major causes and lesion localizations for anosmia, hyposmia, parosmia, phantosmia, and olfactory hallucinations.","batch":1,"stem":"Complete loss of the sense of smell is termed which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Hyposmia, decreased sensitivity to odorants","B":"Parosmia, distorted odor perception","C":"Phantosmia, odor hallucination without stimulus","D":"Hyperosmia, increased olfactory acuity","E":"Anosmia, complete loss of smell"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Anosmia refers specifically to the complete loss of the sense of smell, most commonly from nasal obstruction, upper respiratory infection, or head trauma. Hyposmia describes a partial rather than complete reduction, parosmia and phantosmia describe distorted or hallucinatory perceptions rather than loss, and hyperosmia describes increased rather than decreased or absent olfactory acuity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: precisely distinguishing anosmia, hyposmia, parosmia, and phantosmia by definition is a frequently tested vocabulary set on boards. COMLEX: always clarify whether a patient means true smell loss versus flavor loss, since the two are commonly conflated clinically.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — the general cranial nerve lesion-localization framework, now applied specifically to CN I and the olfactory dysfunction spectrum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify major causes and lesion localizations for anosmia, hyposmia, parosmia, phantosmia, and olfactory hallucinations.","batch":1,"stem":"A distorted perception of an odor that is genuinely present, rather than absent altogether, is termed which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Anosmia, complete loss of smell","B":"Hyposmia, decreased sensitivity to odorants","C":"Phantosmia, odor hallucination without stimulus","D":"Parosmia, distorted odor perception","E":"Hyperosmia, increased olfactory acuity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parosmia, also called dysosmia, refers to a distorted perception of a real, present odor, such as a pleasant smell being perceived as foul. Anosmia and hyposmia describe absent or reduced perception rather than distortion, phantosmia describes perceiving an odor with no actual stimulus present rather than a distorted real one, and hyperosmia describes increased sensitivity rather than distorted quality.","boardPrep":"Step 1: parosmia requires a real odor stimulus that is misperceived, distinguishing it from phantosmia, which occurs with no stimulus at all. COMLEX: parosmia is a well-documented, often distressing feature of post-viral olfactory recovery, including after COVID-19.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — the general cranial nerve lesion-localization framework, now applied specifically to CN I and the olfactory dysfunction spectrum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Anosmia and hyposmia describe "}},{"id":"sdl37_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify major causes and lesion localizations for anosmia, hyposmia, parosmia, phantosmia, and olfactory hallucinations.","batch":1,"stem":"Perception of an odor in the complete absence of any odorant stimulus is termed which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Anosmia, complete loss of smell","B":"Hyposmia, generally decreased sensitivity to odorants","C":"Phantosmia, a hallucinated odor perception","D":"Parosmia, distorted odor perception","E":"Hyperosmia, increased olfactory acuity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Phantosmia, or olfactory hallucination, describes perceiving an odor when no odorant stimulus is actually present, classically arising from irritative lesions of the anterior medial temporal lobe, hippocampus, amygdala, or medial dorsal thalamic nucleus. Anosmia and hyposmia describe absent or reduced perception of real stimuli, parosmia describes distortion of a genuinely present odor rather than a hallucinated one, and hyperosmia describes increased rather than hallucinated sensitivity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: phantosmia occurring as a brief, stereotyped, unpleasant smell aura is a classic sign of temporal lobe seizure activity, the uncinate fit. COMLEX: new-onset phantosmia in an adult warrants evaluation for an underlying seizure focus or structural temporal lobe lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — the general cranial nerve lesion-localization framework, now applied specifically to CN I and the olfactory dysfunction spectrum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Anosmia and hyposmia describe ","B":"Anosmia and hyposmia describe ","E":"Anosmia and hyposmia describe "}},{"id":"sdl37_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the anatomy of taste buds and the distribution of taste fibers carried by CN VII, CN IX, and CN X.","batch":1,"stem":"Which taste bud cell type possesses G protein-coupled receptors for sweet, bitter, and umami compounds?","choices":{"A":"Type I cells, glial support cells","B":"Type III cells, presynaptic cells","C":"Type II cells, taste receptor cells","D":"Sustentacular cells, olfactory support cells","E":"Type IV cells, basal progenitor cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Type II, or receptor, cells possess the G protein-coupled receptors that transduce sweet, bitter, and umami taste qualities. Type I cells perform mainly glial-like supporting functions, type III cells are presynaptic cells that lack G protein-coupled taste receptors but transduce sour taste via acid-sensing channels, type IV cells are basal progenitor cells rather than mature transducers, and sustentacular cells belong to the olfactory rather than gustatory epithelium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: type II cells are the sweet/bitter/umami transducers, type III cells transduce sour via a distinct non-GPCR mechanism, a frequently tested taste bud cell-type distinction. COMLEX: this same GPCR-based sweet/bitter/umami transduction mechanism is exploited pharmacologically by artificial sweeteners and bitter-masking agents.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — somatic motor, branchial motor, parasympathetic, general sensory, special sensory, and visceral sensory CN component classification, now applied to the special visceral afferent taste fibers of CN VII, IX, and X","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the anatomy of taste buds and the distribution of taste fibers carried by CN VII, CN IX, and CN X.","batch":1,"stem":"Taste buds located on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue are found within which type of papillae?","choices":{"A":"Fungiform papillae, anterior tongue location","B":"Vallate papillae, posterior tongue location","C":"Foliate papillae, lateral tongue location","D":"Filiform papillae, nongustatory tongue papillae","E":"Palatal taste buds, extralingual location"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fungiform papillae, mushroom-shaped structures scattered among the more numerous filiform papillae, house the taste buds of the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and are innervated by the chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve. Vallate papillae are located posteriorly at the junction of the oral and pharyngeal tongue, foliate papillae are located along the lateral tongue margins, filiform papillae are nongustatory structures providing only mechanical texture, and palatal taste buds are an extralingual site rather than an anterior tongue structure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fungiform papillae, anterior two-thirds of tongue, chorda tympani/facial nerve is a tightly linked triad worth memorizing together for board vignettes. COMLEX: this anatomic fact directly explains the anterior tongue taste loss classically seen in chorda tympani or facial nerve injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — somatic motor, branchial motor, parasympathetic, general sensory, special sensory, and visceral sensory CN component classification, now applied to the special visceral afferent taste fibers of CN VII, IX, and X","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the anatomy of taste buds and the distribution of taste fibers carried by CN VII, CN IX, and CN X.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cranial nerve innervates taste buds in the vallate papillae via its lingual-tonsillar branch?","choices":{"A":"Facial nerve, cranial nerve VII","B":"Vagus nerve, cranial nerve X","C":"Glossopharyngeal nerve, cranial nerve IX","D":"Trigeminal nerve, cranial nerve V","E":"Hypoglossal nerve, purely motor cranial nerve XII"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The glossopharyngeal nerve, via its lingual-tonsillar branch, carries taste sensation from the vallate papillae and posterior clefts of the foliate papillae on the posterior third of the tongue. The facial nerve instead innervates the anterior two-thirds via the chorda tympani, the vagus nerve carries taste from the epiglottis and supraglottic region via the superior laryngeal nerve, the trigeminal nerve carries general rather than gustatory sensation, and the hypoglossal nerve is a purely motor nerve to tongue musculature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CN IX carries posterior third tongue taste via the lingual-tonsillar branch, completing the anterior CN VII / posterior CN IX taste division of the tongue. COMLEX: this same nerve mediates the afferent limb of the gag reflex, so CN IX lesions impair both taste and gag sensation on the affected side.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — somatic motor, branchial motor, parasympathetic, general sensory, special sensory, and visceral sensory CN component classification, now applied to the special visceral afferent taste fibers of CN VII, IX, and X","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace taste information from the tongue, palate, pharynx, and epiglottis to the nucleus solitarius, thalamus, and gustatory cortex.","batch":1,"stem":"First-order taste afferents carried by cranial nerves VII, IX, and X all terminate in which brainstem nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Rostral nucleus solitarius, gustatory nucleus","B":"Caudal nucleus solitarius, visceral nucleus","C":"Nucleus ambiguus, motor nucleus","D":"Dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, motor nucleus","E":"Spinal trigeminal nucleus, facial sensory nucleus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Taste afferents from CN VII, IX, and X converge on the rostral portion of the solitary nucleus, functionally termed the gustatory nucleus, which contains the majority of second-order taste neurons. The caudal solitary nucleus instead receives general visceral, cardiorespiratory, afferents from CN IX and X, nucleus ambiguus and the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus are motor rather than sensory nuclei, and the spinal trigeminal nucleus relays facial pain and temperature rather than taste.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rostral solitary nucleus equals gustatory nucleus, caudal solitary nucleus equals visceral/cardiorespiratory nucleus, a functional subdivision worth memorizing precisely. COMLEX: this rostral-caudal functional split within a single nucleus is a frequently tested example of regional specialization within one brainstem structure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — the medullary cranial nerve nuclei including the solitary nucleus, now detailed as the principal gustatory afferent relay","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace taste information from the tongue, palate, pharynx, and epiglottis to the nucleus solitarius, thalamus, and gustatory cortex.","batch":1,"stem":"Second-order gustatory neurons from the solitary nucleus ascend to synapse in which thalamic nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Ventral posterolateral nucleus, general body sensory part","B":"Ventral posteromedial nucleus, parvicellular part","C":"Dorsomedial nucleus, limbic association part","D":"Lateral geniculate nucleus, visual relay part","E":"Medial geniculate nucleus, auditory relay part"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Second-order gustatory neurons ascend via the ipsilateral central tegmental tract to synapse in the parvicellular division of the ventral posteromedial thalamic nucleus, medial to the facial somatosensory representation. The ventral posterolateral nucleus relays body rather than gustatory sensation, the dorsomedial nucleus carries the secondary olfactory rather than primary gustatory pathway, and the lateral and medial geniculate nuclei relay vision and hearing respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VPMpc is the dedicated gustatory thalamic relay, sitting immediately medial to the facial sensory VPM representation. COMLEX: unlike most sensory pathways, this taste pathway from solitary nucleus through VPMpc to cortex remains entirely ipsilateral rather than crossing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — the medullary cranial nerve nuclei including the solitary nucleus, now detailed as the principal gustatory afferent relay","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The ventral posterolateral nuc"}},{"id":"sdl37_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace taste information from the tongue, palate, pharynx, and epiglottis to the nucleus solitarius, thalamus, and gustatory cortex.","batch":1,"stem":"Third-order thalamic gustatory neurons project primarily to which cortical region for the discriminative aspects of taste?","choices":{"A":"Primary somatosensory cortex, postcentral gyrus","B":"Primary visual cortex, occipital pole","C":"Primary auditory cortex, superior temporal gyrus","D":"Primary motor cortex, precentral gyrus","E":"Anterior insular cortex and frontal operculum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"VPMpc neurons project through the posterior limb of the internal capsule to the anterior insular cortex and frontal operculum, as well as a rostral extension of Brodmann area 3b, together forming primary gustatory cortex responsible for discriminating taste qualities. Primary somatosensory, visual, and auditory cortices process other modalities entirely, and primary motor cortex is not a sensory processing region at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the anterior insula/frontal operculum is primary gustatory cortex, the final discriminative relay in the taste pathway. COMLEX: this same insular region integrates taste with autonomic and visceral sensation, contributing to nausea and interoceptive awareness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — the medullary cranial nerve nuclei including the solitary nucleus, now detailed as the principal gustatory afferent relay","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Primary somatosensory, visual,"}},{"id":"sdl37_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate true taste loss from perceived flavor loss caused by olfactory dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"Most patients who present complaining of loss of taste actually have dysfunction localized to which sensory system?","choices":{"A":"Gustatory system, not the olfactory system","B":"Olfactory system, not the gustatory system","C":"Trigeminal system, not the gustatory system","D":"Vestibular system, not the gustatory system","E":"Auditory system, not the gustatory system"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because most patients confuse taste with the broader multisensory experience of flavor, the majority of chemosensory complaints framed as \"loss of taste\" actually reflect olfactory rather than true gustatory dysfunction, since flavor depends heavily on retronasal smell. The gustatory system itself is intact in most such patients, and the trigeminal, vestibular, and auditory systems are not the source of taste-labeled complaints at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: always formally test both smell and the five basic tastes separately in a patient complaining of \"taste loss,\" since olfactory dysfunction is the far more common actual cause. COMLEX: this distinction changes the differential diagnosis and workup significantly, favoring nasal/sinus and CN I causes over CN VII/IX/X causes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 → Obj 8 — bedside sensory findings distinguishing DCML from anterolateral dysfunction, now paralleled by distinguishing true gustatory loss from olfactory-mediated flavor loss","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The gustatory system itself is"}},{"id":"sdl37_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate true taste loss from perceived flavor loss caused by olfactory dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"The combined multisensory experience integrating olfactory, gustatory, and somatosensory input from food is properly termed which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Taste, a purely gustatory experience","B":"Smell, a purely olfactory experience","C":"Texture, a purely tactile somatosensory experience","D":"Umami, a single basic taste quality","E":"Flavor, a multisensory integrated experience"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Flavor is the correct term for the integrated, multisensory experience combining retronasal olfaction, true gustation, and trigeminal somatosensory input, whereas taste refers narrowly to the five basic gustatory qualities detected by taste receptor cells. Smell and texture each describe only a single sensory component contributing to flavor, and umami is one of the five basic taste qualities rather than the integrated experience itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: precisely distinguishing \"taste\" from \"flavor\" is essential vocabulary for correctly interpreting chemosensory complaints on boards. COMLEX: patient-facing counseling about post-viral or post-traumatic \"taste loss\" should clarify this same distinction to set accurate recovery expectations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 → Obj 8 — bedside sensory findings distinguishing DCML from anterolateral dysfunction, now paralleled by distinguishing true gustatory loss from olfactory-mediated flavor loss","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Smell and texture each describ","C":"Smell and texture each describ"}},{"id":"sdl37_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate true taste loss from perceived flavor loss caused by olfactory dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient correctly identifies sweet, salty, sour, bitter, and umami on formal taste testing but reports that food tastes bland overall. This pattern most likely reflects dysfunction of which system?","choices":{"A":"Gustatory system, with intact olfaction","B":"Trigeminal system, with intact gustation","C":"Vestibular system, with intact gustation","D":"Olfactory system, with intact gustation","E":"Auditory system, with intact gustation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intact detection of all five basic taste qualities on formal testing confirms that the gustatory system itself is functioning normally, so the perceived blandness of food most likely reflects impaired retronasal olfaction, since most of the complex, food-identifying component of flavor depends on smell rather than basic taste. If the gustatory system were impaired instead, basic taste testing would be abnormal, and the trigeminal, vestibular, and auditory systems are not primary contributors to this specific complaint pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal formal taste testing with a complaint of blandness is the classic pattern confirming olfactory rather than gustatory dysfunction as the true cause. COMLEX: this stepwise testing approach, taste first, then smell, is the correct clinical algorithm for chemosensory complaints.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 → Obj 8 — bedside sensory findings distinguishing DCML from anterolateral dysfunction, now paralleled by distinguishing true gustatory loss from olfactory-mediated flavor loss","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"If the gustatory system were i"}},{"id":"sdl37_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical features to localize lesions involving CN I, chorda tympani, CN IX, CN X, nucleus solitarius, thalamus, or gustatory cortex.","batch":1,"stem":"Loss of taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue accompanied by ipsilateral facial paralysis and hyperacusis localizes to a lesion of which structure?","choices":{"A":"Glossopharyngeal nerve root or lingual branch","B":"Vagus nerve root or superior laryngeal branch","C":"Facial nerve root, proximal to the stapedius branch","D":"Trigeminal nerve root or lingual branch","E":"Hypoglossal nerve root or fascicles"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This combination, anterior two-thirds taste loss, facial paralysis, and hyperacusis from stapedius paralysis, localizes to the facial nerve root proximal to the nerve to stapedius and the chorda tympani, since only there do all three functions travel together; a chorda tympani lesion alone would spare facial movement and hearing. The glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves carry posterior tongue and laryngeal taste respectively rather than anterior tongue taste, the trigeminal nerve carries general rather than gustatory or facial motor function, and the hypoglossal nerve is purely motor to the tongue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the combined triad of anterior tongue taste loss, facial weakness, and hyperacusis is the classic localizing pattern for a facial nerve root lesion, as in Bell palsy. COMLEX: identifying where along the facial nerve course the lesion sits, proximal versus distal to chorda tympani branching, determines whether taste is actually affected.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — cranial nerve findings localizing lesions to brainstem, cavernous sinus, internal acoustic meatus, or jugular foramen, now applied to the chemosensory pathway specifically","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical features to localize lesions involving CN I, chorda tympani, CN IX, CN X, nucleus solitarius, thalamus, or gustatory cortex.","batch":1,"stem":"Isolated loss of taste from taste buds on the epiglottis and upper esophagus localizes to a lesion of which nerve branch?","choices":{"A":"Chorda tympani of the facial nerve","B":"Lingual-tonsillar branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve","C":"Superior laryngeal branch of the vagus nerve","D":"Greater superficial petrosal branch of the facial nerve","E":"Recurrent laryngeal branch of the vagus nerve"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Taste buds on the epiglottis and esophagus are innervated by the superior laryngeal nerve, a branch of the vagus, whose taste cell bodies lie in the nodose ganglion. The chorda tympani supplies fungiform papillae of the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, the lingual-tonsillar branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve supplies the vallate and posterior foliate papillae, and the greater superficial petrosal nerve supplies the soft palate. The recurrent laryngeal nerve is motor to most laryngeal muscles and carries no taste.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Taste map: anterior two-thirds of tongue CN VII (chorda tympani), soft palate CN VII (greater petrosal), posterior third CN IX, epiglottis and esophagus CN X (superior laryngeal). COMLEX: All three nerves end in the rostral, gustatory part of the solitary nucleus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — cranial nerve findings localizing lesions to brainstem, cavernous sinus, internal acoustic meatus, or jugular foramen, now applied to the chemosensory pathway specifically","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical features to localize lesions involving CN I, chorda tympani, CN IX, CN X, nucleus solitarius, thalamus, or gustatory cortex.","batch":1,"stem":"Isolated loss of smell without any accompanying taste or facial motor deficit localizes to a lesion of which cranial nerve?","choices":{"A":"Facial nerve, cranial nerve VII","B":"Glossopharyngeal nerve, cranial nerve IX","C":"Vagus nerve, cranial nerve X","D":"Olfactory nerve, cranial nerve I","E":"Trigeminal nerve, cranial nerve V"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Isolated anosmia without taste or facial motor involvement localizes to the olfactory nerve or its central pathway, since CN I is anatomically and functionally distinct from the taste-carrying cranial nerves VII, IX, and X. Lesions of the facial, glossopharyngeal, or vagus nerves would instead be expected to produce taste loss in their respective tongue or pharyngeal distributions, and trigeminal nerve lesions produce facial sensory rather than olfactory deficits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: isolated anosmia points to CN I or its central olfactory pathway, distinct from the taste-carrying nerves, a key localization distinction. COMLEX: always screen for accompanying visual or facial findings, since anosmia with optic involvement suggests an anterior skull base or olfactory groove mass rather than isolated CN I injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — cranial nerve findings localizing lesions to brainstem, cavernous sinus, internal acoustic meatus, or jugular foramen, now applied to the chemosensory pathway specifically","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q25","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Connect olfaction and taste to feeding behavior, salivation, nausea, autonomic responses, and limbic memory/emotion.","batch":1,"stem":"Olfactory cortex projections that are important for feeding behavior terminate primarily in which hypothalamic region?","choices":{"A":"Lateral hypothalamus, orexin-producing area","B":"Suprachiasmatic nucleus, circadian nucleus","C":"Paraventricular nucleus, hormone-synthesizing nucleus","D":"Supraoptic nucleus, hormone-synthesizing nucleus","E":"Ventromedial nucleus, satiety-related nucleus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Olfactory cortex, primarily the piriform cortex and anterior olfactory nucleus, sends direct projections to the lateral hypothalamus, an area classically associated with driving feeding behavior. The suprachiasmatic nucleus governs circadian rhythm rather than feeding, the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei synthesize oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone rather than mediating olfactory-driven feeding, and the ventromedial nucleus is more classically linked to satiety signaling than to this specific olfactory input.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lateral hypothalamus is the feeding-drive center receiving direct olfactory cortical input, linking smell to appetite and food-seeking behavior. COMLEX: this pathway is part of the physiologic basis for why appealing food smells can stimulate appetite even before any food is consumed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 6 — the hypothalamus coordinating autonomic, endocrine, behavioral, and emotional responses, now linked to olfactory and gustatory input","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q26","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Connect olfaction and taste to feeding behavior, salivation, nausea, autonomic responses, and limbic memory/emotion.","batch":1,"stem":"Taste-evoked signals relayed to medullary reflex circuits help mediate which of the following autonomic responses?","choices":{"A":"Salivary secretion and swallowing reflexes","B":"Pupillary constriction and accommodation reflexes","C":"Heart rate and blood pressure reflexes alone","D":"Respiratory rate and depth reflexes alone","E":"Bladder emptying and continence reflexes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Taste information relayed from the solitary nucleus to adjacent medullary reflex circuits helps drive salivary secretion and coordinated swallowing in response to food in the mouth, an important digestive reflex function. Pupillary and accommodation reflexes are mediated by the pupillary light reflex circuit rather than taste, heart rate/blood pressure and respiratory reflexes are driven by baroreceptor and chemoreceptor afferents rather than taste, and bladder reflexes are unrelated to gustatory input.","boardPrep":"Step 1: taste-triggered salivary and swallowing reflexes are a direct, testable functional consequence of the solitary nucleus's central position in digestive reflex circuitry. COMLEX: this same reflex circuitry is clinically relevant in dysphagia evaluation, where taste-related reflex triggering can be part of swallow therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 6 — the hypothalamus coordinating autonomic, endocrine, behavioral, and emotional responses, now linked to olfactory and gustatory input","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q27","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Connect olfaction and taste to feeding behavior, salivation, nausea, autonomic responses, and limbic memory/emotion.","batch":1,"stem":"The projection from entorhinal cortex that links olfactory input to centers of learning and behavior terminates in which structure?","choices":{"A":"Amygdala, primarily emotional processing structure","B":"Thalamus, sensory relay structure","C":"Cerebellum, coordination structure","D":"Hippocampus, memory and learning structure","E":"Striatum, motor selection structure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The entorhinal cortex, itself part of the olfactory cortex, projects to the hippocampus, linking olfactory input directly to structures responsible for learning and memory formation. The amygdala receives a separate direct olfactory projection more associated with emotional rather than mnemonic processing, the thalamus is largely bypassed by the primary olfactory pathway, and the cerebellum and striatum are motor coordination and selection structures unrelated to this specific olfactory-memory link.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the entorhinal-to-hippocampal projection is the anatomic substrate for why smells so reliably trigger detailed autobiographical memories. COMLEX: this same entorhinal-hippocampal circuit is among the earliest structures affected in Alzheimer disease, paralleling the early olfactory decline seen in that condition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 6 — the hypothalamus coordinating autonomic, endocrine, behavioral, and emotional responses, now linked to olfactory and gustatory input","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q28","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Apply the pathways to board-style questions involving head trauma, skull base tumors, Bell palsy, lateral medullary syndrome, temporal lobe seizures, and neurodegenerative disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Shearing of olfactory fila at the cribriform plate following head trauma classically produces which finding?","choices":{"A":"Anosmia, traumatic olfactory nerve injury","B":"Ageusia, traumatic gustatory nerve injury","C":"Diplopia, traumatic oculomotor nerve injury","D":"Dysarthria, traumatic hypoglossal nerve injury","E":"Hyperacusis, traumatic facial nerve injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Head trauma, particularly with anterior skull base or frontal bone involvement, can shear the thin olfactory fila as they cross the cribriform plate, producing anosmia that may be temporary or permanent. Ageusia, diplopia, dysarthria, and hyperacusis reflect injury to entirely different cranial nerves, gustatory nerves, CN III, CN XII, and CN VII respectively, none of which is the classic finding from cribriform plate shearing injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: traumatic anosmia from cribriform plate/olfactory fila shearing is a classic, frequently tested consequence of head trauma, especially frontal impacts. COMLEX: always screen for accompanying signs of anterior skull base fracture, such as CSF rhinorrhea or periorbital ecchymosis, in a patient with new post-traumatic anosmia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 7-8 — medullary vascular territories and lateral medullary syndrome, now including its chemosensory component alongside other named olfactory/gustatory clinical syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q29","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Apply the pathways to board-style questions involving head trauma, skull base tumors, Bell palsy, lateral medullary syndrome, temporal lobe seizures, and neurodegenerative disease.","batch":1,"stem":"An olfactory groove meningioma compressing the optic nerve, producing anosmia, ipsilateral optic atrophy, and contralateral papilledema, describes which named syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Horner syndrome, sympathetic chain lesion","B":"Foster Kennedy syndrome, anterior skull base mass","C":"Weber syndrome, midbrain lesion","D":"Parinaud syndrome, dorsal midbrain compressive lesion","E":"Millard-Gubler syndrome, pontine lesion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Foster Kennedy syndrome describes exactly this combination, ipsilateral anosmia and optic atrophy from direct compression by an olfactory groove or frontal lobe mass, together with contralateral papilledema from raised intracranial pressure. Horner syndrome involves oculosympathetic findings rather than anosmia and optic atrophy, Weber syndrome is a crossed midbrain motor syndrome, Parinaud syndrome involves dorsal midbrain vertical gaze findings, and Millard-Gubler syndrome is a crossed pontine syndrome, none matching this specific anosmia-optic-papilledema triad.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Foster Kennedy syndrome, ipsilateral optic atrophy plus anosmia plus contralateral papilledema, is a classic named finding for anterior skull base or olfactory groove masses. COMLEX: recognizing this triad should prompt urgent neuroimaging given the underlying mass lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 7-8 — medullary vascular territories and lateral medullary syndrome, now including its chemosensory component alongside other named olfactory/gustatory clinical syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q30","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Apply the pathways to board-style questions involving head trauma, skull base tumors, Bell palsy, lateral medullary syndrome, temporal lobe seizures, and neurodegenerative disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Uncinate fits, characterized by unpleasant olfactory auras, classically arise from irritative seizure foci in which brain region?","choices":{"A":"Occipital pole and visual cortex","B":"Precentral gyrus and motor cortex","C":"Postcentral gyrus and sensory cortex","D":"Uncus and medial temporal lobe","E":"Cerebellar vermis and flocculonodular lobe"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Uncinate fits arise from irritative focal seizure activity in the uncus and adjacent medial temporal lobe structures, producing disagreeable olfactory auras, cacosmia, due to their proximity to primary and limbic olfactory structures. The occipital pole and visual cortex would instead produce visual rather than olfactory auras, the precentral and postcentral gyri would produce motor or sensory rather than olfactory symptoms, and the cerebellar vermis and flocculonodular lobe are unrelated to seizure-generated sensory auras.","boardPrep":"Step 1: uncinate fits localize seizure activity to the medial temporal lobe/uncus, a classic olfactory-aura seizure semiology tested on boards. COMLEX: these auras can occur in patients with tumor, vascular disease, trauma, infection, or a genetic predisposition to seizures, making a broad differential appropriate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 7-8 — medullary vascular territories and lateral medullary syndrome, now including its chemosensory component alongside other named olfactory/gustatory clinical syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q31","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the anatomy of the olfactory epithelium, olfactory receptor neurons, cribriform plate, olfactory bulb, glomeruli, mitral cells, tufted cells, and olfactory tract.","batch":2,"stem":"A pathologist examining the olfactory bulb identifies small interneurons in its deepest cellular layer. They have no axons, and their only output is through dendrodendritic synapses onto the secondary dendrites of mitral and tufted cells, providing lateral and feedback inhibition. Which cell type and transmitter are these?","choices":{"A":"Periglomerular cells releasing glutamate","B":"Granule cells releasing GABA","C":"Tufted cells releasing glutamate","D":"Sustentacular cells releasing glycine","E":"Basal cells releasing acetylcholine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Granule cells in the granular cell layer are the principal interneurons of the olfactory bulb; they lack axons and act through reciprocal dendrodendritic GABAergic synapses on mitral and tufted cell dendrites, shutting down bulb output through feedback inhibition. Periglomerular cells sit beside the glomeruli and are also GABAergic onto mitral cells, so pairing them with glutamate is wrong, and their location is superficial. Tufted cells are excitatory efferent neurons that send axons out of the bulb, sustentacular cells are supporting cells of the epithelium, and basal cells are epithelial stem cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Olfactory bulb: receptor axons excite mitral and tufted cells in glomeruli (glutamate); periglomerular and granule cells inhibit them (GABA). COMLEX: The bulb is a layered forebrain structure on the cribriform plate, so lesions here cause ipsilateral anosmia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — olfactory epithelium and bulb anatomy, applied here to the inhibitory circuitry of the bulb","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Tufted cells are excitatory ef"}},{"id":"sdl37_q32","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the anatomy of the olfactory epithelium, olfactory receptor neurons, cribriform plate, olfactory bulb, glomeruli, mitral cells, tufted cells, and olfactory tract.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman undergoes biopsy of nasal mucosa as part of a research protocol studying olfactory epithelium turnover. The pathologist notes ongoing mitotic activity replenishing sensory neurons throughout adulthood. Which epithelial cell type is responsible for this regenerative capacity?","choices":{"A":"Sustentacular cells, non-mitotic support population","B":"Bipolar receptor neurons, postmitotic population","C":"Basal cells, mitotic progenitor population","D":"Microvillar cells, minor postmitotic population","E":"Bowman gland cells, secretory non-mitotic population"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Basal cells serve as the stem cell population of the olfactory epithelium, undergoing continuous mitotic division to regenerate olfactory receptor neurons throughout life, an unusual capability among central nervous system-derived neurons. Sustentacular cells provide structural and secretory support rather than division, bipolar receptor neurons and microvillar cells are postmitotic differentiated cells rather than the dividing progenitor, and Bowman gland cells are secretory rather than regenerative.","boardPrep":"Step 1: basal cells are the stem cell source enabling lifelong olfactory receptor neuron turnover, a distinctive and testable feature of this sensory system. COMLEX: this regenerative capacity is clinically relevant to counseling patients about the potential, though not guaranteed, for partial recovery from post-viral or post-traumatic anosmia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — olfactory epithelium cell types, applied to a regenerative biology vignette","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q33","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the anatomy of the olfactory epithelium, olfactory receptor neurons, cribriform plate, olfactory bulb, glomeruli, mitral cells, tufted cells, and olfactory tract.","batch":2,"stem":"A neuroanatomy student examines a histologic section of the olfactory bulb and is asked to identify the layer containing the largest, most conspicuous cell bodies, whose primary dendrites arborize within olfactory glomeruli. Which cell layer is being examined?","choices":{"A":"Mitral cell layer, principal efferent layer","B":"Granule cell layer, principal interneuron layer","C":"Glomerular layer, primary afferent synaptic layer","D":"External plexiform layer, dendritic layer","E":"Olfactory nerve layer, primary afferent axon layer"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The mitral cell layer contains the large, distinctive somata of mitral cells, the principal efferent projection neurons of the olfactory bulb whose primary apical dendrites extend into and arborize within individual glomeruli. The granule cell layer instead contains small, axon-lacking interneurons, the glomerular layer contains the actual glomerular synaptic structures rather than mitral cell bodies, the external plexiform layer contains dendrites and tufted cell somata rather than mitral cell bodies specifically, and the olfactory nerve layer contains incoming primary afferent axons rather than mitral cell somata.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the mitral cell layer is identifiable by its large, sparse, conspicuous cell bodies, a classic histology identification point. COMLEX: mitral cells, together with tufted cells, constitute the sole efferent output of the olfactory bulb, making their identification clinically relevant to understanding olfactory bulb lesion effects.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — olfactory bulb laminar histology, applied to a cell-layer identification vignette","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q34","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace olfactory information from odorant binding to primary olfactory cortex and limbic-associated targets.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher records extracellular activity from a single olfactory receptor neuron axon as it exits the cribriform plate and enters the skull. Following this axon centrally, the recording electrode would next detect activity within which structure?","choices":{"A":"Piriform cortex of the ipsilateral hemisphere","B":"Dorsomedial thalamic nucleus contralaterally","C":"Anterior olfactory nucleus contralaterally","D":"Olfactory glomeruli of the ipsilateral bulb","E":"Orbitofrontal cortex of the ipsilateral hemisphere"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Immediately after crossing the cribriform plate, olfactory receptor neuron axons terminate within olfactory glomeruli of the ipsilateral olfactory bulb, their first and only synaptic target before any further central relay. Piriform cortex, the dorsomedial thalamic nucleus, and orbitofrontal cortex are all further downstream targets reached only after the glomerular synapse and subsequent processing, and the anterior olfactory nucleus, while an early target, is not the axon's first termination site, and the pathway at this early stage remains ipsilateral rather than crossing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tracing the olfactory pathway step by step, glomerulus first, then piriform cortex, then optionally dorsomedial thalamus, is a useful way to master this system's unique organization. COMLEX: recognizing exactly where in this sequence a lesion occurs determines the resulting clinical deficit pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — sequential olfactory pathway tracing, applied to an electrophysiology vignette","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q35","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace olfactory information from odorant binding to primary olfactory cortex and limbic-associated targets.","batch":2,"stem":"In a research mouse, the olfactory-specific G protein in the cilia of olfactory receptor neurons is knocked out. Odorants still bind their receptors, but the cells fail to depolarize. Which downstream step is most directly lost?","choices":{"A":"Activation of adenylyl cyclase, so cAMP cannot open cyclic nucleotide-gated cation channels","B":"Opening of amiloride-sensitive sodium channels in the apical membrane of the receptor neuron","C":"Release of ATP through gap junction hemichannels onto adjacent presynaptic cells","D":"Closing of apical voltage-sensitive potassium channels by protons from the odorant","E":"Transport of the odorant across the mucus by water-soluble odorant-binding proteins"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Odorant receptors are G protein-coupled receptors; the olfactory-specific G protein activates adenylyl cyclase, and the rise in ciliary cAMP opens cyclic nucleotide-gated cation channels, producing the generator potential, so loss of the G protein abolishes this step. Amiloride-sensitive sodium channels, ATP release through hemichannels, and proton block of apical potassium channels are mechanisms of taste transduction rather than olfaction. Odorant-binding proteins act before receptor binding, which the stem says still occurs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Olfactory transduction: odorant, G protein-coupled receptor, olfactory G protein, adenylyl cyclase, cAMP, cation channel opening, depolarization; an IP3 pathway may act in parallel. COMLEX: Each receptor neuron expresses one odorant receptor type, which gives the epithelium a receptor map.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — slower metabotropic G protein-coupled signaling, applied here to olfactory transduction","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Odorant-binding proteins act b"}},{"id":"sdl37_q36","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace olfactory information from odorant binding to primary olfactory cortex and limbic-associated targets.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman reports that certain smells trigger vivid, intrusive memories of a traumatic event. This strong olfactory-memory association is best explained by projections from the olfactory tract that ultimately reach which structure?","choices":{"A":"Cerebellum via the middle cerebellar peduncle","B":"Hippocampus via the entorhinal cortex","C":"Basal ganglia via the striatum directly","D":"Thalamus via the ventral posteromedial nucleus","E":"Brainstem via the reticular formation directly"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Olfactory tract fibers project to the entorhinal cortex, which in turn projects to the hippocampus, providing a close, short anatomic link between olfactory stimuli and memory encoding and retrieval circuits, explaining the vivid, intrusive quality of odor-triggered memories. The cerebellum, basal ganglia, and brainstem reticular formation are not part of this specific olfactory-memory pathway, and the ventral posteromedial thalamic nucleus is the gustatory rather than olfactory-memory relay.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the entorhinal-hippocampal projection is the anatomic substrate for the well-known phenomenon of powerful, involuntary odor-triggered memories. COMLEX: this same circuit's vulnerability in trauma and stress-related disorders is relevant to understanding olfactory triggers in post-traumatic stress presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — olfactory-limbic memory projections, applied to a clinical psychiatry-adjacent vignette","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The cerebellum, basal ganglia,"}},{"id":"sdl37_q37","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why olfaction is unique among sensory systems with respect to thalamic relay.","batch":2,"stem":"A student learns that the dorsomedial thalamic nucleus relays olfactory information to the orbitofrontal cortex for odor discrimination. Her tutor points out that olfactory information also reaches this neocortex by a second route. What is that second route?","choices":{"A":"A direct projection from olfactory cortex to orbitofrontal and insular cortex","B":"A relay through the ventral posteromedial nucleus to the frontal operculum and insula","C":"A relay through the medial geniculate nucleus to the temporal neocortex","D":"A relay through the pulvinar to the posterior parietal association cortex","E":"A relay through the anterior thalamic nucleus to the cingulate gyrus cortex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Olfactory information reaches neocortex both by a direct projection from olfactory cortex to the orbitofrontal and ventral agranular insular cortices and by a relay in the dorsomedial thalamic nucleus; neither route is obligatory, which is why olfaction is the exception to the thalamic relay rule. The parvicellular ventral posteromedial nucleus relays taste, the medial geniculate nucleus relays hearing, the pulvinar serves visual association, and the anterior nucleus belongs to the Papez circuit rather than the olfactory pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Olfactory bulb projects directly to cortex with no thalamic relay; the neocortical representation, especially orbitofrontal, is needed for odor discrimination and identification. COMLEX: Orbitofrontal lesions impair odor identification while basic detection may be preserved.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — thalamic relay nuclei and their cortical targets, contrasted with the thalamus-optional olfactory route","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The parvicellular ventral post"}},{"id":"sdl37_q38","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why olfaction is unique among sensory systems with respect to thalamic relay.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher demonstrates that lesioning the dorsomedial thalamic nucleus in an experimental model impairs an animal's ability to discriminate between structurally similar odorants, while basic odor detection remains intact. This finding best illustrates which principle regarding olfactory thalamic relay?","choices":{"A":"Olfaction requires an obligatory thalamic relay","B":"Olfaction has no thalamic connections","C":"The dorsomedial nucleus mediates odor detection only","D":"The dorsomedial nucleus is the primary olfactory relay","E":"A secondary, non-obligatory thalamic olfactory pathway exists"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This finding demonstrates that a secondary, non-obligatory olfactory pathway does exist through the dorsomedial thalamic nucleus, responsible specifically for higher-order discrimination rather than basic detection, since detection remains intact despite the lesion. Describing an obligatory thalamic relay would contradict the preserved basic detection observed, describing no thalamic connections at all would contradict the discrimination deficit produced by this specific lesion, and calling the dorsomedial nucleus either detection-only or the primary relay both misstate its actual secondary, discrimination-specific role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the dorsomedial thalamic pathway is secondary and non-obligatory, responsible for discrimination and identification rather than basic odor detection, which the direct primary pathway alone can support. COMLEX: this dissociation, detection preserved but discrimination impaired, is a useful pattern for localizing lesions to this specific secondary pathway on boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 3 — primary versus secondary olfactory thalamic relay, applied to an experimental lesion vignette","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Describing an obligatory thala","C":"Describing an obligatory thala"}},{"id":"sdl37_q39","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why olfaction is unique among sensory systems with respect to thalamic relay.","batch":2,"stem":"Compared to the pathways for vision, hearing, and somatosensation, which single anatomic feature makes the primary olfactory pathway distinct?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral rather than unilateral cortical projection","B":"Direct bulb-to-cortex projection bypassing thalamus","C":"Ipsilateral rather than contralateral cortical projection","D":"Multisynaptic rather than monosynaptic bulb relay","E":"Subcortical rather than cortical final destination"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The single defining distinction of the primary olfactory pathway is its direct bulb-to-cortex projection that bypasses the thalamus entirely, unlike vision, hearing, and somatosensation, which all require an obligatory thalamic synapse before reaching their respective primary cortices. Bilateral versus unilateral projection and ipsilateral versus contralateral organization are not the defining distinguishing features here, and the primary olfactory pathway does still terminate in a cortical, not subcortical, structure, piriform cortex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the defining, single most testable feature of primary olfaction is the absence of an obligatory thalamic relay, not laterality or synapse count. COMLEX: precisely identifying which single feature is unique, rather than listing several true-but-non-distinguishing facts, is a common higher-order testing strategy on boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 3 — the single distinguishing feature of olfactory pathway anatomy, applied to a comparative vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q40","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify major causes and lesion localizations for anosmia, hyposmia, parosmia, phantosmia, and olfactory hallucinations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with early Alzheimer disease is noted by his family to have lost his sense of smell well before his memory symptoms became apparent. Olfactory dysfunction as an early, sometimes premotor or precognitive, feature is also classically described in which other neurodegenerative disease?","choices":{"A":"Multiple sclerosis, demyelinating disease","B":"Parkinson disease, early premotor feature","C":"Guillain-Barre syndrome, demyelinating disease","D":"Myasthenia gravis, neuromuscular junction disease","E":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, motor neuron disease"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Parkinson disease classically features olfactory dysfunction as one of its earliest manifestations, often preceding the onset of the classic motor triad by years, alongside REM sleep behavior disorder and constipation as early non-motor markers. Multiple sclerosis, Guillain-Barre syndrome, myasthenia gravis, and ALS are not classically associated with prominent early olfactory dysfunction as a premotor or precognitive feature in the way that Alzheimer and Parkinson disease are.","boardPrep":"Step 1: early olfactory dysfunction is a shared, frequently tested premotor/precognitive feature of both Alzheimer disease and Parkinson disease, useful for early recognition. COMLEX: screening for anosmia alongside other early non-motor features, such as REM sleep behavior disorder, can support earlier clinical suspicion of Parkinson disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 4 — neurodegenerative causes of olfactory dysfunction, applied to a clinical recognition vignette","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Multiple sclerosis, Guillain-B","D":"Multiple sclerosis, Guillain-B","E":"Multiple sclerosis, Guillain-B"}},{"id":"sdl37_q41","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify major causes and lesion localizations for anosmia, hyposmia, parosmia, phantosmia, and olfactory hallucinations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman reports that for the past year she has intermittently smelled a foul, burning odor that no one else notices, occurring in brief episodes lasting seconds. Video EEG captures focal spike activity during these episodes. Which term best describes her symptom?","choices":{"A":"Parosmia, a distortion of a real odor","B":"Anosmia, a complete loss of smell","C":"Hyposmia, reduced sensitivity to odorants","D":"Hyperosmia, an increased olfactory acuity","E":"Phantosmia, an ictal olfactory hallucination"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient's perception of an odor with no actual stimulus present, correlating with focal EEG spike activity, is phantosmia, an ictal olfactory hallucination arising from a temporal lobe seizure focus. Parosmia requires a genuinely present odor that is distorted rather than hallucinated entirely, anosmia and hyposmia describe absent or reduced perception rather than hallucinated presence, and hyperosmia describes increased sensitivity to real stimuli rather than hallucination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: phantosmia correlating with focal EEG spikes confirms an ictal olfactory hallucination, localizing to the temporal lobe seizure focus. COMLEX: this presentation warrants standard seizure workup and consideration of an underlying structural lesion in the medial temporal lobe.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 4 — phantosmia as a seizure-related finding, applied to an EEG-correlated vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q42","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify major causes and lesion localizations for anosmia, hyposmia, parosmia, phantosmia, and olfactory hallucinations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man reports that, for as long as he can remember, he has been completely unable to smell one specific compound, though his sense of smell is otherwise entirely normal and he denies any history of nasal trauma or infection. Which condition best explains this isolated finding?","choices":{"A":"General anosmia, an acquired olfactory nerve injury","B":"Hyposmia, a generalized reduction in sensitivity","C":"Parosmia, a distortion of odor perception","D":"Specific anosmia, an inherited receptor absence","E":"Olfactory agnosia, a failure to name detected odors"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Specific anosmia describes a lifelong, isolated inability to detect a particular compound or class of compounds despite otherwise normal olfactory function, thought to result from absence of the specific odorant receptor for that compound, sometimes inherited as an autosomal recessive trait. General anosmia and hyposmia describe broader, typically acquired deficits rather than this narrow inherited pattern, parosmia describes distortion rather than complete inability to detect one compound, and olfactory agnosia describes a failure of identification despite intact detection, which does not match this vignette's described inability to detect the compound at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: specific anosmia is a narrow, often genetically determined inability to detect one compound, distinct from the broader acquired anosmias caused by trauma or infection. COMLEX: recognizing this benign, inherited pattern avoids unnecessary workup for a structural or acquired cause.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 4 — inherited specific anosmia, applied to a genetic/developmental vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q43","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the anatomy of taste buds and the distribution of taste fibers carried by CN VII, CN IX, and CN X.","batch":2,"stem":"A histology specimen from the posterior lateral tongue shows taste receptor cells embedded in the epithelium lining deep clefts along the tongue margin. Which type of papilla is being examined?","choices":{"A":"Foliate papilla, lateral tongue clefts","B":"Fungiform papilla, anterior tongue mushroom shape","C":"Vallate papilla, posterior tongue central structure","D":"Filiform papilla, nongustatory tongue surface","E":"Palatal taste bud, extralingual soft palate location"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Foliate papillae appear as a series of clefts along the lateral margin of the posterior tongue, with taste buds located in the epithelium lining these clefts, matching the described location and histologic appearance. Fungiform papillae are located anteriorly rather than posterolaterally, vallate papillae form a discrete row at the junction of the oral and pharyngeal tongue rather than lateral clefts, filiform papillae are nongustatory structures lacking any taste buds, and palatal taste buds are an extralingual site rather than a tongue margin structure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: foliate papillae are the lateral-margin, cleft-forming papillae, distinct in both location and structure from the anterior fungiform and posterior-central vallate papillae. COMLEX: knowing papilla location helps predict which cranial nerve, VII for anterior clefts, IX for posterior clefts, is responsible for a given foliate taste deficit.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 5 — papillae classification, applied to a histology identification vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q44","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the anatomy of taste buds and the distribution of taste fibers carried by CN VII, CN IX, and CN X.","batch":2,"stem":"A taste bud biopsy reveals a cell type expressing candidate acid transducer channels and conventional synaptic machinery, but lacking G protein-coupled taste receptors. Which taste bud cell type is being described?","choices":{"A":"Type I cells, glial-like supporting cells","B":"Type II cells, G protein receptor cells","C":"Type IV cells, basal progenitor cells","D":"Type III cells, the sour-transducing cells","E":"Sustentacular cells, olfactory epithelial support cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Type III, or presynaptic, cells express synapse-related proteins and candidate acid-sensing channels important for sour taste transduction, but notably lack the G protein-coupled receptors used by type II cells for sweet, bitter, and umami detection. Type I cells perform glial-like supporting functions without this synaptic or acid-sensing machinery, type II cells are defined precisely by having G protein-coupled receptors, which this vignette excludes, type IV cells are undifferentiated basal progenitors, and sustentacular cells belong to the olfactory rather than gustatory system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: type III cells are the sour-taste specialists, using a distinct non-GPCR acid-sensing mechanism, contrasted with type II cells' GPCR-based sweet/bitter/umami detection. COMLEX: this receptor-cell-type distinction underlies why sour and salty taste transduction mechanisms differ fundamentally from sweet, bitter, and umami transduction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 5 — taste bud cell type classification, applied to a biopsy-based vignette","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Type I cells perform glial-lik"}},{"id":"sdl37_q45","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the anatomy of taste buds and the distribution of taste fibers carried by CN VII, CN IX, and CN X.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man undergoes middle ear surgery complicated by injury to a small nerve branch running through the middle ear cavity, resulting in taste loss confined to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. Which nerve branch was most likely injured?","choices":{"A":"Lingual-tonsillar branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve","B":"Superior laryngeal branch of the vagus nerve","C":"Greater petrosal branch of the facial nerve","D":"Chorda tympani, branch of the facial nerve","E":"Auriculotemporal branch of the trigeminal nerve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The chorda tympani, a branch of the facial nerve, passes through the middle ear cavity before joining the lingual nerve to carry taste sensation from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, making it uniquely vulnerable to injury during middle ear surgery. The lingual-tonsillar branch of CN IX carries posterior tongue taste rather than anterior, the superior laryngeal branch of CN X carries epiglottic taste, the greater petrosal branch of CN VII carries palatal taste and lacrimal secretomotor fibers rather than anterior tongue taste, and the auriculotemporal branch of CN V is sensory rather than gustatory.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the chorda tympani's course through the middle ear cavity is a classic surgical risk point, since middle ear procedures can injure it and cause isolated anterior tongue taste loss. COMLEX: this iatrogenic complication is worth counseling patients about prior to middle ear surgery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 5 — chorda tympani anatomy and vulnerability, applied to a surgical complication vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q46","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace taste information from the tongue, palate, pharynx, and epiglottis to the nucleus solitarius, thalamus, and gustatory cortex.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher records from second-order gustatory neurons within the rostral solitary nucleus and traces their ascending axons. Which fiber tract carries these axons toward the thalamus?","choices":{"A":"Contralateral central tegmental tract","B":"Ipsilateral medial lemniscus","C":"Contralateral spinothalamic tract","D":"Ipsilateral lateral lemniscus","E":"Ipsilateral central tegmental tract"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Second-order gustatory neurons ascend from the rostral, gustatory, solitary nucleus via the ipsilateral central tegmental tract to reach the ventral posteromedial thalamic nucleus, keeping the entire taste pathway uncrossed from tongue to cortex. A contralateral central tegmental tract does not carry the primary gustatory pathway, the medial lemniscus carries dorsal column somatosensory rather than gustatory information, the spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature, and the lateral lemniscus carries auditory rather than gustatory information.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the central tegmental tract is the specific ascending fiber pathway for taste, remaining ipsilateral throughout, unlike most other sensory pathways that cross. COMLEX: this exclusively ipsilateral organization is a frequently tested distinguishing feature of the gustatory pathway.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 6 — the central tegmental tract as the gustatory ascending pathway, applied to a tract-tracing vignette","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A contralateral central tegmen","D":"A contralateral central tegmen"}},{"id":"sdl37_q47","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace taste information from the tongue, palate, pharynx, and epiglottis to the nucleus solitarius, thalamus, and gustatory cortex.","batch":2,"stem":"A stroke selectively damages the parvicellular division of the ventral posteromedial thalamic nucleus on one side. Which specific sensory deficit would this lesion most likely produce?","choices":{"A":"Contralateral loss of taste discrimination","B":"Bilateral loss of taste discrimination","C":"Ipsilateral loss of facial pain sensation","D":"Ipsilateral loss of taste discrimination","E":"Contralateral loss of facial pain sensation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because the gustatory pathway remains entirely ipsilateral from tongue to cortex, a lesion of VPMpc produces ipsilateral rather than contralateral taste discrimination deficits, unlike most other sensory pathways. A contralateral or bilateral taste deficit would be inconsistent with this pathway's known ipsilateral organization, and facial pain sensation is carried by a different thalamic subdivision, the main VPM rather than its parvicellular gustatory portion, making facial pain findings an inaccurate prediction for this specific lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VPMpc lesions produce ipsilateral taste deficits, a frequently tested exception to the general rule that thalamic sensory lesions produce contralateral findings. COMLEX: always specify laterality carefully when predicting deficits from a gustatory pathway lesion, since this ipsilateral pattern is easy to mix up with the contralateral pattern typical of most other sensory systems.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 6 — the ipsilateral organization of the gustatory pathway, applied to a thalamic stroke vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q48","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace taste information from the tongue, palate, pharynx, and epiglottis to the nucleus solitarius, thalamus, and gustatory cortex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman develops a lesion involving the anterior insular cortex and adjacent frontal operculum on one side following a stroke. Which taste-related deficit would this lesion most likely produce?","choices":{"A":"Complete inability to detect any taste stimulus","B":"Impaired discrimination of taste qualities","C":"Loss of salivary reflex responses to taste alone","D":"Loss of swallowing reflex responses to taste alone","E":"Hypersensitivity to all taste stimuli equally"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The anterior insular cortex and frontal operculum constitute primary gustatory cortex, responsible for the discriminative, conscious identification of taste qualities, so a lesion here produces impaired taste discrimination, while more basic detection reflexes mediated at the brainstem level typically remain intact. Complete inability to detect any taste stimulus would suggest a more peripheral or brainstem lesion rather than a cortical one, isolated loss of salivary or swallowing reflexes would instead suggest brainstem, medullary reflex circuit involvement, and hypersensitivity is not the expected consequence of a cortical lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cortical gustatory lesions impair conscious discrimination while sparing brainstem-mediated basic taste reflexes, distinguishing cortical from brainstem gustatory pathology. COMLEX: this dissociation parallels the general principle that cortical sensory lesions impair discrimination more than basic detection across multiple sensory systems.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 6 — primary gustatory cortex function, applied to a cortical stroke vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q49","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate true taste loss from perceived flavor loss caused by olfactory dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man presents reporting that food has tasted bland for the past two weeks, occurring shortly after an upper respiratory infection. Formal testing shows normal detection of sweet, salty, sour, bitter, and umami stimuli, but he cannot identify the specific flavor of common foods when his nose is unblocked versus pinched. Which system is most likely responsible for his complaint?","choices":{"A":"Gustatory system, post-viral dysfunction","B":"Olfactory system, post-viral dysfunction","C":"Trigeminal system, post-viral dysfunction","D":"Vestibular system, post-viral dysfunction","E":"Auditory system, post-viral dysfunction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Normal basic taste testing combined with a difference in flavor perception depending on nasal patency confirms that his complaint reflects post-viral olfactory rather than gustatory dysfunction, since retronasal smell, not basic taste, provides the flavor-identifying component of eating. The gustatory system is functioning normally per formal testing, and the trigeminal, vestibular, and auditory systems are not implicated by this specific pattern of findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: post-viral chemosensory complaints are overwhelmingly olfactory rather than gustatory, confirmed here by intact basic taste testing alongside nasal-patency-dependent flavor perception. COMLEX: counsel patients that this pattern reflects a common, often self-limited post-viral olfactory dysfunction rather than a taste disorder per se.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 7 — distinguishing olfactory from gustatory dysfunction, applied to a post-viral clinical vignette","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The gustatory system is functi"}},{"id":"sdl37_q50","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate true taste loss from perceived flavor loss caused by olfactory dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A clinician evaluating a patient with a chief complaint of \"loss of taste\" performs formal taste testing with all five basic taste qualities and finds them intact, then performs formal smell testing and finds significant hyposmia. Which conclusion is most appropriate based on these findings?","choices":{"A":"The patient's complaint reflects olfactory, not gustatory, dysfunction","B":"The patient's complaint more likely reflects gustatory, not olfactory, dysfunction","C":"The patient has combined gustatory and olfactory dysfunction","D":"The patient's complaint is most likely psychogenic in origin","E":"The patient has an isolated chorda tympani lesion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intact basic taste testing with confirmed hyposmia demonstrates that this patient's subjective \"taste loss\" complaint actually reflects olfactory rather than gustatory dysfunction, the most common pattern seen clinically. Concluding gustatory dysfunction would contradict the normal taste testing result, combined dysfunction is not supported since gustation tested normally, a psychogenic conclusion is premature without first excluding organic olfactory causes, which testing has already identified, and an isolated chorda tympani lesion would be expected to impair taste testing rather than leave it intact.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this stepwise evaluation, taste testing then smell testing, is the correct clinical algorithm for working up a \"taste loss\" complaint and directly demonstrates why most such complaints are ultimately olfactory. COMLEX: documenting this distinction changes counseling, prognosis, and further workup considerably.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 7 — stepwise clinical evaluation of chemosensory complaints, applied to a diagnostic reasoning vignette","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Concluding gustatory dysfuncti","D":"Concluding gustatory dysfuncti"}},{"id":"sdl37_q51","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate true taste loss from perceived flavor loss caused by olfactory dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient reports profound loss of \"taste\" following COVID-19 infection. Detailed testing reveals normal detection of the five basic tastes but impaired ability to distinguish complex flavors. Which mechanism best accounts for this clinical picture?","choices":{"A":"Gustatory receptor damage sparing olfactory epithelium","B":"Combined gustatory and olfactory receptor destruction","C":"Olfactory epithelial damage sparing gustatory receptors","D":"Central gustatory cortex damage sparing all periphery","E":"Chorda tympani damage sparing the olfactory system"},"correct":"C","explanation":"SARS-CoV-2 preferentially damages the olfactory epithelium, in part via ACE2 receptor expression there, while sparing gustatory receptor cells, explaining why basic taste detection remains normal even as complex flavor perception, which depends heavily on retronasal olfaction, is profoundly impaired. Gustatory receptor damage sparing olfaction would produce the opposite pattern, combined destruction would be expected to impair basic taste testing as well, central gustatory cortex damage would not typically spare all peripheral gustatory function while producing this specific flavor complaint, and chorda tympani damage would impair anterior tongue taste testing rather than sparing it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COVID-19-associated chemosensory dysfunction is predominantly olfactory, mediated by ACE2 receptor expression in the olfactory epithelium rather than gustatory receptor damage. COMLEX: this mechanism explains the characteristic pattern of preserved basic taste with impaired flavor complexity reported in post-COVID chemosensory complaints.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 7 — COVID-19 chemosensory pathophysiology, applied to a post-infectious vignette","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Gustatory receptor damage spar"}},{"id":"sdl37_q52","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical features to localize lesions involving CN I, chorda tympani, CN IX, CN X, nucleus solitarius, thalamus, or gustatory cortex.","batch":2,"stem":"During middle cranial fossa surgery, a 48-year-old woman sustains injury to a facial nerve branch that leaves at the geniculate ganglion. Afterward she has reduced tearing on that side and loss of taste on the soft palate, while taste on the anterior tongue and facial strength are normal. Which nerve was injured?","choices":{"A":"Chorda tympani branch","B":"Nerve to the stapedius","C":"Deep petrosal nerve","D":"Greater petrosal nerve","E":"Lesser petrosal nerve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The greater (superficial) petrosal nerve branches from the facial nerve at the geniculate ganglion and carries taste fibers from soft palate taste buds and preganglionic parasympathetic fibers for the lacrimal gland, so its injury explains palatal taste loss with a dry eye. The chorda tympani carries anterior tongue taste, which is intact, and the nerve to the stapedius would produce hyperacusis. The deep petrosal nerve carries sympathetic fibers from the carotid plexus rather than taste or lacrimal secretomotor fibers, and the lesser petrosal nerve carries glossopharyngeal fibers to the parotid gland.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Greater petrosal nerve: soft palate taste plus lacrimal and nasal gland secretion via the pterygopalatine ganglion. COMLEX: A lesion at or proximal to the geniculate ganglion adds dry eye to facial palsy; a lesion distal to it spares tearing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 4 — craniosacral parasympathetic outflow through CN VII to the lacrimal gland","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q53","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical features to localize lesions involving CN I, chorda tympani, CN IX, CN X, nucleus solitarius, thalamus, or gustatory cortex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man correctly reports that an odor is present every time a test vial is opened, but he cannot recognize or name any of the familiar scents, such as coffee or orange. Nasal exam is normal. Where does this deficit most likely localize?","choices":{"A":"Olfactory epithelium, from receptor neuron loss","B":"Olfactory fila, from shearing at the cribriform plate","C":"Nasal cavity, from polyps blocking airflow","D":"Higher olfactory cortex, sparing the peripheral pathway","E":"Olfactory bulb, from compression by a groove meningioma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Detecting an odorant shows that the peripheral nerve and its pathway are intact, whereas identifying it requires intact cortical function, so awareness of a smell without recognition, olfactory agnosia, places the problem at higher levels of the system. Loss of receptor neurons, shearing of the fila, obstruction by polyps, and compression of the bulb would all reduce or abolish detection of the odor itself, which is preserved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Smell testing has two parts: detection tests the peripheral pathway, identification tests cortex; olfactory agnosia is detection without recognition. COMLEX: Distinguish perception from recognition before blaming the nose or the olfactory nerve.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 3 — the orbitofrontal neocortical representation needed for odor identification","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loss of receptor neurons, shea","C":"Loss of receptor neurons, shea"}},{"id":"sdl37_q54","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical features to localize lesions involving CN I, chorda tympani, CN IX, CN X, nucleus solitarius, thalamus, or gustatory cortex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man develops a lateral medullary infarct. Among his findings, he reports altered taste sensation. Which structure's involvement within the infarcted territory best explains this specific finding?","choices":{"A":"Nucleus ambiguus within the lateral medulla","B":"Spinal trigeminal nucleus within the lateral medulla","C":"Inferior cerebellar peduncle within the lateral medulla","D":"Descending sympathetic fibers within the lateral medulla","E":"Nucleus solitarius within the lateral medulla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The nucleus solitarius, including its rostral gustatory portion, lies within the lateral medullary territory supplied by the posterior inferior cerebellar artery, so its involvement in a lateral medullary infarct explains altered taste sensation as part of the broader Wallenberg syndrome presentation. Nucleus ambiguus involvement instead produces dysphagia and hoarseness, spinal trigeminal nucleus involvement produces ipsilateral facial pain and temperature loss, inferior cerebellar peduncle involvement produces ataxia, and descending sympathetic fiber involvement produces Horner syndrome, none of which directly explains the taste finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: taste disturbance is a less commonly emphasized but real component of lateral medullary syndrome, reflecting nucleus solitarius involvement alongside the more classically taught crossed sensory, ataxia, and Horner syndrome findings. COMLEX: recognizing this less prominent finding rounds out a complete understanding of Wallenberg syndrome's full territory of involvement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 8 — nucleus solitarius involvement in lateral medullary syndrome, applied to a stroke vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q55","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Connect olfaction and taste to feeding behavior, salivation, nausea, autonomic responses, and limbic memory/emotion.","batch":2,"stem":"In an animal study, investigators touch a bitter solution to taste buds on the laryngeal surface of the epiglottis and the aryepiglottic folds. Which response do these extralingual taste buds most characteristically trigger?","choices":{"A":"Conscious discrimination of bitter quality in the insula","B":"Brainstem-mediated reflexes that prevent aspiration","C":"Secretion from von Ebner glands at vallate papillae","D":"Odor-guided feeding through the lateral hypothalamus","E":"Reflex lacrimation through the pterygopalatine ganglion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Stimulation of extralingual taste buds, particularly those near the larynx, elicits brainstem-mediated reflexes that prevent accidental aspiration of ingested material. Conscious discrimination of taste quality depends on the solitary nucleus to parvicellular VPM to insula pathway rather than a characteristic laryngeal reflex. Von Ebner gland secretion is driven by taste stimulation of vallate and foliate papillae, odor-guided feeding involves olfactory projections to the lateral hypothalamus, and lacrimation is a facial nerve parasympathetic response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Laryngeal taste buds are innervated by the superior laryngeal nerve of CN X and feed airway-protective reflexes. COMLEX: Loss of laryngeal sensation after vagal injury raises aspiration risk in dysphagic patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 8 — vagal afferent clinical patterns, including laryngeal sensation and airway protection","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q56","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Connect olfaction and taste to feeding behavior, salivation, nausea, autonomic responses, and limbic memory/emotion.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with a lesion of the nucleus solitarius loses the ability to trigger reflexive salivation in response to food in the mouth. This finding best illustrates which functional connection?","choices":{"A":"Olfactory-evoked cortical association circuits","B":"Taste-evoked cortical discrimination circuits","C":"Olfactory-evoked limbic memory circuits","D":"Taste-evoked medullary reflex circuits","E":"Taste-evoked thalamic relay circuits alone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This finding illustrates the functional connection between taste input relayed to the solitary nucleus and adjacent medullary reflex circuits, which normally drive reflexive salivary secretion in response to food, a connection lost when the solitary nucleus itself is damaged. Olfactory-evoked cortical association and limbic memory circuits involve different structures entirely, unrelated to this brainstem reflex, taste-evoked cortical discrimination circuits involve conscious perception rather than reflexive salivation, and taste-evoked thalamic relay circuits alone would not account for a reflex that is mediated at the brainstem level without requiring thalamic or cortical involvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reflexive, food-triggered salivation is mediated at the brainstem level via solitary nucleus connections to adjacent medullary reflex circuits, independent of conscious cortical taste perception. COMLEX: this brainstem-level reflex organization explains why salivary reflexes can remain intact or be selectively lost independent of conscious taste discrimination, depending on lesion location.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 9 — solitary nucleus-mediated salivary reflexes, applied to a lesion-based functional vignette","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q57","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Connect olfaction and taste to feeding behavior, salivation, nausea, autonomic responses, and limbic memory/emotion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman reports that a specific perfume reliably triggers a strong emotional response and a vivid autobiographical memory each time she encounters it, more so than a photograph from the same event. Which anatomic feature of the olfactory system best explains this heightened memory-emotion association compared to other sensory modalities?","choices":{"A":"Olfactory information relaying through the thalamus first, as with other senses","B":"Olfactory information bypassing all limbic structures","C":"Olfactory information relaying through the cerebellum first","D":"Olfactory information processed exclusively in the brainstem","E":"Direct olfactory projections to amygdala and entorhinal cortex"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The direct, unfiltered projections from olfactory cortex to the amygdala and to the entorhinal cortex, which then relays onward to the hippocampus, give olfactory stimuli privileged, rapid access to emotional and memory processing circuits compared to visual information, which must first pass through the thalamus and undergo more extensive cortical processing before reaching these same limbic structures. Describing olfaction as relaying through the thalamus first is inaccurate and would not explain a heightened rather than diminished limbic association, bypassing all limbic structures would contradict the actual direct limbic projections responsible for this phenomenon, and cerebellar or exclusively brainstem processing does not accurately describe primary olfactory processing at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the direct, thalamus-bypassing olfactory projection to amygdala and entorhinal cortex is the anatomic explanation for why smells are unusually powerful memory and emotion triggers compared to other senses. COMLEX: this same anatomic principle is often invoked in discussions of olfactory-based interventions in memory and mood-related clinical contexts.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 9 — the anatomic basis for olfactory-emotional memory potency, applied to a psychological phenomenon vignette","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q58","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Apply the pathways to board-style questions involving head trauma, skull base tumors, Bell palsy, lateral medullary syndrome, temporal lobe seizures, and neurodegenerative disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman develops sudden right-sided facial weakness affecting both the upper and lower face, along with ipsilateral hyperacusis and diminished taste on the anterior right tongue. Symptoms began after a herpetic prodrome. Which cranial nerve is most likely affected at its root?","choices":{"A":"Trigeminal nerve, cranial nerve V","B":"Glossopharyngeal nerve, cranial nerve IX","C":"Abducens nerve, cranial nerve VI","D":"Vagus nerve, cranial nerve X","E":"Facial nerve, cranial nerve VII"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This combination, complete upper and lower facial weakness, hyperacusis, and anterior tongue taste loss following a herpetic prodrome, is the classic presentation of Bell palsy from a facial nerve root lesion, likely due to herpes simplex virus reactivation causing nerve swelling within the facial canal. The trigeminal, glossopharyngeal, vagus, and abducens nerves do not together account for this specific combination of complete facial weakness, hyperacusis, and anterior tongue taste loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bell palsy's complete upper and lower facial weakness, distinguishing it from the forehead-sparing pattern of a central UMN lesion, is a critical exam finding paired here with hyperacusis and taste loss confirming the proximal facial nerve root localization. COMLEX: prednisone, often with an antiviral, is standard treatment to reduce nerve swelling and improve outcomes in Bell palsy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 10 — Bell palsy as a proximal facial nerve syndrome, applied to a complete clinical vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q59","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Apply the pathways to board-style questions involving head trauma, skull base tumors, Bell palsy, lateral medullary syndrome, temporal lobe seizures, and neurodegenerative disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman has a right facial palsy that involves the forehead, with altered taste on the front of the tongue, which began after a brief viral prodrome. The clinician starts prednisone. Which rationale for this treatment is described for this condition?","choices":{"A":"Killing the herpes virus within the geniculate ganglion","B":"Reducing nerve swelling within the bony facial canal","C":"Blocking acetylcholine release at the facial muscles","D":"Promoting regrowth of fungiform papilla taste buds","E":"Restoring stapedius reflex contraction in the middle ear"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bell palsy has been linked to herpes simplex virus, and prednisone may reduce damage from swelling of the facial nerve inside the narrow facial canal. Corticosteroids are anti-inflammatory rather than antiviral, they do not act at the neuromuscular junction, and taste bud turnover and the stapedius reflex recover as a consequence of nerve recovery rather than as direct drug targets.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bell palsy: idiopathic, often attributed to HSV reactivation; lower motor neuron palsy involving the forehead, with possible taste loss and hyperacusis. COMLEX: Early corticosteroids are standard to limit compressive injury in the facial canal; protect the eye if closure is incomplete.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 5 — lower motor neuron facial palsy involving the forehead, as in Bell palsy","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Corticosteroids are anti-infla","C":"Corticosteroids are anti-infla","D":"Corticosteroids are anti-infla"}},{"id":"sdl37_q60","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Apply the pathways to board-style questions involving head trauma, skull base tumors, Bell palsy, lateral medullary syndrome, temporal lobe seizures, and neurodegenerative disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with a long history of well-controlled epilepsy reports recurring brief episodes of an unpleasant burning smell, occurring seconds before impaired awareness and lip-smacking automatisms. Video EEG localizes the seizure onset to the medial temporal lobe. This aura is best classified as which of the following?","choices":{"A":"A confirmed phantosmia unrelated to seizure activity","B":"An uncinate fit, temporal lobe seizure aura","C":"A parosmia unrelated to seizure activity","D":"A true anosmia from postictal dysfunction","E":"A hyperosmia from postictal dysfunction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This presentation, an unpleasant olfactory aura preceding impaired awareness and automatisms, with EEG confirmation of medial temporal lobe seizure onset, is the classic uncinate fit, a focal seizure aura arising from the uncus and adjacent medial temporal structures. Describing this as phantosmia or parosmia unrelated to seizure activity would ignore the clear EEG-confirmed seizure correlation, and true anosmia or hyperosmia from postictal dysfunction do not match this vignette's description of a preictal hallucinated smell followed by impaired awareness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an unpleasant preictal olfactory aura with automatisms and impaired awareness is the classic uncinate fit, localizing seizure onset to the medial temporal lobe. COMLEX: recognizing this aura pattern supports appropriate temporal lobe epilepsy workup and guides surgical evaluation in medically refractory cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 10 — the uncinate fit as a complete clinical seizure vignette, integrating olfactory aura with temporal lobe epilepsy semiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the anatomy of the olfactory epithelium, olfactory receptor neurons, cribriform plate, olfactory bulb, glomeruli, mitral cells, tufted cells, and olfactory tract.","batch":3,"stem":"A 71-year-old man reports gradually worsening smell over several years, with no history of trauma, infection, or nasal disease. Biopsy of his olfactory epithelium shows a markedly reduced population of the mitotically active progenitor cell type relative to age-matched controls decades younger. Which cell type's decline best explains his presentation?","choices":{"A":"Sustentacular cells, age-related decline","B":"Bipolar receptor neurons, age-related decline","C":"Basal cells, age-related decline","D":"Mitral cells, age-related decline","E":"Bowman gland cells, age-related decline"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Basal cells are the mitotically active progenitor population responsible for lifelong regeneration of olfactory receptor neurons, and age-related decline in this stem cell population is a recognized contributor to presbyosmia, the gradual olfactory decline of normal aging. Sustentacular and Bowman gland cells provide support and secretion rather than regeneration, bipolar receptor neurons are the postmitotic product of basal cell division rather than the progenitor itself, and mitral cells reside in the bulb rather than the epithelium and play no role in epithelial regeneration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: presbyosmia is attributed in part to declining basal cell regenerative capacity, extending the \"basal cell equals stem cell\" fact to an aging-physiology context. COMLEX: counsel older patients that some olfactory decline is a normal aging finding, distinct from pathologic anosmia requiring further workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — olfactory epithelium and bulb cytoarchitecture, tested here as a board-style structure-identification vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)","whyNot":{"A":"Sustentacular and Bowman gland"}},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the anatomy of the olfactory epithelium, olfactory receptor neurons, cribriform plate, olfactory bulb, glomeruli, mitral cells, tufted cells, and olfactory tract.","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old man sustains a frontal impact in a motor vehicle collision. He develops clear watery drainage from his nose that increases when he leans forward, along with new-onset anosmia. Shearing at a single anatomic site best explains both findings together. Which structure is most likely damaged?","choices":{"A":"Optic canal, isolated visual pathway site","B":"Foramen ovale, isolated trigeminal site","C":"Internal acoustic meatus, isolated CN VII/VIII site","D":"Cribriform plate, dural and fila injury","E":"Superior orbital fissure, isolated ocular motor site"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The cribriform plate is the single site where both the thin dura overlying the ethmoid bone and the traversing olfactory fila can be sheared together during frontal impact, producing simultaneous CSF rhinorrhea, from the dural tear, and anosmia, from fila disruption. The optic canal, foramen ovale, internal acoustic meatus, and superior orbital fissure each transmit a single unrelated cranial nerve and are not associated with this combined CSF leak plus anosmia presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CSF rhinorrhea plus anosmia after frontal trauma localizes to the cribriform plate, a two-finding integration worth recognizing as a single anatomic answer. COMLEX: positional worsening of clear nasal drainage on leaning forward is a classic bedside clue for CSF rhinorrhea, distinct from ordinary rhinitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — olfactory epithelium and bulb cytoarchitecture, tested here as a board-style structure-identification vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the anatomy of the olfactory epithelium, olfactory receptor neurons, cribriform plate, olfactory bulb, glomeruli, mitral cells, tufted cells, and olfactory tract.","batch":3,"stem":"A researcher genetically labels every olfactory receptor neuron expressing a single specific odorant receptor gene. Despite these labeled neurons being scattered widely across the olfactory epithelium, their axons are found to converge tightly onto only one or two discrete structures within the bulb. This convergence pattern best demonstrates the functional role of which structure?","choices":{"A":"Glomeruli, receptor-specific convergence points","B":"Mitral cell bodies, convergence-independent relay","C":"Granule cell bodies, convergence-independent interneurons","D":"Anterior olfactory nucleus, convergence-independent relay","E":"Olfactory tract, convergence-independent conduit"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Despite their scattered distribution across the epithelium, olfactory receptor neurons expressing the same receptor gene converge their axons onto the same one or two glomeruli, so each glomerulus functions as a receptor-specific convergence point that anatomically encodes odorant identity before any further processing. Mitral and granule cell bodies, the anterior olfactory nucleus, and the olfactory tract are all downstream of this initial convergence step and do not themselves perform the receptor-specific sorting demonstrated by this experiment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: glomerular convergence of same-receptor axons is the anatomic basis of odorant coding, a mechanistic detail examiners like to test via an experimental framing. COMLEX: this precise convergence explains why localized glomerular injury can produce surprisingly specific gaps in odor perception rather than uniform anosmia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — olfactory epithelium and bulb cytoarchitecture, tested here as a board-style structure-identification vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace olfactory information from odorant binding to primary olfactory cortex and limbic-associated targets.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with a slowly enlarging olfactory groove meningioma develops progressive unilateral anosmia on the side of the mass, with no other cranial nerve deficits. The tumor is most likely compressing which structure lying directly above it?","choices":{"A":"Anterior commissure, bilateral connecting pathway","B":"Fornix, hippocampal output pathway structure","C":"Stria terminalis, amygdalar output pathway structure","D":"Medial lemniscus, brainstem sensory pathway","E":"Olfactory bulb and tract, overlying the cribriform plate"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The olfactory bulb and tract lie on the cribriform plate directly over an olfactory groove meningioma, so unilateral compression here produces isolated ipsilateral anosmia without other deficits. The anterior commissure carries bilateral interhemispheric fibers and lies well behind the olfactory groove, the fornix and stria terminalis are hippocampal and amygdalar output tracts located elsewhere, and the medial lemniscus carries dorsal column-medial lemniscal sensory information entirely unrelated to olfaction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: olfactory groove meningioma causing isolated unilateral anosmia localizes to compression of the olfactory bulb and tract, before the pathway reaches bilateral or limbic targets. COMLEX: unilateral, gradually progressive anosmia in an older adult should prompt imaging to exclude a slow-growing anterior skull base mass like this one.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — the olfactory tract's cortical and limbic targets, tested here as a board-style pathway-tracing vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace olfactory information from odorant binding to primary olfactory cortex and limbic-associated targets.","batch":3,"stem":"A tracer injected into the anterior olfactory nucleus on one side labels terminals in both olfactory bulbs and in the opposite anterior olfactory nucleus, consistent with interhemispheric processing of odors. Through which pathway do the crossing fibers travel?","choices":{"A":"Anterior limb of the anterior commissure","B":"Lateral olfactory stria to the amygdala","C":"Corpus callosum through its genu","D":"Posterior commissure of the rostral midbrain","E":"Optic chiasm at the hypothalamus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The olfactory tract contains the lateral olfactory tract, cells of the anterior olfactory nucleus, and fibers of the anterior limb of the anterior commissure, through which the anterior olfactory nucleus reaches both bulbs and the contralateral nucleus and many centrifugal fibers return to the bulb. The lateral olfactory stria carries ipsilateral bulb output to piriform cortex and amygdala, the genu of the corpus callosum links frontal neocortex, the posterior commissure serves the pupillary and vertical gaze circuits, and the optic chiasm carries retinal fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior commissure, anterior limb: interbulbar olfactory connections; posterior limb: temporal lobes. COMLEX: Bilateral olfactory connections help explain why unilateral central lesions rarely abolish smell.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — commissural white matter, including the anterior commissure","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace olfactory information from odorant binding to primary olfactory cortex and limbic-associated targets.","batch":3,"stem":"Single-cell studies of olfactory receptor neurons show that receptors tuned to specific structural features are distributed in zones across the epithelium. How many types of odorant receptor does each individual olfactory receptor neuron express?","choices":{"A":"All of the roughly 1000 receptor types","B":"About ten related receptor types","C":"One sweet and one bitter receptor type","D":"A single odorant receptor type","E":"No receptor of its own, relying on sustentacular cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Individual olfactory neurons express a single type of odorant receptor, and receptor subtypes are distributed within one of four bilaterally symmetric zones of the epithelium, forming a receptor map that patterns information for the bulb. Humans have as many as 1000 receptor types across the whole population of neurons, not within each cell. Sweet and bitter receptors are taste receptors of type II taste cells, and sustentacular cells provide support and secretions rather than odorant transduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: One neuron, one odorant receptor; neurons with the same receptor converge on the same glomeruli. COMLEX: Loss of a single receptor type explains specific anosmia, an inherited inability to smell one compound.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — glomerular convergence of receptor neurons that share an odorant receptor","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)","whyNot":{"E":"Sweet and bitter receptors are"}},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why olfaction is unique among sensory systems with respect to thalamic relay.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient sustains a thalamic stroke that produces contralateral loss of vision, hearing, and body sensation. Despite the extensive thalamic damage, the patient's sense of smell remains completely intact. Which feature of the olfactory pathway best accounts for this sparing?","choices":{"A":"Olfactory pathway is bilaterally represented in the thalamus","B":"Primary olfactory pathway bypasses the thalamus entirely","C":"Olfactory pathway relays through a thalamic nucleus resistant to stroke","D":"Olfactory pathway regenerates faster than other thalamic relays","E":"Olfactory pathway is represented in a separate, unaffected thalamus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vision, hearing, and somatosensation all require an obligatory thalamic relay through the lateral geniculate, medial geniculate, and ventral posterolateral/posteromedial nuclei respectively, so a thalamic stroke disrupts all three, but the primary olfactory pathway reaches piriform cortex directly from the bulb without any thalamic synapse, sparing smell entirely. The pathway is not simply bilaterally represented, relayed through a stroke-resistant nucleus, capable of unusually fast regeneration, or dependent on an unaffected contralateral thalamus, since it does not depend on thalamic relay in the first place.","boardPrep":"Step 1: predicting which sensory modality would be spared by an extensive thalamic lesion is a classic higher-order application of the \"olfaction bypasses the thalamus\" rule. COMLEX: this same logic explains why olfactory testing remains a useful, thalamus-independent component of the neurologic exam even after significant thalamic injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 3 — olfaction's thalamic-relay exception, tested here as a board-style comparative-lesion vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why olfaction is unique among sensory systems with respect to thalamic relay.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with small bilateral infarcts of the dorsomedial thalamic nuclei on MRI has difficulty distinguishing similar-smelling odorants on formal identification testing, though she reliably detects that an odorant is present on every trial. Which statement best reconciles this pattern with the rule that olfaction lacks an obligatory thalamic relay?","choices":{"A":"The rule is incorrect and olfaction always requires thalamic relay","B":"A separate pathway relays discrimination through the dorsomedial nucleus","C":"Detection and discrimination are mediated by the identical primary pathway","D":"The dorsomedial nucleus is required for basic odor detection itself","E":"Discrimination deficits indicate primary olfactory bulb rather than thalamic pathology"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Haines (course SDL 37 PDF p11–13) states that the olfactory bulb projects directly to olfactory cortex, the exception to the thalamic-relay rule, but that olfactory information also reaches the neocortex, either directly from olfactory cortex to orbitofrontal and insular cortex or through a relay in the dorsomedial nucleus of the thalamus; this neocortical representation is important for discrimination and identification of odors, and lesions there cause loss of these abilities. Haines also notes that detecting an odor shows the peripheral nerve and its pathway are intact, while identifying it reflects cortical function (p16). Damage to the dorsomedial relay therefore fits impaired discrimination with preserved detection. The rule is not wrong, because detection does not need the thalamus; detection and discrimination do not use one identical pathway; the dorsomedial nucleus is not needed for basic detection; and a bulb lesion would impair detection itself rather than leave it intact.","boardPrep":"Step 1: olfaction reaches cortex without a thalamic relay, but a secondary route through the dorsomedial thalamus to orbitofrontal cortex supports odor discrimination and identification (Haines). COMLEX: preserved detection with impaired identification places the lesion above the bulb and tract, in the thalamocortical or orbitofrontal levels of the pathway.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 3 — olfaction's thalamic-relay exception, tested here as a board-style comparative-lesion vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"A":"The rule is not wrong, because","E":"The rule is not wrong, because"}},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why olfaction is unique among sensory systems with respect to thalamic relay.","batch":3,"stem":"A physiology lecturer asks students to predict which single sensory testing modality would remain most reliably interpretable in a patient with a bilateral lesion confined entirely to the thalamus, sparing all cortex and subcortical white matter. Which modality should the students select?","choices":{"A":"Vision, a thalamus-dependent modality","B":"Hearing, a thalamus-dependent modality","C":"Touch and proprioception, thalamus-dependent modalities","D":"Smell, the thalamus-independent modality","E":"Pain and temperature, thalamus-dependent modalities"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because the primary olfactory pathway reaches cortex without an obligatory thalamic synapse, smell is the one modality whose basic testing would remain interpretable despite a bilateral thalamic lesion, while vision, hearing, touch/proprioception, and pain/temperature all require intact lateral geniculate, medial geniculate, or ventral posterior thalamic relay nuclei and would be disrupted by the same lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: identifying smell as the single thalamus-independent modality is a direct, single-best-answer application of this SDL's central organizing fact. COMLEX: this same principle is occasionally leveraged clinically, using preserved smell testing as one data point supporting a purely thalamic, rather than more diffuse, lesion pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 3 — olfaction's thalamic-relay exception, tested here as a board-style comparative-lesion vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify major causes and lesion localizations for anosmia, hyposmia, parosmia, phantosmia, and olfactory hallucinations.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with chronic rhinosinusitis and bilateral nasal polyps reports progressively worsening smell over two years. Nasal endoscopy shows polyps completely obstructing both olfactory clefts, and smell partially improves after a short course of oral corticosteroids. Which mechanism best explains her olfactory loss compared with a typical post-viral or post-traumatic anosmia?","choices":{"A":"Sensorineural loss from receptor neuron injury","B":"Conductive olfactory loss from obstructed odorant access","C":"Sensorineural loss from olfactory bulb infarction","D":"Central loss from primary olfactory cortex injury","E":"Central loss from dorsomedial thalamic injury"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Physical obstruction of the olfactory cleft by nasal polyps prevents odorant molecules from ever reaching an intact olfactory epithelium, a conductive mechanism analogous to conductive hearing loss, which explains why reducing polyp burden with corticosteroids improves smell, unlike the receptor neuron or central pathway injury underlying most post-viral or post-traumatic sensorineural anosmia. Sensorineural loss from receptor neuron injury or bulb infarction, and central loss from cortical or thalamic injury, would not be expected to improve with anti-inflammatory treatment aimed at reducing physical obstruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguishing conductive, obstruction-based olfactory loss from sensorineural or central causes is directly analogous to the conductive-versus-sensorineural hearing loss framework and is a favorite higher-order comparison. COMLEX: a trial of corticosteroids with objective improvement supports a conductive, reversible mechanism and guides subsequent sinus surgical planning.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 4 — the anosmia/hyposmia/parosmia/phantosmia vocabulary, tested here as a board-style etiology-discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"A":"Sensorineural loss from recept","D":"Sensorineural loss from recept"}},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify major causes and lesion localizations for anosmia, hyposmia, parosmia, phantosmia, and olfactory hallucinations.","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-year-old boy presents for evaluation of delayed puberty. He reports that he has never been able to smell anything, for as long as he can remember, and denies any nasal trauma or infection. Examination shows absent secondary sexual characteristics and low gonadotropin levels. Which embryologic process best links his two findings into a single diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Isolated cribriform plate agenesis without endocrine effect","B":"Isolated pituitary gland agenesis without olfactory effect","C":"Autoimmune destruction of both olfactory and gonadotropic cells","D":"Unrelated congenital anosmia coinciding with isolated hypogonadism","E":"Failed GnRH neuron migration along the olfactory placode"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In Kallmann syndrome, GnRH neurons normally migrate from the olfactory placode alongside developing olfactory axons into the forebrain, so a defect in this shared migratory pathway produces both congenital anosmia and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism as a single unifying embryologic diagnosis. Isolated cribriform plate agenesis or isolated pituitary agenesis would not explain both findings from one shared mechanism, autoimmune destruction is not the recognized mechanism of this congenital syndrome, and describing the findings as coincidental ignores the well-established shared developmental origin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kallmann syndrome, congenital anosmia plus hypogonadotropic hypogonadism from failed GnRH neuron migration along the olfactory placode, is a classic high-yield embryology-endocrinology crossover diagnosis. COMLEX: recognizing this link changes management to include reproductive endocrinology referral rather than treating the anosmia and delayed puberty as unrelated complaints.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 4 — the anosmia/hyposmia/parosmia/phantosmia vocabulary, tested here as a board-style etiology-discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify major causes and lesion localizations for anosmia, hyposmia, parosmia, phantosmia, and olfactory hallucinations.","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with a long-standing history of migraine reports that during her headache episodes, ordinary household odors, particularly cleaning products, become intensely unpleasant and worsen her headache, prompting her to leave the room. Between episodes, formal olfactory threshold testing is entirely normal. Which term best describes this symptom, and how does it differ from true hyperosmia?","choices":{"A":"Hyperosmia, a persistently lowered detection threshold","B":"Parosmia, a distortion of a genuinely present odor","C":"Osmophobia, an aversion without a lowered detection threshold","D":"Phantosmia, a hallucinated odor without any stimulus present","E":"Anosmia, a complete loss of odor detection"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Osmophobia describes an aversion to and worsening of symptoms from otherwise ordinary odors, occurring characteristically during migraine attacks, without any true lowering of the olfactory detection threshold, distinguishing it from hyperosmia, in which the threshold itself is persistently reduced. Parosmia and phantosmia describe qualitative distortion or hallucination rather than an aversive migraine-associated reaction, and anosmia describes loss of detection, which contradicts this patient's normal interictal threshold testing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmophobia is a recognized migraine-associated symptom distinct from true hyperosmia, worth distinguishing precisely from the other chemosensory vocabulary terms. COMLEX: identifying osmophobia as a migraine feature supports the diagnosis and can guide trigger-avoidance counseling as part of a comprehensive migraine management plan.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 4 — the anosmia/hyposmia/parosmia/phantosmia vocabulary, tested here as a board-style etiology-discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the anatomy of taste buds and the distribution of taste fibers carried by CN VII, CN IX, and CN X.","batch":3,"stem":"Taste cells in each taste bud are continuously replaced by new cells arising from basal cells. What is the typical life span of a taste receptor cell?","choices":{"A":"1 to 2 days","B":"3 to 5 days","C":"10 to 14 days","D":"30 to 60 days","E":"1 to 2 years"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Taste cells turn over continuously with a life span of about 10 to 14 days, replaced by polygonal basal cells in the basolateral taste bud that do not themselves transduce taste. Thirty to 60 days is the average life span of olfactory receptor neurons, which are replaced by basal stem cells of the olfactory epithelium. One to 5 days is too short and 1 to 2 years too long for either chemosensory cell.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chemosensory turnover: taste cells about 10 to 14 days, olfactory receptor neurons about 30 to 60 days; both regenerate from basal cells. COMLEX: Rapid turnover makes taste cells vulnerable to chemotherapy and radiation, causing temporary dysgeusia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — basal stem cells that regenerate olfactory receptor neurons","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the anatomy of taste buds and the distribution of taste fibers carried by CN VII, CN IX, and CN X.","batch":3,"stem":"Primary taste afferents from the fungiform papillae enter the brainstem at the pontomedullary junction in the intermediate nerve. Where are the cell bodies of these first-order taste neurons located?","choices":{"A":"Geniculate ganglion of the facial nerve","B":"Petrosal ganglion of the glossopharyngeal nerve","C":"Nodose ganglion of the vagus nerve","D":"Trigeminal ganglion in Meckel cave","E":"Submandibular ganglion near the gland"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Facial nerve taste fibers from the chorda tympani have their cell bodies in the geniculate ganglion, and their central processes run in the intermediate nerve to the solitary tract. The petrosal and nodose ganglia are the inferior ganglia of the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves, which hold taste neurons for the posterior tongue and the epiglottis. The trigeminal ganglion carries general sensation from the tongue, and the submandibular ganglion is a parasympathetic motor ganglion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Taste first-order cell bodies: CN VII geniculate, CN IX petrosal, CN X nodose; all synapse in the rostral solitary nucleus. COMLEX: Herpes zoster of the geniculate ganglion can impair taste along with facial strength and hearing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 6 — the rostral solitary nucleus where these first-order taste fibers terminate","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the anatomy of taste buds and the distribution of taste fibers carried by CN VII, CN IX, and CN X.","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy undergoes tonsillectomy complicated by inadvertent injury to the lingual-tonsillar branch of a cranial nerve coursing immediately adjacent to the tonsillar bed. He subsequently reports diminished taste over the back of his tongue and a blunted gag reflex on that side. Injury to which nerve best explains both findings?","choices":{"A":"Facial nerve, cranial nerve VII branch","B":"Vagus nerve, cranial nerve X branch","C":"Hypoglossal nerve, cranial nerve XII","D":"Lingual nerve, trigeminal nerve branch","E":"Glossopharyngeal nerve, cranial nerve IX"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The glossopharyngeal nerve's lingual-tonsillar branch runs directly adjacent to the tonsillar bed, carrying both posterior third tongue taste and the afferent limb of the gag reflex, so injury here during tonsillectomy produces this exact combined deficit. The facial nerve governs anterior rather than posterior tongue taste, the vagus nerve's contribution is to the efferent motor limb of the gag reflex and epiglottic taste rather than this tonsillar-adjacent branch, the hypoglossal nerve is purely motor to tongue musculature, and the lingual nerve carries general rather than gustatory sensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CN IX's combined role in posterior tongue taste and the gag reflex afferent limb explains why tonsillectomy, performed immediately adjacent to this nerve, carries a recognized risk of this specific complication. COMLEX: bedside gag reflex testing alongside taste testing helps confirm CN IX involvement in this and other posterior oropharyngeal injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 5 — taste bud and papillae anatomy with CN VII/IX/X fiber distribution, tested here as a board-style localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)","whyNot":{"D":"The facial nerve governs anter"}},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace taste information from the tongue, palate, pharynx, and epiglottis to the nucleus solitarius, thalamus, and gustatory cortex.","batch":3,"stem":"Third-order taste neurons in the parvicellular ventral posteromedial thalamic nucleus send their axons to the frontal operculum, anterior insula, and rostral area 3b. Through which white matter structure do these axons travel?","choices":{"A":"Contralateral central tegmental tract","B":"External capsule beside the putamen","C":"Posterior limb of the internal capsule","D":"Splenium of the corpus callosum","E":"Uncinate fasciculus of the temporal lobe"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Axons from the parvicellular VPM travel through the ipsilateral posterior limb of the internal capsule to the inner frontal operculum, anterior insular cortex, and rostral area 3b of the postcentral gyrus; the whole pathway is ipsilateral. The central tegmental tract carries second-order axons from the solitary nucleus up to the thalamus, and it does so ipsilaterally rather than contralaterally. The external capsule is a thin sheet lateral to the putamen carrying claustral and association fibers, the splenium links occipital and parietal cortex, and the uncinate fasciculus is an association bundle between frontal and temporal lobes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Taste pathway: rostral solitary nucleus, ipsilateral central tegmental tract, VPMpc, posterior limb of internal capsule, frontal operculum and anterior insula, all ipsilateral. COMLEX: A capsular lacune can cause ipsilateral hypogeusia alongside contralateral sensory loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 3 — the internal capsule as the route for corticospinal and thalamocortical fibers","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace taste information from the tongue, palate, pharynx, and epiglottis to the nucleus solitarius, thalamus, and gustatory cortex.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old man develops a right middle cerebral artery territory stroke involving the anterior insula and frontal operculum. He reports that food now tastes markedly abnormal and describes intermittent unprovoked nausea, though basic swallowing and salivation remain intact. Damage to which functional region best explains both the taste distortion and the visceral symptom together?","choices":{"A":"Insular gustatory-visceral integration cortex","B":"Primary sensory cortex, postcentral gyrus","C":"Primary motor cortex, precentral gyrus","D":"Broca area, frontal language cortex","E":"Wernicke area, temporal language cortex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The anterior insula and frontal operculum constitute primary gustatory cortex and also integrate visceral and interoceptive sensation, so a lesion here can simultaneously distort taste discrimination and produce unprovoked nausea through disrupted visceral integration, a combination unique to this region among cortical areas. The postcentral and precentral gyri process general somatosensory and motor function respectively, and Broca and Wernicke areas govern language production and comprehension, none of which would produce this specific combined taste-and-visceral presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the insula's dual role in gustatory discrimination and visceral/interoceptive integration explains why insular lesions can produce this seemingly unrelated symptom pairing. COMLEX: recognizing the insula as a shared substrate for taste and nausea helps make sense of visceral symptoms accompanying strokes in this vascular territory.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 6 — the central gustatory relay from solitary nucleus to VPMpc to insular cortex, tested here as a board-style lesion-tracing vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace taste information from the tongue, palate, pharynx, and epiglottis to the nucleus solitarius, thalamus, and gustatory cortex.","batch":3,"stem":"Two patients each have a small lesion confined to a different portion of the same brainstem nucleus. Patient one loses taste sensation but has entirely normal heart rate and blood pressure regulation. Patient two has labile blood pressure and abnormal cardiorespiratory reflexes but entirely normal taste. Which single nucleus, and which of its subdivisions, best explains this double dissociation?","choices":{"A":"Nucleus ambiguus, branchial motor versus cardiac subdivisions","B":"Dorsal motor vagal nucleus, gastric versus cardiac parts","C":"Spinal trigeminal nucleus, facial versus oral subdivisions","D":"Vestibular nuclei, superior versus inferior subdivisions","E":"Solitary nucleus, rostral gustatory versus caudal visceral parts"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The solitary nucleus has a functionally specialized rostral portion, the gustatory nucleus receiving taste afferents from CN VII, IX, and X, and a distinct caudal portion receiving general visceral, cardiorespiratory, afferents, so a lesion confined to the rostral part selectively impairs taste while sparing cardiovascular reflexes, and a lesion confined to the caudal part produces the opposite dissociation, exactly matching both patients. The nucleus ambiguus, dorsal motor vagal nucleus, spinal trigeminal nucleus, and vestibular nuclei are each single-function or differently organized structures that do not account for this specific rostral-taste versus caudal-visceral double dissociation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the solitary nucleus's rostral-gustatory versus caudal-visceral functional split within one anatomically continuous structure is a classic example of regional specialization worth mastering as a double dissociation. COMLEX: recognizing this rostrocaudal organization helps predict which combination of deficits a given medullary lesion will produce.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 6 — the central gustatory relay from solitary nucleus to VPMpc to insular cortex, tested here as a board-style lesion-tracing vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate true taste loss from perceived flavor loss caused by olfactory dysfunction.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman recovering from a upper respiratory infection reports that food \"has no taste at all\" for the past three weeks. Formal testing with sweet, salty, sour, bitter, and umami solutions applied directly to the tongue is entirely normal and symmetric. Which finding would you expect on formal olfactory threshold testing, and what does the overall pattern indicate?","choices":{"A":"Normal smell testing, confirming true gustatory loss","B":"Abnormal smell testing, confirming olfactory-mediated flavor loss","C":"Abnormal smell testing, confirming true gustatory loss","D":"Normal smell testing with no further explanation available","E":"Abnormal trigeminal testing, confirming a trigeminal cause"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because formal basic taste testing is normal, the gustatory system itself is intact, so this patient's complaint of complete taste loss most likely reflects impaired retronasal olfaction, since flavor perception depends heavily on smell, and formal olfactory threshold testing would be expected to show reduced sensitivity, consistent with common post-viral olfactory dysfunction. A normal gustatory result cannot simultaneously confirm true gustatory loss, since basic taste testing is normal by definition in this vignette, and trigeminal testing is not the relevant confirmatory test for this specific flavor-loss pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal basic taste testing plus a complaint of taste loss should always prompt formal smell testing, since post-viral olfactory dysfunction is a far more common explanation than true ageusia. COMLEX: this stepwise testing algorithm, taste first to confirm gustatory integrity, then smell, is the correct clinical approach to any chemosensory complaint.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 7 — true gustatory loss versus olfactory-mediated flavor loss, tested here as a board-style diagnostic-testing vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate true taste loss from perceived flavor loss caused by olfactory dysfunction.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old man undergoing chemotherapy reports food tastes bland and unpleasant. Formal testing reveals impaired detection of bitter and umami qualities on direct tongue application, while sweet and salty detection remain intact, and formal olfactory threshold testing is entirely normal. This pattern is best explained by dysfunction of which system, and why does it differ from a purely olfactory-mediated flavor complaint?","choices":{"A":"Olfactory system, since basic taste testing is itself abnormal","B":"Trigeminal system, since basic taste testing is itself abnormal","C":"Both gustatory and olfactory systems equally, with no way to distinguish them","D":"True gustatory system, since basic taste testing is itself abnormal","E":"Neither system, since chemotherapy has no chemosensory effects"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Unlike the purely olfactory-mediated flavor loss pattern, in which basic taste testing is normal, this patient has genuinely abnormal detection of specific taste qualities on direct tongue testing with entirely normal smell, confirming true gustatory system dysfunction, a recognized effect of certain chemotherapeutic agents on taste receptor cell turnover. Attributing this abnormal-taste-testing pattern to the olfactory or trigeminal systems, both of which are not directly tested by tongue-applied taste solutions, would be incorrect, the two systems are distinguishable precisely by this pattern of results rather than indistinguishable, and chemotherapy-induced dysgeusia is a well-recognized clinical entity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: abnormal basic taste testing with normal smell testing is the mirror-image pattern confirming true ageusia or dysgeusia rather than olfactory-mediated flavor loss, a discrimination worth mastering in both directions. COMLEX: chemotherapy-induced taste changes are a common, clinically significant contributor to poor oral intake and weight loss during cancer treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 7 — true gustatory loss versus olfactory-mediated flavor loss, tested here as a board-style diagnostic-testing vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"A":"Attributing this abnormal-tast","C":"Attributing this abnormal-tast","E":"Attributing this abnormal-tast"}},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate true taste loss from perceived flavor loss caused by olfactory dysfunction.","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman taking an ACE inhibitor for hypertension develops a persistent metallic taste. She has no nasal symptoms, and formal smell testing is normal, but she describes the metallic sensation as present even when nothing is in her mouth. Which mechanism best distinguishes this presentation from an olfactory-mediated flavor complaint?","choices":{"A":"Direct drug effect on taste receptor cell turnover or zinc handling","B":"Impaired retronasal olfaction from medication-induced rhinitis","C":"Reduced detection of all five basic taste qualities equally","D":"Central dorsomedial thalamic dysfunction from the medication","E":"Trigeminal nerve irritation caused directly by the medication"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ACE inhibitors and certain other medications are recognized to cause true dysgeusia through a direct effect on taste receptor cell turnover or zinc-dependent taste bud function, producing a persistent abnormal taste sensation independent of eating and with normal olfactory testing, distinguishing this mechanism from an olfactory-mediated flavor complaint, which would instead show abnormal smell testing. Rhinitis-driven olfactory impairment does not fit given normal smell testing, a uniform reduction across all five basic qualities is not the described pattern, and neither central dorsomedial thalamic dysfunction nor trigeminal irritation is the recognized mechanism for this common medication-associated symptom.","boardPrep":"Step 1: medication-induced dysgeusia, exemplified by ACE inhibitors, is a true gustatory phenomenon with normal smell testing, useful for building a complete differential of taste complaints beyond olfactory causes. COMLEX: reviewing medications is an essential step whenever a patient reports a persistent abnormal taste, since drug-induced dysgeusia is common and often reversible.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 7 — true gustatory loss versus olfactory-mediated flavor loss, tested here as a board-style diagnostic-testing vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical features to localize lesions involving CN I, chorda tympani, CN IX, CN X, nucleus solitarius, thalamus, or gustatory cortex.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a facial nerve schwannoma has loss of taste over the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, decreased tearing on the same side, hyperacusis, and facial weakness. A second patient with facial nerve injury during parotid surgery, distal to the stylomastoid foramen, has facial weakness alone, with normal taste, tearing, and hearing. Which structure's involvement, present in the first patient and spared in the second, best explains the difference?","choices":{"A":"Facial nerve segment distal to the stylomastoid foramen","B":"Facial nerve segment within the parotid gland","C":"Chorda tympani branch in isolation, without proximal trunk involvement","D":"Facial nerve segment proximal to chorda tympani branching","E":"Greater petrosal nerve in isolation, without proximal trunk involvement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The first patient's combined taste loss, decreased tearing, hyperacusis, and facial weakness indicates a lesion proximal to where the chorda tympani, greater petrosal nerve, and nerve to stapedius branch off the facial nerve trunk, so all of these functions are lost together, while the second patient's isolated facial weakness from a distal, post-stylomastoid foramen injury occurs after all of these branches have already departed the trunk, sparing taste, tearing, and hearing modulation entirely. A lesion confined to the parotid segment or to either branch in isolation would not reproduce the first patient's full combined deficit pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mapping exactly where along the facial nerve's intracranial and intratemporal course a lesion sits, using which branches are affected, is one of the most powerful board localization tools for CN VII pathology. COMLEX: this same stepwise branch-point reasoning distinguishes a proximal facial nerve palsy from an isolated peripheral branch injury after parotid or other distal surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 8 — the chemosensory lesion-localization framework, tested here as a board-style multi-finding discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"B":"A lesion confined to the parot","C":"A lesion confined to the parot"}},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical features to localize lesions involving CN I, chorda tympani, CN IX, CN X, nucleus solitarius, thalamus, or gustatory cortex.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with a glomus jugulare tumor develops loss of taste and gag sensation over the posterior third of his tongue, hoarseness with vocal cord paralysis, and weakness turning his head against resistance. Compression at which skull base structure best accounts for this combination of findings?","choices":{"A":"Jugular foramen, combined CN IX, X, XI compression","B":"Internal acoustic meatus, combined CN VII, VIII compression","C":"Cavernous sinus, combined CN III, IV, VI compression","D":"Foramen magnum, combined lower brainstem compression","E":"Superior orbital fissure, combined ocular motor compression"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The jugular foramen transmits cranial nerves IX, X, and XI together, so a mass here, such as a glomus jugulare tumor, compresses all three simultaneously, producing posterior tongue taste and gag loss from CN IX, hoarseness and vocal cord paralysis from CN X, and weakness of the sternocleidomastoid from CN XI, together known as Vernet syndrome. The internal acoustic meatus carries CN VII and VIII, the cavernous sinus carries the ocular motor nerves, the foramen magnum transmits the lower brainstem and spinal cord rather than these specific cranial nerves as isolated exiting structures, and the superior orbital fissure carries the same ocular motor nerves as the cavernous sinus, none matching this triad.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the jugular foramen as the shared exit point for CN IX, X, and XI, producing Vernet syndrome when compressed, is a classic skull base localization fact. COMLEX: this combined cranial nerve pattern in an older adult should prompt imaging to evaluate for a glomus tumor or other jugular foramen mass.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 8 — the chemosensory lesion-localization framework, tested here as a board-style multi-finding discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical features to localize lesions involving CN I, chorda tympani, CN IX, CN X, nucleus solitarius, thalamus, or gustatory cortex.","batch":3,"stem":"Two patients each present with loss of taste on one side of the tongue. Patient one also has labile blood pressure and irregular breathing patterns. Patient two has isolated taste loss confined to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, with entirely normal cardiovascular and respiratory function. Which localization best distinguishes the two, given that both findings could theoretically arise from damage to the same brainstem nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Patient one has a central nucleus lesion; patient two has a peripheral lesion","B":"Patient one has a peripheral nerve lesion; patient two has a caudal solitary nucleus lesion","C":"Both patients necessarily share the identical central lesion location","D":"Neither pattern can be explained by solitary nucleus anatomy","E":"Only a peripheral lesion can explain isolated taste loss in either patient"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A lesion extending into the caudal, visceral portion of the solitary nucleus, as in patient one, would produce both taste loss, from rostral gustatory nucleus involvement, and cardiorespiratory instability, from caudal visceral nucleus involvement, whereas a peripheral gustatory nerve lesion, as in patient two, produces isolated taste loss without any central visceral reflex disturbance, since the solitary nucleus itself remains intact. Reversing these localizations would be incorrect, the two patients clearly do not share an identical lesion location, solitary nucleus anatomy does explain this exact dissociation, a peripheral lesion cannot explain patient one's cardiorespiratory instability, and taste loss limited to the anterior two-thirds fits a single peripheral chorda tympani or facial nerve lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: using accompanying cardiorespiratory findings, or their absence, to distinguish a central solitary nucleus lesion from a peripheral gustatory nerve lesion is a high-level integrative localization skill. COMLEX: this same central-versus-peripheral reasoning pattern generalizes to nearly every cranial nerve localization problem on the exam.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 8 — the chemosensory lesion-localization framework, tested here as a board-style multi-finding discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"D":"Reversing these localizations ","E":"Reversing these localizations "}},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q25","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Connect olfaction and taste to feeding behavior, salivation, nausea, autonomic responses, and limbic memory/emotion.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient undergoing chemotherapy develops nausea shortly after each infusion. Over subsequent cycles, she reports that the smell of a specific herbal tea she happened to drink before her first infusion now independently triggers nausea, even when the chemotherapy itself is not being administered. Which circuit best explains this learned association between a chemosensory stimulus and a visceral response?","choices":{"A":"Cerebellar association between olfactory input and motor coordination","B":"Basal ganglia association between olfactory input and movement selection","C":"Primary visual cortex association between olfactory input and visual memory","D":"Corticospinal association between olfactory input and voluntary motor output pathway","E":"Amygdalar and insular association between olfactory input and visceral illness"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This pattern describes conditioned taste and odor aversion, in which the amygdala and insular cortex link a chemosensory stimulus present at the time of visceral illness to that illness itself, so the smell alone becomes sufficient to trigger nausea on subsequent exposure, a well-documented phenomenon in chemotherapy patients. The cerebellum, basal ganglia, primary visual cortex, and corticospinal system are not the structures responsible for associating chemosensory input with visceral illness in this learned aversion circuit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: conditioned taste and odor aversion via amygdalar-insular association is a classic example of olfactory-limbic-visceral integration with direct clinical relevance to oncology patients. COMLEX: anticipating and counseling patients about this phenomenon, avoiding favorite foods or scents around chemotherapy administration, can prevent unwanted long-term aversions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 9 — olfactory and gustatory links to feeding, autonomic, and limbic circuits, tested here as a board-style integrative vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"A":"The cerebellum, basal ganglia,","D":"The cerebellum, basal ganglia,"}},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q26","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Connect olfaction and taste to feeding behavior, salivation, nausea, autonomic responses, and limbic memory/emotion.","batch":3,"stem":"A researcher measures salivary flow in volunteers before any food is placed in the mouth, simply upon exposure to the smell of baking bread. Salivary flow increases significantly during this anticipatory, cephalic phase response. Which pathway most directly mediates this smell-triggered increase in salivation?","choices":{"A":"Olfactory input relayed directly to the parotid gland without any brainstem relay","B":"Olfactory input relayed through forebrain and hypothalamic centers to the brainstem salivatory nuclei","C":"Olfactory input relayed to the cerebellar vermis for motor coordination purposes","D":"Olfactory input relayed to primary motor cortex for direct glandular control purposes","E":"Olfactory input relayed to the pupillary light reflex circuit"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The smell of food reaches limbic and hypothalamic centers that project to the superior and inferior salivatory nuclei of the brainstem, driving anticipatory, cephalic phase salivation before food ever reaches the mouth, an adaptive digestive reflex. Salivary glands have no direct connection to olfactory input bypassing the brainstem, the cerebellar vermis governs motor coordination rather than secretomotor reflexes, primary motor cortex does not directly control glandular secretion, and the pupillary light reflex circuit is unrelated to salivation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cephalic phase salivary response, triggered by smell acting through brainstem salivatory nuclei, illustrates the functional link between chemosensory input and digestive reflex physiology. COMLEX: this same anticipatory reflex principle underlies why appetizing food aromas can stimulate appetite and digestive readiness before eating begins.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 9 — olfactory and gustatory links to feeding, autonomic, and limbic circuits, tested here as a board-style integrative vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q27","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Connect olfaction and taste to feeding behavior, salivation, nausea, autonomic responses, and limbic memory/emotion.","batch":3,"stem":"An 81-year-old man with post-traumatic anosmia following a fall one year ago has since lost significant weight and reports that he no longer finds any food enjoyable, though he denies depressed mood otherwise and his gastrointestinal workup is unremarkable. Disruption of which functional link best explains this behavioral consequence of his anosmia?","choices":{"A":"Olfactory-cerebellar circuit driving fully coordinated chewing movements themselves","B":"Olfactory-corticospinal circuit driving voluntary swallowing initiation","C":"Olfactory-limbic-hypothalamic circuit driving food-related reward and appetite","D":"Olfactory-vestibular circuit driving postural stability during eating itself","E":"Olfactory-basal ganglia circuit driving fine motor utensil use itself"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because flavor and food-related reward depend heavily on olfactory input reaching limbic structures and the lateral hypothalamus, complete anosmia can disrupt this circuit sufficiently to reduce food-related pleasure and drive, producing decreased intake and weight loss even without primary depression or gastrointestinal disease. The cerebellar, corticospinal, vestibular, and basal ganglia circuits listed govern motor coordination, swallowing initiation, postural stability, and fine motor control respectively, none of which explain a purely hedonic, appetite-related consequence of anosmia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: anosmia-associated weight loss through disrupted olfactory-limbic-hypothalamic reward circuitry is an underrecognized but testable real-world consequence of this SDL's core anatomy. COMLEX: screening for unintentional weight loss and reduced food enjoyment is a worthwhile part of following any patient with new persistent anosmia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 9 — olfactory and gustatory links to feeding, autonomic, and limbic circuits, tested here as a board-style integrative vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"A":"The cerebellar, corticospinal,"}},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q28","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Apply the pathways to board-style questions involving head trauma, skull base tumors, Bell palsy, lateral medullary syndrome, temporal lobe seizures, and neurodegenerative disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 54-year-old man has months of right-sided nasal obstruction, recurrent nosebleeds, and loss of smell. Endoscopy shows a mass high in the nasal cavity, and biopsy shows a tumor of primitive neuroectodermal origin arising from the olfactory neuroepithelium. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Olfactory groove meningioma","B":"Inflammatory nasal polyposis","C":"Pituitary macroadenoma","D":"Craniopharyngioma","E":"Esthesioneuroblastoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Olfactory neuroblastoma, or esthesioneuroblastoma, is thought to arise from olfactory neuroepithelial cells in the nasal cavity and causes nasal obstruction and epistaxis along with smell loss. An olfactory groove meningioma arises from arachnoid cells along the cribriform plate inside the skull, nasal polyps are noncancerous inflammatory growths, and pituitary adenoma and craniopharyngioma are sellar masses that present with visual field and endocrine changes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Esthesioneuroblastoma: nasal mass with epistaxis and anosmia from olfactory neuroepithelium. COMLEX: Unilateral anosmia with nasal bleeding warrants endoscopy and skull base imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 5 — imaging and histopathology of intracranial and skull base tumors","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q29","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Apply the pathways to board-style questions involving head trauma, skull base tumors, Bell palsy, lateral medullary syndrome, temporal lobe seizures, and neurodegenerative disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient develops acute unilateral facial weakness. On further testing, taste is lost over the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, hyperacusis is present, but tearing is entirely normal on the affected side. Which precise segment of the facial nerve's course best localizes this lesion, given that a more proximal lesion would also impair tearing?","choices":{"A":"Facial canal, proximal to both the greater petrosal nerve and the chorda tympani","B":"Facial canal, distal to the petrosal nerve but proximal to the chorda tympani","C":"Facial canal, distal to both the greater petrosal nerve and chorda tympani branches","D":"Cerebellopontine angle, proximal to the entire facial canal itself","E":"Stylomastoid foramen, distal to the entire facial canal itself"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because tearing, governed by the greater petrosal nerve, is spared while taste and hyperacusis, governed by the chorda tympani and nerve to stapedius respectively, are both affected, the lesion must lie within the facial canal at a point distal to where the greater petrosal nerve has already branched off but still proximal to the chorda tympani and stapedial branches. A lesion proximal to both branches would impair tearing as well, a lesion distal to both branches would spare taste and hyperacusis, a cerebellopontine angle lesion proximal to the facial canal would typically also affect tearing and often additional cranial nerves, and a stylomastoid foramen lesion distal to the entire facial canal would spare taste, hyperacusis, and tearing altogether, producing facial weakness alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sequentially mapping which facial nerve branches are spared versus affected pinpoints the lesion to a specific segment of the facial canal, the single most sophisticated localization skill this SDL builds toward. COMLEX: this stepwise branch-by-branch reasoning is exactly how Bell palsy severity and expected recovery pattern are clinically assessed at the bedside.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 10 — the full chemosensory pathway applied to named clinical syndromes, tested here as a board-style capstone vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl37_bloom_q30","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Apply the pathways to board-style questions involving head trauma, skull base tumors, Bell palsy, lateral medullary syndrome, temporal lobe seizures, and neurodegenerative disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman with a known olfactory groove meningioma has had anosmia for a year. She now develops double vision from ophthalmoplegia and numbness over the upper face on the same side. Extension of the tumor into which structure best explains the new findings?","choices":{"A":"Jugular foramen","B":"Internal acoustic meatus","C":"Cavernous sinus","D":"Foramen magnum","E":"Cerebellopontine angle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Olfactory groove meningiomas can extend into the cavernous sinus, where they produce extraocular palsies from CN III, IV, and VI and facial numbness from V1 and V2, in addition to the original smell loss. The jugular foramen holds CN IX, X, and XI, the internal acoustic meatus and cerebellopontine angle involve CN VII and VIII, and the foramen magnum involves the lower medulla and upper cord; none of these would explain ophthalmoplegia with upper facial numbness from an anterior skull base tumor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cavernous sinus contents: CN III, IV, V1, V2, VI and the internal carotid artery. COMLEX: An anterior skull base meningioma may also compress the optic nerve, producing Foster Kennedy syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 8 — cavernous sinus lesions as a high-yield visual motor pattern","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"}],"38":[{"id":"sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the anterior horn cell as the lower motor neuron final common pathway for skeletal muscle.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the anterior horn motor neuron called the final common pathway for skeletal muscle?","choices":{"A":"It is the first neuron activated whenever a movement is planned in cortex","B":"It receives input only from the lateral corticospinal tract and no other source","C":"It converts incoming proprioceptive signals into conscious awareness of limb position","D":"It is the only neuron whose axon synapses directly on skeletal muscle fibers","E":"It is found only within the cervical and lumbosacral spinal cord enlargements"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lower motor neurons in the anterior horn are the sole point of direct synaptic contact between the nervous system and skeletal muscle. Every descending pathway, corticospinal, vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, rubrospinal, and every spinal reflex arc converges on this same neuron before a muscle can contract, hence final common pathway, a term coined by Sherrington. Damage here produces lower motor neuron signs: flaccid weakness, hyporeflexia, atrophy, and fasciculations, distinct from the pattern produced by upper motor neuron lesions above the anterior horn.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LMN = 'everything lowered,' tone, reflexes, and toes go down, a classic First Aid mnemonic. COMLEX: reflex and tone testing at the bedside directly probes final common pathway integrity, a core physical exam skill.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 5 — the UMN vs LMN sign differentiation framework, now grounding the anterior horn cell itself as the LMN and final common pathway for skeletal muscle","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Every descending pathway, cort"}},{"id":"sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the anterior horn cell as the lower motor neuron final common pathway for skeletal muscle.","batch":1,"stem":"Extraocular muscles need very fine control, whereas the large antigravity muscles of the leg generate high force. Compared with the leg muscles, the motor units of the extraocular muscles have which feature?","choices":{"A":"Many more muscle fibers supplied by each motor axon","B":"Far fewer muscle fibers supplied by each motor axon","C":"Several motor end plates on each individual muscle fiber","D":"Two separate alpha motor axons supplying each muscle fiber","E":"Muscle fibers supplied directly by gamma motor neurons"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A motor unit is one alpha motor neuron with all the muscle fibers it innervates; as the need for fine control rises, the innervation ratio falls, so extraocular muscles have small units of about 10 to 100 fibers, whereas powerful lumbosacral-supplied muscles have units of 600 to 1000 fibers. Each muscle fiber receives only one motor end plate from one alpha motor axon, so multiple end plates or dual innervation do not occur. Gamma motor neurons supply intrafusal fibers of spindles, not the working extrafusal fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Small motor units give precision, large motor units give force; small units are recruited first by the size principle. COMLEX: In denervation, surviving axons sprout and enlarge their units, producing large motor unit potentials on EMG.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 2 — alpha versus gamma motor neurons and the fibers each supplies","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Each muscle fiber receives onl"}},{"id":"sdl38_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the anterior horn cell as the lower motor neuron final common pathway for skeletal muscle.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair of findings most specifically points to a lower motor neuron lesion rather than an upper motor neuron lesion?","choices":{"A":"Spasticity and a positive Babinski sign on the affected side","B":"Hyperreflexia and clonus without any change in muscle bulk","C":"Fasciculations and muscle atrophy on the affected side","D":"Clasp-knife rigidity with preserved deep tendon reflexes","E":"Increased tone with pronator drift on the affected arm"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fasciculations, visible spontaneous contractions of whole motor units from an irritable or degenerating lower motor neuron, and atrophy from loss of trophic innervation are hallmark LMN signs because they require direct denervation of the muscle, something only a lesion of the anterior horn cell or its axon can cause. Spasticity, hyperreflexia, clonus, a positive Babinski sign, and clasp-knife rigidity all reflect loss of descending inhibitory control and are UMN signs instead.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fasciculations point to active denervation, tested with EMG showing fibrillations and positive sharp waves. COMLEX: always pair reflex and tone findings with atrophy/fasciculation status to separate UMN from LMN on the boards.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 5 — the UMN vs LMN sign differentiation framework, now grounding the anterior horn cell itself as the LMN and final common pathway for skeletal muscle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate alpha motor neurons, gamma motor neurons, extrafusal muscle fibers, and intrafusal muscle fibers.","batch":1,"stem":"Which motor neuron type directly innervates the ordinary, force-generating extrafusal fibers of skeletal muscle?","choices":{"A":"Type Ib afferent neuron","B":"Gamma motor neuron","C":"Type Ia afferent neuron","D":"Alpha motor neuron","E":"Postganglionic neuron"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Alpha motor neurons are large, fast-conducting neurons whose axons leave the anterior horn and terminate on ordinary extrafusal muscle fibers, the workhorse fibers that generate force and shorten the whole muscle. Gamma motor neurons, by contrast, innervate only the small intrafusal fibers housed inside muscle spindles and never contact extrafusal fibers directly, so they cannot by themselves produce whole-muscle contraction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alpha motor neuron loss causes flaccid paralysis of the whole muscle; gamma motor neuron loss subtly impairs spindle sensitivity without causing overt weakness. COMLEX: this alpha/gamma distinction underlies why deep tendon reflex testing remains useful even in partial nerve injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — gray matter horns as the anatomic substrate for localization, now split into the alpha and gamma motor neuron populations that occupy the anterior horn","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate alpha motor neurons, gamma motor neurons, extrafusal muscle fibers, and intrafusal muscle fibers.","batch":1,"stem":"Gamma motor neurons innervate which structure?","choices":{"A":"Extrafusal muscle fibers throughout the muscle belly","B":"Golgi tendon organs at the musculotendinous junction","C":"Free nerve endings scattered within the muscle fascia","D":"Intrafusal muscle fibers within the muscle spindle","E":"Motor end plates on fast-twitch extrafusal fibers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gamma motor neurons synapse exclusively on the contractile polar regions of intrafusal muscle fibers, the specialized striated fibers found only inside muscle spindles. Their contraction stretches the noncontractile equatorial region of the spindle, which raises the firing rate of the Ia and II sensory endings wrapped around it. This is distinct from alpha motor neurons, which innervate the bulk extrafusal fibers that actually shorten the whole muscle. Golgi tendon organs are sensory endings in the tendon whose type Ib afferents signal tension; no motor neuron supplies them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gamma motor neurons are the efferent limb of the gamma loop that maintains spindle sensitivity during active contraction. COMLEX: alpha-gamma coactivation keeps deep tendon reflexes testable throughout a voluntary movement, not just at rest.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — gray matter horns as the anatomic substrate for localization, now split into the alpha and gamma motor neuron populations that occupy the anterior horn","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"This is distinct from alpha mo","E":"This is distinct from alpha mo"}},{"id":"sdl38_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate alpha motor neurons, gamma motor neurons, extrafusal muscle fibers, and intrafusal muscle fibers.","batch":1,"stem":"Which statement best distinguishes extrafusal from intrafusal muscle fibers?","choices":{"A":"Extrafusal fibers occur only in postural muscles; intrafusal fibers only in phasic muscles","B":"Extrafusal fibers are innervated by sensory neurons; intrafusal fibers by motor neurons only","C":"Extrafusal fibers lack myelinated innervation; intrafusal fibers are heavily myelinated","D":"Extrafusal fibers are smooth muscle; intrafusal fibers are striated skeletal muscle","E":"Extrafusal fibers generate force; intrafusal fibers regulate spindle sensitivity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Extrafusal fibers make up the bulk of a skeletal muscle and are innervated by alpha motor neurons; their contraction produces the muscle's actual force and movement. Intrafusal fibers are a small, specialized population enclosed within the muscle spindle capsule, innervated by gamma motor neurons at their polar ends and by Ia and II sensory afferents at their equatorial region; their job is sensory, not force-generating, keeping the spindle able to report muscle length throughout a contraction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: both fiber types are striated skeletal muscle, a common distractor is calling intrafusal fibers smooth muscle. COMLEX: understanding this split explains why spindle-based reflexes stay accurate during active, not just passive, movement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — gray matter horns as the anatomic substrate for localization, now split into the alpha and gamma motor neuron populations that occupy the anterior horn","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how muscle spindles detect length/stretch and how Golgi tendon organs detect tension.","batch":1,"stem":"Within the muscle spindle, which type of intrafusal fiber is primarily sensitive to the rate of change in muscle length rather than to length alone?","choices":{"A":"Static nuclear bag fiber","B":"Nuclear chain fiber","C":"Dynamic nuclear bag fiber","D":"Type I extrafusal fiber","E":"Type IIb extrafusal fiber"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dynamic nuclear bag fibers are primarily sensitive to the rate of change in muscle length, and they are controlled by dynamic gamma motor neurons. Static nuclear bag fibers and nuclear chain fibers signal a change in length but not its rate, and they are supplied by static gamma motor neurons. Type I and type IIb fibers are extrafusal working fibers of slow and fast-fatigable motor units, not spindle receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spindle fibers: dynamic bag (rate, Ia), static bag and chain (length, Ia and II); in bag fibers the nuclei cluster centrally, in chain fibers they form a row. COMLEX: Gamma drive sets spindle sensitivity, which is why reflex gain rises in spasticity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 5 — type Ia and type II spindle afferents and the parameters they encode","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how muscle spindles detect length/stretch and how Golgi tendon organs detect tension.","batch":1,"stem":"The Golgi tendon organ is best described as a receptor that detects which parameter?","choices":{"A":"Length of the muscle at rest before any contraction begins","B":"Rate of passive stretch applied to the muscle belly","C":"Tension developed within the tendon during muscle contraction","D":"Position of the joint relative to neutral anatomic resting posture","E":"Velocity of active shortening during a ballistic movement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Golgi tendon organs sit in series with extrafusal muscle fibers at the musculotendinous junction, so tension generated anywhere along the muscle-tendon unit, most strongly during active contraction, stretches the collagen fibers interlaced with the sensory terminals and activates type Ib afferents. This is fundamentally different from the muscle spindle, which lies in parallel with extrafusal fibers and signals length and stretch rate rather than tension. Joint position is reported by joint receptors that signal how far each joint is rotated, not by a tension sensor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Golgi tendon organs use type Ib fibers and drive autogenic inhibition through an inhibitory interneuron. COMLEX: excessive tendon organ activation during a stretch reflex test can cause a protective clasp-knife release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — the cutaneous mechanoreceptor and proprioceptor survey, now applied to the two dedicated muscle proprioceptors that report length and tension","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Joint position is reported by "}},{"id":"sdl38_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how muscle spindles detect length/stretch and how Golgi tendon organs detect tension.","batch":1,"stem":"Which afferent fiber type carries information from the Golgi tendon organ to the spinal cord?","choices":{"A":"Type Ia","B":"Type Ib","C":"Type II","D":"Type III","E":"Type IV"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Type Ib fibers are large, heavily myelinated afferents that arise from Golgi tendon organs and enter the spinal cord to synapse on inhibitory interneurons, which in turn inhibit the alpha motor neurons of the same muscle, autogenic inhibition. Type Ia fibers instead arise from the primary endings of muscle spindles, and type II fibers from the secondary spindle endings; types III and IV are the smaller, slower fibers that carry pain and temperature information, not proprioception.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fiber typing by receptor, Ia = spindle primary, Ib = Golgi tendon organ, II = spindle secondary, is a favorite matching-style question. COMLEX: know that Ib input is inhibitory to the agonist, opposite of Ia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — the cutaneous mechanoreceptor and proprioceptor survey, now applied to the two dedicated muscle proprioceptors that report length and tension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace the stretch reflex, inverse stretch reflex, reciprocal inhibition, flexor withdrawal reflex, and crossed-extension reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"Striking the patellar tendon with a reflex hammer produces knee extension through which reflex circuit?","choices":{"A":"Polysynaptic flexor withdrawal reflex","B":"Autogenic inverse stretch reflex","C":"Crossed-extension reflex","D":"Reciprocal inhibition circuit alone","E":"Monosynaptic stretch reflex"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tapping the patellar tendon stretches the quadriceps muscle spindle, activating Ia afferents that form a monosynaptic excitatory synapse directly onto the quadriceps alpha motor neuron, producing rapid reflex contraction and knee extension. This same Ia afferent simultaneously activates an inhibitory interneuron that suppresses the antagonist hamstring motor neuron, reciprocal inhibition, but the extension itself is generated by the monosynaptic Ia-to-alpha connection, not by reciprocal inhibition alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the deep tendon reflex is the textbook example of a monosynaptic reflex arc, one synapse between afferent and efferent. COMLEX: reflex grading, 0 to 4+, is a core neuromuscular exam skill tested repeatedly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 7 — dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes used for bedside lesion localization, now tracing the actual reflex arc circuitry being tested","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace the stretch reflex, inverse stretch reflex, reciprocal inhibition, flexor withdrawal reflex, and crossed-extension reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"Which reflex is mediated by type Ib afferents from the Golgi tendon organ inhibiting the same muscle's alpha motor neuron?","choices":{"A":"Monosynaptic stretch reflex","B":"Flexor withdrawal reflex","C":"Crossed-extension reflex","D":"Inverse stretch reflex","E":"Vestibulo-ocular reflex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The inverse stretch reflex, also called autogenic inhibition, begins when tension in the tendon activates Golgi tendon organ Ib afferents; these fibers excite an inhibitory interneuron in the spinal cord that suppresses the alpha motor neuron supplying the same muscle, protecting it from excessive tension. This is the functional opposite of the monosynaptic stretch reflex, in which Ia afferents excite, rather than inhibit, the same-muscle alpha motor neuron.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 'inverse' stretch reflex is inhibitory and polysynaptic, unlike the excitatory monosynaptic stretch reflex; a classic paired-contrast question. COMLEX: excessive Golgi tendon organ inhibition contributes to the clasp-knife phenomenon in spasticity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 7 — dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes used for bedside lesion localization, now tracing the actual reflex arc circuitry being tested","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace the stretch reflex, inverse stretch reflex, reciprocal inhibition, flexor withdrawal reflex, and crossed-extension reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"Stepping on a tack with the right foot causes right leg flexion and left leg extension through which combined reflex?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral monosynaptic stretch reflex without cutaneous input","B":"Bilateral inverse stretch reflex triggered by tendon tension","C":"Reciprocal inhibition circuit acting without any withdrawal","D":"Flexor withdrawal with crossed-extension reflex","E":"Vestibulospinal righting reflex triggered by loss of balance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A noxious cutaneous stimulus activates the flexor withdrawal reflex, a polysynaptic circuit that flexes the ipsilateral limb to pull it away from the stimulus, while activating extensors and inhibiting flexors on the contralateral side through the crossed-extension reflex so that limb can bear weight and maintain balance. Both reflexes are driven by cutaneous and nociceptive afferents relayed through spinal interneurons and propriospinal connections, not by the muscle spindle or Golgi tendon organ.","boardPrep":"Step 1: crossed-extension always pairs with flexor withdrawal, tested together as a single functional unit for postural support. COMLEX: this circuit is the physiologic basis for withdrawal-type reflex testing in infants.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 7 — dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes used for bedside lesion localization, now tracing the actual reflex arc circuitry being tested","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the role of Ia, Ib, II, and cutaneous afferents in spinal motor control.","batch":1,"stem":"Which afferent fiber type arises from the primary annulospiral ending of the muscle spindle?","choices":{"A":"Type Ib","B":"Type Ia","C":"Type II","D":"Type III","E":"Type IV"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Type Ia afferents are large, heavily myelinated fibers whose annulospiral endings wrap around the equatorial region of both dynamic and static nuclear bag intrafusal fibers, firing phasically in proportion to the rate and magnitude of stretch. Type Ib fibers instead arise from Golgi tendon organs, and type II fibers arise from the secondary flower-spray endings on nuclear chain fibers; types III and IV carry nociceptive and thermal information, not spindle proprioception.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ia is the fastest, largest fiber, from the spindle primary ending; this fiber-to-receptor map is tested directly and via conduction velocity questions. COMLEX: Ia conduction velocity, roughly 80 to 120 meters per second, is the fastest in the body.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — the A-alpha/A-beta/A-delta/C fiber classification by diameter and conduction velocity, now applied to the Ia, Ib, and II afferents of spinal motor control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the role of Ia, Ib, II, and cutaneous afferents in spinal motor control.","batch":1,"stem":"Type II afferent fibers from the muscle spindle secondary ending mainly encode which parameter?","choices":{"A":"Static muscle length without signaling the rate of change","B":"Dynamic rate of stretch with only minimal length information provided","C":"Tension generated at the musculotendinous junction during contraction","D":"Cutaneous pressure sensed over the overlying skin surface","E":"Joint angle sensed by capsular joint mechanoreceptors directly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Type II fibers form secondary, flower-spray endings mainly on nuclear chain intrafusal fibers and fire tonically at a rate proportional to the current length of the muscle, but they do not strongly encode the rate of stretch the way type Ia fibers from dynamic nuclear bag fibers do. Golgi tendon organs, not spindle afferents, signal tension, and cutaneous or joint receptors are separate systems entirely from the muscle proprioceptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pair type Ia with 'dynamic, rate of change' and type II with 'static, length only,' a distinction commonly tested against each other. COMLEX: this length-coding role supports postural muscle tone maintenance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — the A-alpha/A-beta/A-delta/C fiber classification by diameter and conduction velocity, now applied to the Ia, Ib, and II afferents of spinal motor control","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Golgi tendon organs, not spind","D":"Golgi tendon organs, not spind","E":"Golgi tendon organs, not spind"}},{"id":"sdl38_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the role of Ia, Ib, II, and cutaneous afferents in spinal motor control.","batch":1,"stem":"Cutaneous afferents contribute to spinal motor control primarily by driving which type of reflex circuit?","choices":{"A":"Polysynaptic withdrawal and crossed-extension reflexes","B":"Autogenic inhibition identical to Ib input","C":"Direct alpha motor neuron excitation with no interneuron","D":"Gamma loop modulation of spindle sensitivity","E":"Monosynaptic stretch reflexes identical to Ia input"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Unlike the monosynaptic Ia connection that mediates the stretch reflex, cutaneous afferents activate interneurons and propriospinal neurons that distribute their input across multiple spinal segments and to the contralateral side, forming the polysynaptic basis for flexor withdrawal and crossed-extension reflexes in response to noxious or tactile stimuli. Cutaneous input does not synapse directly on alpha motor neurons and plays no direct role in the gamma loop, which is driven by supraspinal input to gamma motor neurons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cutaneous afferents are polysynaptic and multisegmental, contrasted with the single-segment monosynaptic Ia stretch reflex. COMLEX: withdrawal reflex testing in a comatose or spinal-cord-injured patient localizes the level of intact cutaneous-motor circuitry.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — the A-alpha/A-beta/A-delta/C fiber classification by diameter and conduction velocity, now applied to the Ia, Ib, and II afferents of spinal motor control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the anterior horn cell as the lower motor neuron final common pathway for skeletal muscle.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man presents with progressive weakness of his right hand over 8 months. Exam shows atrophy of the thenar and hypothenar muscles, visible fasciculations in the forearm, and absent deep tendon reflexes at the wrist. Strength is reduced but sensation is fully intact throughout the limb. Which structure is most likely primarily affected?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal root ganglion of the cervical spinal nerve","B":"Lateral corticospinal tract in the cervical cord","C":"Anterior horn cell of the cervical spinal cord","D":"Dorsal column of the cervical spinal cord","E":"Median nerve at the carpal tunnel"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Atrophy, fasciculations, and hyporeflexia with fully preserved sensation and no single peripheral nerve distribution points to a pure motor, lower motor neuron process at the anterior horn cell itself, the final common pathway for the involved myotomes. A dorsal root ganglion or dorsal column lesion would produce sensory loss, which is absent here; a corticospinal tract lesion would produce upper motor neuron signs, spasticity and hyperreflexia, not atrophy and fasciculations; a single peripheral nerve lesion like carpal tunnel would not explain both thenar and hypothenar involvement together.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the classic ALS presentation pattern when confined to LMN findings early on; watch for later UMN signs to appear as the disease progresses. COMLEX: pure LMN findings with no sensory loss should always raise anterior horn cell disease on the differential.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — anterior horn cell disease presenting as isolated LMN findings with sensation spared","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the anterior horn cell as the lower motor neuron final common pathway for skeletal muscle.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-month-old infant is brought in for marked hypotonia, a floppy posture, and inability to hold up her head. Exam reveals tongue fasciculations, absent deep tendon reflexes, and symmetric proximal weakness. Sensory exam appears grossly intact. Genetic testing reveals a homozygous SMN1 mutation. Which cellular population is being destroyed in this condition?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal root ganglion sensory neurons bilaterally","B":"Oligodendrocytes of the lateral corticospinal tract","C":"Schwann cells of the peripheral motor nerves","D":"Anterior horn cells throughout the spinal cord","E":"Purkinje cells within the cerebellar cortex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This vignette describes spinal muscular atrophy, an autosomal recessive SMN1 mutation that causes defective snRNP assembly and apoptosis of anterior horn cells, the pure lower motor neurons, producing symmetric flaccid weakness, hypotonia, absent reflexes, and fasciculations, including of the tongue, with sensation spared because sensory neurons are never involved. Type 1 SMA, the most common and severe form, is also known as Werdnig-Hoffmann disease. Dorsal root ganglia, oligodendrocytes, Schwann cells, and Purkinje cells are not the cells destroyed in this disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SMA1/Werdnig-Hoffmann disease is a heavily tested First Aid entry, floppy baby plus tongue fasciculations plus absent reflexes. COMLEX: distinguish SMA, a pure LMN disease of infancy, from cerebral palsy, which produces UMN signs instead.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — anterior horn cell destruction in spinal muscular atrophy versus corticospinal disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the anterior horn cell as the lower motor neuron final common pathway for skeletal muscle.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with a remote history of paralytic poliomyelitis as a child now has new, gradually worsening weakness and fatigue in the same leg that was affected decades earlier. She denies any new sensory symptoms. Which structure was originally destroyed by the poliovirus infection in childhood?","choices":{"A":"Posterior column sensory tracts","B":"Anterior horn motor neurons","C":"Dorsal root ganglion cell bodies","D":"Peripheral sensory nerve fibers","E":"Corticospinal tract axons"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Poliovirus is transmitted fecal-orally, replicates in oropharyngeal and intestinal lymphoid tissue, and then spreads hematogenously to selectively destroy anterior horn motor neurons in the spinal cord, producing acute asymmetric LMN weakness alongside viral meningitis symptoms, with sensation preserved because sensory pathways are not involved. The new, decades-later fatigue and weakness in the same limb described here is consistent with post-polio syndrome, thought to reflect decompensation of chronically overworked surviving motor units, not a new anterior horn insult. The corticospinal tracts are spared too, which is why the weakness is flaccid with LMN signs rather than spastic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: poliomyelitis destroys anterior horns and spares sensation, distinguishing it from Guillain-Barre syndrome, which is sensorimotor. COMLEX: post-polio syndrome years after recovery is a recognized late complication worth recognizing on exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — poliovirus tropism for anterior horn cells and late post-polio decompensation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate alpha motor neurons, gamma motor neurons, extrafusal muscle fibers, and intrafusal muscle fibers.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher records intracellular activity from a spinal motor neuron pool while a muscle is passively stretched. She notes that a subset of large motor neurons fire and their axons are traced to ordinary force-generating fibers making up the bulk of the muscle, while a separate, smaller population of motor neurons remains silent. Which neurons remain silent under this passive-stretch condition?","choices":{"A":"Alpha motor neurons to extrafusal fibers","B":"Type Ia sensory neurons from the spindle","C":"Type Ib sensory neurons from the tendon organ","D":"Gamma motor neurons innervating intrafusal fibers","E":"Postganglionic sympathetic neurons to the vessel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"During a purely passive stretch, the change in muscle length is detected by Ia and II sensory afferents from the spindle, which reflexively excite alpha motor neurons supplying the extrafusal fibers of that muscle, the monosynaptic stretch reflex. Gamma motor neurons are not activated by passive stretch itself; they instead receive independent supraspinal drive during voluntary movement to keep the spindle sensitive as the muscle actively shortens, so they remain silent in this purely passive scenario.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gamma motor neuron activity is supraspinally driven and dissociable from alpha motor neuron activity, a distinction tested with figure-based questions. COMLEX: this dissociation explains why a purely passive stretch reflex test does not require gamma drive to work.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — alpha versus gamma motor neuron activation patterns during passive stretch","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate alpha motor neurons, gamma motor neurons, extrafusal muscle fibers, and intrafusal muscle fibers.","batch":2,"stem":"During a voluntary biceps curl, both the extrafusal and intrafusal fibers of the biceps brachii shorten together, and the muscle spindle remains sensitive to stretch throughout the movement rather than going slack. Which mechanism explains why the spindle does not go slack during this active shortening?","choices":{"A":"Simultaneous alpha and gamma motor neuron coactivation","B":"Golgi tendon organ inhibition of the antagonist","C":"Reciprocal inhibition of the triceps motor neurons","D":"Increased type Ib afferent firing from the tendon","E":"Withdrawal of descending vestibulospinal excitation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"If only alpha motor neurons fired during active shortening, the intrafusal fibers would passively shorten along with the muscle and the spindle would go slack, silencing the Ia and II afferents and making the spindle useless for monitoring ongoing length. Supraspinal centers instead coactivate gamma motor neurons in parallel with alpha motor neurons, so the intrafusal fibers contract at their poles at the same time, keeping the equatorial sensory region under tension and the spindle sensitive throughout the movement, alpha-gamma coactivation. Golgi tendon organ (Ib) input inhibits the muscle the tendon organ belongs to, not an antagonist, and vestibulospinal drive serves posture and extensor tone; neither keeps the intrafusal fibers contracting during the curl.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alpha-gamma coactivation is a favorite integrative physiology concept connecting motor output to sensory feedback. COMLEX: intact coactivation is why tendon reflexes remain elicitable during ongoing voluntary contraction, a bedside pearl.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — alpha-gamma coactivation preventing spindle unloading during active shortening","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Golgi tendon organ (Ib) input ","E":"Golgi tendon organ (Ib) input "}},{"id":"sdl38_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate alpha motor neurons, gamma motor neurons, extrafusal muscle fibers, and intrafusal muscle fibers.","batch":2,"stem":"An investigator applies a selective toxin that destroys only the small-diameter motor neurons of a spinal motor pool while sparing the large-diameter motor neurons in the same pool. Which structural change is expected at the neuromuscular level supplied by the destroyed neurons?","choices":{"A":"Loss of contractile drive to extrafusal fibers only","B":"Complete paralysis of the entire muscle at rest","C":"Loss of contractile drive to intrafusal muscle fibers only","D":"Loss of tension sensing at the Golgi tendon organ","E":"Loss of cutaneous sensation over the muscle belly"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gamma motor neurons are smaller in diameter than alpha motor neurons and selectively innervate the contractile polar regions of intrafusal fibers within muscle spindles. Their selective loss would eliminate the ability to keep spindles taut during active contraction, alpha-gamma coactivation would fail, but the extrafusal fibers, innervated by the spared large alpha motor neurons, would continue to contract normally, so gross strength would be preserved even though spindle-based reflex sensitivity would be impaired.","boardPrep":"Step 1: neuron diameter correlates with motor neuron type, alpha are large and fast, gamma are small and slower, testable via conduction velocity vignettes. COMLEX: isolated gamma dysfunction is subtle and easy to miss without formal reflex testing during active movement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — selective gamma motor neuron loss sparing extrafusal contractile function","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Their selective loss would eli"}},{"id":"sdl38_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how muscle spindles detect length/stretch and how Golgi tendon organs detect tension.","batch":2,"stem":"A physiology student loads a strain gauge onto an isolated muscle tendon and records a sharp increase in afferent firing whenever the muscle is made to contract against a fixed load, even though the muscle length does not change. Which receptor is being activated in this isometric contraction?","choices":{"A":"Golgi tendon organ","B":"Spindle primary ending","C":"Spindle secondary ending","D":"Pacinian corpuscle","E":"Nociceptor ending"},"correct":"A","explanation":"An isometric contraction generates tension without any change in muscle length, so the length-sensitive muscle spindle, both its primary Ia and secondary II endings, would show little to no increase in firing since the intrafusal fibers are not being stretched. The Golgi tendon organ, arranged in series with the muscle fibers at the musculotendinous junction, is exquisitely sensitive to the tension generated during contraction regardless of whether the muscle shortens, exactly matching the scenario described. Pacinian corpuscles are rapidly adapting receptors for high-frequency vibration and pressure in deep skin, ligaments and joints, not in-series tension sensors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: isometric contraction activates the Golgi tendon organ, not the spindle, a classic distractor pairing on integrative physiology questions. COMLEX: this tension-versus-length distinction underlies interpretation of manual muscle testing findings.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 3 — Golgi tendon organ activation during isometric contraction without length change","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how muscle spindles detect length/stretch and how Golgi tendon organs detect tension.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with a spinal cord lesion is found to have an exaggerated stretch reflex that, when the examiner continues to apply passive stretch, suddenly gives way into a brief period of reduced resistance before tone returns. Which afferent pathway is primarily responsible for the sudden give-way phase of this finding?","choices":{"A":"Type Ia afferents from the muscle spindle","B":"Type II afferents from the muscle spindle","C":"Type III afferents from muscle nociceptors","D":"Postganglionic sympathetic efferent fibers","E":"Type Ib afferents from the Golgi tendon organ"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The finding described is the clasp-knife phenomenon seen in spasticity, in which continued passive stretch generates enough muscle tension to activate Golgi tendon organ type Ib afferents, triggering autogenic inhibition of the same muscle alpha motor neurons and producing the abrupt give-way in resistance. Type Ia and II afferents from the spindle are responsible for generating the initial exaggerated resistance to stretch itself, not the sudden release phase. Postganglionic sympathetic fibers are autonomic efferents to glands, smooth and cardiac muscle and carry no afferent signal from the stretched muscle; the give-way is carried by Ib afferents.","boardPrep":"Step 1: clasp-knife spasticity is listed alongside Babinski and hyperreflexia as a UMN sign in First Aid, driven by Ib-mediated autogenic inhibition. COMLEX: recognizing clasp-knife on exam helps localize a lesion above the anterior horn.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 3 — Ib-mediated autogenic inhibition producing the clasp-knife phenomenon","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how muscle spindles detect length/stretch and how Golgi tendon organs detect tension.","batch":2,"stem":"An experimental preparation selectively severs the afferent fibers from a muscle's Golgi tendon organs while leaving the muscle spindle afferents fully intact. Passive stretch of the muscle is then tested. Which reflex response is most affected by this selective lesion?","choices":{"A":"Loss of the monosynaptic stretch reflex response","B":"Loss of cutaneous withdrawal reflex responses","C":"Loss of length sensation detected during passive muscle stretch","D":"Loss of the crossed-extension reflex bilaterally at rest","E":"Loss of autogenic inhibition during strong contraction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because the monosynaptic stretch reflex and the sensing of passive muscle length depend on intact Ia and II afferents from the muscle spindle, both remain functional after a Golgi tendon organ afferent lesion. What is lost is the protective inverse stretch reflex, or autogenic inhibition, normally triggered by Ib afferents when tension becomes excessive during strong contraction, since only the tendon organ pathway was severed. Cutaneous and crossed-extension reflexes involve entirely separate cutaneous afferent circuits, unaffected by this lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: isolating which reflex depends on which receptor, spindle versus tendon organ, is a recurring theme across integrative physiology vignettes. COMLEX: loss of protective autogenic inhibition can predispose to tendon or muscle injury during forceful contraction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 3 — selective loss of Golgi tendon organ afferents sparing spindle-based reflexes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace the stretch reflex, inverse stretch reflex, reciprocal inhibition, flexor withdrawal reflex, and crossed-extension reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman undergoes a neurologic exam. When the examiner taps her patellar tendon, her quadriceps contracts and her hamstrings simultaneously relax without any conscious effort on her part. Which mechanism explains the simultaneous hamstring relaxation accompanying quadriceps contraction?","choices":{"A":"Reciprocal inhibition of the antagonist motor neuron by the same Ia afferent","B":"Autogenic inhibition of the antagonist motor neuron by Golgi tendon organ Ib fibers","C":"Crossed-extension reflex activating the contralateral limb antagonist muscle","D":"Direct inhibitory synapse from the quadriceps alpha motor neuron itself","E":"Presynaptic inhibition of cutaneous afferents supplying the hamstring"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The same Ia afferent that excites the quadriceps alpha motor neuron monosynaptically also branches to activate an inhibitory interneuron, the Ia inhibitory interneuron, which synapses on and inhibits the hamstring, antagonist, alpha motor neuron at the same spinal level. This reciprocal inhibition ensures the antagonist does not oppose the reflex movement. Autogenic inhibition instead involves Ib afferents inhibiting the same muscle that generated the tension, and the crossed-extension reflex involves the contralateral limb, not the ipsilateral antagonist.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reciprocal inhibition and autogenic inhibition are commonly confused, know which afferent, Ia versus Ib, and which muscle, antagonist versus same muscle, each targets. COMLEX: intact reciprocal inhibition is implicit whenever a normal patellar reflex is elicited.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 4 — reciprocal inhibition of the antagonist during the patellar stretch reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace the stretch reflex, inverse stretch reflex, reciprocal inhibition, flexor withdrawal reflex, and crossed-extension reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher shows that muscle afferent signals from a stretched forearm muscle ascend through the thalamus to sensorimotor cortex, which then raises or lowers the gain of the spinal reflex through descending pathways. Which type of reflex circuit is being described?","choices":{"A":"A monosynaptic stretch reflex confined to one spinal segment","B":"A long-loop reflex that travels through the thalamus and cortex","C":"A crossed-extension reflex driven by cutaneous nociceptors","D":"A disynaptic reciprocal inhibition circuit at the same segment","E":"An autogenic inhibition circuit driven by Golgi tendon organs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Most spinal reflexes run in the background without volitional control, but long-loop reflexes send muscle sensory information through ascending pathways and a thalamic relay to the cortex, which can increase or decrease reflex gain through descending supraspinal pathways. The monosynaptic stretch reflex, reciprocal inhibition through Ia interneurons, and autogenic inhibition through Ib interneurons are all organized locally in the cord. The crossed-extension reflex is driven by cutaneous input crossing to the opposite side of the cord, not by a cortical loop.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Local reflexes act on alpha motor neurons or spinal interneurons; supraspinal pathways adjust their gain. COMLEX: Loss of descending control after an upper motor neuron lesion leaves reflex gain high, producing hyperreflexia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 1 — corticofugal systems through which cortex influences spinal motor neurons","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace the stretch reflex, inverse stretch reflex, reciprocal inhibition, flexor withdrawal reflex, and crossed-extension reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"An investigator records from an isolated spinal cord preparation. Stimulating a single Ia afferent produces a short-latency excitatory postsynaptic potential in the homonymous alpha motor neuron with only one synapse in between, while stimulating a cutaneous nociceptive afferent produces a longer-latency response in a flexor motor neuron several segments away. What best explains the longer latency and wider distribution of the cutaneous response?","choices":{"A":"Cutaneous afferents conduct more slowly than Ia afferents at the same fiber diameter","B":"Cutaneous input requires a supraspinal relay before reaching the anterior horn cells","C":"Cutaneous afferents synapse only on gamma motor neurons, never on alpha motor neurons","D":"Cutaneous input is blocked entirely by the same Ia inhibitory interneuron pathway","E":"Cutaneous input is relayed through multiple interneurons across spinal segments"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The Ia-to-alpha motor neuron stretch reflex is monosynaptic, one synapse, so it has the shortest possible latency and stays confined to the same spinal segment. Cutaneous and nociceptive afferents instead synapse first on spinal interneurons that distribute the signal to propriospinal neurons connecting multiple rostral and caudal segments and to the contralateral side, adding synaptic delay at each relay and explaining both the longer latency and the wider, multisegmental distribution of the response, entirely at the spinal level without requiring any supraspinal relay.","boardPrep":"Step 1: latency differences on reflex testing map directly onto circuit complexity, monosynaptic versus polysynaptic, a favorite neurophysiology exam theme. COMLEX: multisegmental withdrawal responses are used clinically to assess spinal cord integrity below a suspected injury level.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 4 — polysynaptic, multisegmental basis of cutaneous reflex latency and spread","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the role of Ia, Ib, II, and cutaneous afferents in spinal motor control.","batch":2,"stem":"A 51-year-old man develops a peripheral neuropathy that selectively damages the largest-diameter, most heavily myelinated sensory fibers first, while smaller fibers remain relatively spared early in the disease course. Which two fiber types are expected to be affected earliest?","choices":{"A":"Type II and type III afferents","B":"Type III and type IV afferents","C":"Type Ia and Ib afferents","D":"Type IV afferents and C fibers","E":"Postganglionic autonomic C fibers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Type Ia afferents from the muscle spindle primary ending and type Ib afferents from the Golgi tendon organ are the largest-diameter, most heavily myelinated sensory fibers in the body, conducting fastest, and are therefore the most vulnerable early in length-dependent, large-fiber-predominant neuropathies. Type II fibers are somewhat smaller, and types III and IV, along with unmyelinated C fibers, are the smallest and slowest, typically the last, not first, to be affected in a large-fiber process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: large-fiber neuropathies classically impair proprioception and deep tendon reflexes early, with pain and temperature sensation relatively preserved until later. COMLEX: absent ankle reflexes with preserved pinprick sensation should suggest a large-fiber-predominant process.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 5 — large-fiber-predominant vulnerability of type Ia and Ib afferents","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Type II fibers are somewhat sm"}},{"id":"sdl38_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the role of Ia, Ib, II, and cutaneous afferents in spinal motor control.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher compares conduction velocities recorded from four different afferent fiber populations supplying a single limb muscle and its overlying skin. One population conducts fastest, at 80 to 120 meters per second, a second conducts at 35 to 70 meters per second, a third conducts more slowly and carries fine touch and pressure from the skin, and a fourth, unmyelinated, conducts slowest and carries only pain and temperature. Which fiber population conducts at 35 to 70 meters per second?","choices":{"A":"Type Ia afferents from the muscle spindle primary ending","B":"Type Ib afferents originating from the Golgi tendon organ","C":"A-delta fibers carrying fast, sharp pinprick pain","D":"Type II afferents from the muscle spindle secondary ending","E":"C fibers carrying slow, dull, poorly localized pain"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Type Ia and Ib afferents are the largest and fastest, both conducting at roughly 80 to 120 meters per second, matching the first population described. Type II afferents from the spindle secondary ending are next fastest, at roughly 35 to 70 meters per second in the SDL 38–39 reading (the SDL 29 fiber table lists group II/A-beta at 35 to 75), matching the second population. A-delta fibers carrying fast pain and light touch conduct more slowly still, and unmyelinated C fibers carrying slow, dull pain conduct slowest of all, matching the third and fourth populations respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ranking conduction velocities, Ia/Ib fastest, then II, then A-delta, then C fibers slowest, is tested with numeric-value matching questions. COMLEX: this hierarchy underlies why nerve conduction studies target the largest fibers first when localizing a lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 5 — conduction velocity hierarchy among Ia, Ib, II, and cutaneous afferents","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the role of Ia, Ib, II, and cutaneous afferents in spinal motor control.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with a complete spinal cord injury at T10 is noted on exam to still generate a flexor withdrawal response in her feet when the examiner applies a noxious pinprick stimulus below the level of injury, even though she reports no conscious sensation of the stimulus and cannot voluntarily move her legs. Which statement best explains this preserved reflex?","choices":{"A":"Residual corticospinal fibers below the lesion still permit voluntary-like withdrawal movements","B":"The Ia monosynaptic stretch reflex substitutes for conscious withdrawal below the lesion level","C":"Golgi tendon organ afferents ascend all the way to the thalamus to trigger withdrawal","D":"Vestibulospinal input below the lesion generates the withdrawal response independently","E":"Cutaneous afferents complete the withdrawal reflex within the cord below the lesion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The flexor withdrawal reflex is a polysynaptic spinal reflex; cutaneous nociceptive afferents entering below the level of injury synapse on interneurons and propriospinal neurons entirely within the isolated segments of the cord below the lesion, and alpha motor neurons there can still produce a withdrawal response even though ascending sensory information cannot reach the cortex for conscious perception and descending voluntary input cannot reach the cord below a complete lesion. Conscious sensation, voluntary movement, and reflex generation are therefore dissociable after complete cord transection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reflexes that persist below a complete spinal cord lesion, once spinal shock resolves, are a classic testable point separating reflex arcs from conscious sensation and voluntary control. COMLEX: this dissociation is used clinically to assess whether a spinal reflex arc remains intact after severe cord injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 5 — cutaneous withdrawal reflex persisting entirely below a complete spinal cord lesion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_bloom_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the anterior horn cell as the lower motor neuron final common pathway for skeletal muscle.","batch":3,"stem":"A 54-year-old man develops acute asymmetric flaccid weakness of his left leg one week after a febrile illness with headache and neck stiffness. Exam shows reduced tone, absent patellar and Achilles reflexes on the left, and visible fasciculations, with vibration, position, and pinprick sensation fully intact throughout both legs. Serology confirms a mosquito-borne flavivirus infection. Which structure has this virus most likely targeted?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal root ganglia, viral tropism for sensory neurons","B":"Peripheral myelin, viral tropism for Schwann cells","C":"Neuromuscular junction, viral tropism for postsynaptic receptors","D":"Anterior horn cells, viral tropism for lower motor neurons","E":"Lateral corticospinal tract, viral tropism for descending axons"},"correct":"D","explanation":"West Nile virus, like poliovirus, shows selective tropism for anterior horn motor neurons, producing an acute asymmetric flaccid paralysis with hyporeflexia and fasciculations while sparing all sensory modalities, since sensory neurons are not the target. Dorsal root ganglia, Schwann cells, and the neuromuscular junction would all be expected to produce sensory loss or fatigable weakness rather than this pure asymmetric LMN pattern, and a corticospinal tract infection would produce upper, not lower, motor neuron signs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: West Nile virus is a modern, non-poliovirus cause of acute flaccid paralysis with the same anterior horn cell tropism, worth recognizing alongside classic poliomyelitis. COMLEX: a preceding febrile illness with meningeal signs followed by asymmetric flaccid weakness should prompt West Nile virus testing in the appropriate season and region.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — the anterior horn cell as final common pathway, tested here as a board-style comparative-etiology vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"B":"Dorsal root ganglia, Schwann c","C":"Dorsal root ganglia, Schwann c","E":"Dorsal root ganglia, Schwann c"}},{"id":"sdl38_bloom_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the anterior horn cell as the lower motor neuron final common pathway for skeletal muscle.","batch":3,"stem":"Two patients each present with leg weakness. Patient one has a lesion confined to the lateral corticospinal tract; patient two has a lesion confined to the anterior horn cells at the same spinal level. Needle electromyography is performed on the weak muscles of both patients. In which patient would you expect to see fibrillation potentials and positive sharp waves on EMG, and why?","choices":{"A":"Patient one, since only corticospinal tract loss denervates the muscle fiber itself","B":"Patient two, since only anterior horn cell loss denervates the muscle fiber itself","C":"Both patients equally, since either lesion denervates the muscle fiber itself","D":"Neither patient, since denervation potentials require a peripheral nerve lesion","E":"Patient one, since corticospinal loss increases muscle fiber excitability directly"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fibrillation potentials and positive sharp waves on EMG reflect spontaneous firing of denervated muscle fibers that have lost their direct innervation, a finding that occurs only when the final common pathway itself, the anterior horn cell or its axon, is destroyed, as in patient two. Patient one's corticospinal tract lesion leaves the anterior horn cell and its connection to muscle intact, so the muscle remains innervated and would not show these denervation potentials, instead showing normal insertional activity with reduced voluntary recruitment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EMG denervation potentials, fibrillations and positive sharp waves, specifically confirm LMN rather than UMN pathology, a key electrodiagnostic correlate of the \"final common pathway\" concept. COMLEX: EMG findings are frequently used on boards to distinguish a corticospinal lesion from an anterior horn cell or peripheral nerve lesion presenting with similar weakness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — the anterior horn cell as final common pathway, tested here as a board-style comparative-etiology vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"A":"Patient one's corticospinal tr"}},{"id":"sdl38_bloom_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the anterior horn cell as the lower motor neuron final common pathway for skeletal muscle.","batch":3,"stem":"A researcher injects a retrograde tracer into a single leg muscle and finds it labels one neuron in the anterior horn that also receives direct synaptic contacts from corticospinal, vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, and rubrospinal axons, along with monosynaptic Ia afferents. This convergence pattern best illustrates which organizing principle of motor control?","choices":{"A":"The anterior horn cell as the final common pathway for movement","B":"The anterior horn cell as a purely sensory relay structure","C":"The anterior horn cell as an exclusively reflex-only structure","D":"The anterior horn cell as a site of pure gamma motor neuron convergence","E":"The anterior horn cell as a structure isolated from all descending input"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Every descending motor pathway and every spinal reflex arc must ultimately converge on and act through the same anterior horn alpha motor neuron before a muscle can contract, exactly the convergence pattern traced in this experiment, which is the anatomic basis for calling this neuron the final common pathway. The anterior horn cell is a motor, not sensory, relay, receives far more than reflex-only input, is not a site of gamma-specific convergence for this experiment, which specifically labeled an alpha motor neuron reaching the muscle, and is clearly not isolated from descending input given the convergence demonstrated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: visualizing the anterior horn cell as the literal convergence point for every descending tract plus spinal reflexes is the clearest way to internalize the \"final common pathway\" concept for exam purposes. COMLEX: this convergence explains why a lesion anywhere upstream of the anterior horn cell can still produce weakness, but only anterior horn cell or peripheral lesions produce true LMN signs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — the anterior horn cell as final common pathway, tested here as a board-style comparative-etiology vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)","whyNot":{"C":"The anterior horn cell is a mo"}},{"id":"sdl38_bloom_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate alpha motor neurons, gamma motor neurons, extrafusal muscle fibers, and intrafusal muscle fibers.","batch":3,"stem":"A toxin that blocks acetylcholine release at all cholinergic neuromuscular synapses, without any selectivity for fiber type, is injected into a single muscle. Which combination of effects on that muscle's force generation and reflex sensitivity would be expected?","choices":{"A":"Weakness from extrafusal blockade only, with fully preserved reflex sensitivity","B":"Reduced reflex sensitivity only, with fully preserved voluntary force generation itself","C":"No measurable effect on either muscle force generation or reflex sensitivity","D":"Weakness from extrafusal blockade plus reduced reflex sensitivity from intrafusal blockade","E":"Increased force generation from unopposed intrafusal fiber contraction alone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because both alpha motor neurons, which innervate extrafusal fibers, and gamma motor neurons, which innervate intrafusal fibers, use acetylcholine at a cholinergic neuromuscular synapse, a nonselective toxin blocks both simultaneously, producing overt weakness from extrafusal fiber paralysis together with reduced stretch reflex sensitivity from intrafusal fiber paralysis and consequent spindle unloading. A selectively extrafusal-only or intrafusal-only effect would require fiber-type selectivity that this toxin does not have, and no effect or an increase in force are both inconsistent with blocking acetylcholine release at either fiber type.","boardPrep":"Step 1: predicting the combined effect of a nonselective neuromuscular blocker on both extrafusal and intrafusal fibers integrates the alpha/gamma distinction with real pharmacologic reasoning. COMLEX: this same nonselectivity is why botulinum toxin injections can subtly blunt reflex testing in the treated muscle in addition to their intended weakening effect.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 2 — the alpha/gamma and extrafusal/intrafusal distinctions, tested here as a board-style mechanism-prediction vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"A":"A selectively extrafusal-only ","B":"A selectively extrafusal-only ","C":"A selectively extrafusal-only "}},{"id":"sdl38_bloom_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate alpha motor neurons, gamma motor neurons, extrafusal muscle fibers, and intrafusal muscle fibers.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a severe midbrain lesion develops sustained extension and rigidity of all four limbs. Physiologic studies show markedly increased firing of gamma motor neurons in the affected limbs, driving intrafusal fiber contraction and heightened Ia afferent sensitivity even at rest. Loss of which normal influence best explains this unopposed gamma hyperactivity?","choices":{"A":"Loss of descending excitatory control over alpha motor neurons","B":"Loss of Golgi tendon organ afferent input to the spinal cord","C":"Loss of descending inhibitory control over gamma motor neuron excitability","D":"Loss of cutaneous afferent input driving flexor withdrawal reflexes","E":"Loss of type II afferent input from the muscle spindle secondary ending"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This describes decerebrate rigidity, in which a midbrain lesion disconnects the cortex and other higher centers from unopposed excitatory vestibulospinal and reticulospinal drive to gamma motor neurons, releasing them from normal descending inhibitory control and producing sustained gamma hyperactivity, heightened spindle sensitivity, and extensor rigidity even at rest. Loss of excitatory rather than inhibitory alpha motor neuron control would reduce rather than increase tone, and loss of Golgi tendon organ, cutaneous, or type II afferent input does not explain a primary increase in gamma motor neuron firing itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: decerebrate rigidity is a classic demonstration that the gamma loop is normally kept in check by descending control, and its release produces measurable, exaggerated stretch reflex sensitivity. COMLEX: distinguishing decerebrate, extension of all four limbs, from decorticate, arm flexion with leg extension, posturing localizes the level of a severe brain lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 2 — the alpha/gamma and extrafusal/intrafusal distinctions, tested here as a board-style mechanism-prediction vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"D":"Loss of excitatory rather than","E":"Loss of excitatory rather than"}},{"id":"sdl38_bloom_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate alpha motor neurons, gamma motor neurons, extrafusal muscle fibers, and intrafusal muscle fibers.","batch":3,"stem":"During a slowly graded voluntary contraction, small, slow-fatigable alpha motor neurons within a motor pool are recruited first, followed only later by progressively larger, fast-fatigable alpha motor neurons as more force is demanded. Which organizing principle governs this specific order of recruitment?","choices":{"A":"Size principle, smallest motor neurons recruited first","B":"Gamma loop principle, spindle sensitivity dictates recruitment order","C":"All-or-none principle, every motor neuron fires simultaneously","D":"Reciprocal inhibition principle, antagonist recruitment dictates order","E":"Autogenic inhibition principle, tendon feedback dictates order"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Henneman's size principle states that motor neurons within a pool are recruited in order of increasing size, smallest and most excitable first, which corresponds to small, fatigue-resistant motor units generating fine, sustained force before progressively larger, fast-fatigable units are recruited for greater force demands, exactly the pattern described. The gamma loop governs spindle sensitivity rather than alpha motor neuron recruitment order, the all-or-none principle describes single-fiber firing rather than pool-wide sequencing, and reciprocal and autogenic inhibition govern antagonist and same-muscle feedback rather than the orderly recruitment sequence within one motor pool.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the size principle explains why fine, precise movements rely on small motor units while forceful movements recruit progressively larger ones, a foundational motor control concept. COMLEX: this orderly recruitment pattern underlies the rationale for graded, low-force therapeutic exercise progression in rehabilitation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 2 — the alpha/gamma and extrafusal/intrafusal distinctions, tested here as a board-style mechanism-prediction vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl38_bloom_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how muscle spindles detect length/stretch and how Golgi tendon organs detect tension.","batch":3,"stem":"A physiologist records afferent activity from a muscle during an unexpected sudden perturbation that stretches the muscle rapidly, followed a moment later by the subject voluntarily contracting the same muscle forcefully against resistance. Which receptor shows its greatest relative increase in firing during the initial rapid stretch phase specifically, as opposed to the subsequent voluntary contraction phase?","choices":{"A":"Muscle spindle, dynamic response to rapid length change","B":"Pacinian corpuscle, dynamic response to rapid length change","C":"Golgi tendon organ, dynamic response to rapid length change","D":"Free nerve ending, dynamic response to rapid length change","E":"Merkel disc, dynamic response to rapid length change"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The muscle spindle, particularly its dynamic nuclear bag fibers and type Ia afferents, is exquisitely sensitive to the rate of length change and fires maximally during a rapid stretch itself, before any voluntary contraction begins, whereas the Golgi tendon organ becomes most active only once active tension develops during the subsequent voluntary contraction phase. Pacinian corpuscles and Merkel discs are cutaneous mechanoreceptors, and free nerve endings are largely nociceptive, none of which are the dedicated muscle-length receptor responsible for this specific dynamic stretch response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguishing the muscle spindle's dynamic, rate-sensitive response to sudden stretch from the Golgi tendon organ's tension-driven response during subsequent contraction is a classic paired-timing physiology question. COMLEX: this same dynamic spindle response is the physiologic basis for the exaggerated reflex seen when a tendon hammer strike catches a muscle off guard.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — spindle length-sensing versus Golgi tendon organ tension-sensing, tested here as a board-style receptor-discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl38_bloom_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how muscle spindles detect length/stretch and how Golgi tendon organs detect tension.","batch":3,"stem":"A rare congenital condition results in complete absence of muscle spindles throughout the body, while Golgi tendon organs, alpha motor neurons, and all descending pathways remain entirely normal. Which reflex would you expect to be preserved in an affected patient, and which would be absent?","choices":{"A":"Monosynaptic stretch reflex preserved; inverse stretch reflex absent","B":"Both the stretch reflex and inverse stretch reflex preserved equally","C":"Both the stretch reflex and inverse stretch reflex absent equally","D":"Flexor withdrawal reflex absent; both stretch reflexes preserved equally","E":"Inverse stretch reflex preserved; monosynaptic stretch reflex absent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The monosynaptic stretch reflex depends entirely on Ia afferents from the muscle spindle, so its complete absence would abolish this reflex, producing absent deep tendon reflexes throughout, while the inverse stretch reflex depends on Ib afferents from the Golgi tendon organ, an entirely separate receptor that remains intact in this condition, so autogenic inhibition during strong contraction would still function normally. The flexor withdrawal reflex depends on cutaneous, not muscle spindle, afferents and would also be expected to remain intact, unaffected by this specific lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reasoning through which specific reflex depends on which specific receptor, rather than assuming all reflexes fail together, is exactly the discriminating skill this objective builds. COMLEX: a patient with globally absent deep tendon reflexes but normal strength and no other findings should prompt consideration of a primary sensory or spindle-afferent process rather than a motor one.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — spindle length-sensing versus Golgi tendon organ tension-sensing, tested here as a board-style receptor-discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl38_bloom_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how muscle spindles detect length/stretch and how Golgi tendon organs detect tension.","batch":3,"stem":"A weightlifter performing a heavy eccentric contraction, in which the muscle is forcibly lengthened while still actively generating tension to control a descending load, suddenly loses tension and the muscle briefly goes slack, protecting it from injury just as tension peaks. Which receptor's reflex response is most directly responsible for this protective give-way?","choices":{"A":"Muscle spindle, autogenic inhibition at peak tension","B":"Golgi tendon organ, autogenic inhibition at peak tension","C":"Pacinian corpuscle, autogenic inhibition at peak tension","D":"Cutaneous nociceptor, autogenic inhibition at peak tension","E":"Free nerve ending, autogenic inhibition at peak tension"},"correct":"B","explanation":"During a heavy eccentric contraction, both the spindle, from lengthening, and the Golgi tendon organ, from rising tension, are simultaneously activated, but it is specifically the Golgi tendon organ's Ib afferent input, once tension crosses a protective threshold, that drives autogenic inhibition of the alpha motor neuron, producing the sudden protective give-way described, rather than the spindle, which instead reflexively excites, not inhibits, the same muscle. Pacinian corpuscles, cutaneous nociceptors, and free nerve endings are not the receptors responsible for this specific tension-triggered protective reflex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing the Golgi tendon organ's protective role during eccentric loading, distinct from the spindle's simultaneous but opposite reflex action, integrates both proprioceptors into one realistic athletic scenario. COMLEX: this same protective mechanism is relevant to understanding injury risk and training adaptations in eccentric-heavy resistance exercise.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — spindle length-sensing versus Golgi tendon organ tension-sensing, tested here as a board-style receptor-discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl38_bloom_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace the stretch reflex, inverse stretch reflex, reciprocal inhibition, flexor withdrawal reflex, and crossed-extension reflex.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with chronic spasticity is noted on exam to have simultaneous, inappropriate co-contraction of both the biceps and triceps when attempting to flex the elbow, rather than the smooth triceps relaxation normally accompanying biceps activation. Failure of which specific spinal circuit best explains this abnormal co-contraction?","choices":{"A":"Reciprocal inhibition of the antagonist motor neuron pool","B":"Autogenic inhibition of the same muscle's motor neurons","C":"Crossed-extension reflex of the contralateral limb","D":"Flexor withdrawal reflex of the ipsilateral limb","E":"Recurrent inhibition of the agonist motor neuron pool"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Normal voluntary agonist activation depends on intact reciprocal inhibition, in which Ia afferent collaterals from the contracting agonist inhibit the antagonist motor neuron pool through an inhibitory interneuron, and disruption of this circuit in spasticity, from altered descending modulation of spinal interneurons, produces inappropriate antagonist co-contraction instead of smooth relaxation. Autogenic inhibition targets the same, not antagonist, muscle, the crossed-extension and flexor withdrawal reflexes involve cutaneous input and different limbs or circuits entirely, and recurrent inhibition targets the agonist pool itself rather than explaining antagonist co-contraction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: impaired reciprocal inhibition producing antagonist co-contraction is a recognized contributor to the abnormal movement patterns seen in spasticity, beyond simple hyperreflexia alone. COMLEX: this same co-contraction pattern is a target of physical therapy techniques aimed at retraining smoother reciprocal muscle activation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 4 — the full spinal reflex circuitry, tested here as a board-style circuit-integration vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl38_bloom_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace the stretch reflex, inverse stretch reflex, reciprocal inhibition, flexor withdrawal reflex, and crossed-extension reflex.","batch":3,"stem":"A physiology student learns that an activated alpha motor neuron sends a collateral branch back onto a specialized interneuron, which in turn inhibits that same alpha motor neuron and its neighboring synergist motor neurons, limiting excessive or prolonged firing within the pool. This negative feedback circuit is distinct from both reciprocal and autogenic inhibition because its trigger arises from which source?","choices":{"A":"The alpha motor neuron's own axon collateral, not a peripheral sensory afferent","B":"A type Ib afferent from the same muscle's Golgi tendon organ","C":"A cutaneous afferent from the overlying skin surface entirely, not the muscle","D":"A type II afferent from the same muscle spindle's secondary sensory ending region","E":"A type Ia afferent from the antagonist muscle spindle itself, not this one"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This circuit describes recurrent inhibition via the Renshaw cell, an interneuron activated by a recurrent collateral of the alpha motor neuron's own axon rather than by any peripheral sensory afferent, providing negative feedback that limits excessive or prolonged firing of that motor neuron and its synergists, a mechanism distinct from Ia-mediated reciprocal inhibition of the antagonist and Ib-mediated autogenic inhibition triggered by tendon tension. Type Ia, Ib, II, and cutaneous afferents are all peripheral sensory inputs, not the motor axon collateral that specifically defines this recurrent inhibitory circuit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Renshaw cell recurrent inhibition, triggered by the motor neuron's own collateral rather than any sensory afferent, is a distinct third inhibitory circuit worth separating clearly from reciprocal and autogenic inhibition. COMLEX: this same circuit is the target of certain toxins, such as tetanus toxin, which blocks Renshaw cell inhibitory output and produces sustained muscle rigidity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 4 — the full spinal reflex circuitry, tested here as a board-style circuit-integration vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"B":"Type Ia, Ib, II, and cutaneous","C":"Type Ia, Ib, II, and cutaneous","D":"Type Ia, Ib, II, and cutaneous","E":"Type Ia, Ib, II, and cutaneous"}},{"id":"sdl38_bloom_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace the stretch reflex, inverse stretch reflex, reciprocal inhibition, flexor withdrawal reflex, and crossed-extension reflex.","batch":3,"stem":"A researcher applies the same mild noxious stimulus to a subject's foot repeatedly over several minutes and observes that the flexor withdrawal reflex response progressively diminishes in magnitude with each repetition, despite the peripheral nociceptors themselves showing no change in their own firing threshold. Which level of the reflex circuit best explains this progressive decline in response?","choices":{"A":"Peripheral nociceptor terminals, showing use-dependent synaptic depression","B":"Alpha motor neuron cell bodies, showing use-dependent synaptic depression","C":"Muscle spindle afferents, showing use-dependent synaptic depression","D":"Spinal interneuron synapses, showing use-dependent synaptic depression","E":"Golgi tendon organ afferents, showing use-dependent synaptic depression"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because the peripheral nociceptor threshold is explicitly unchanged in this scenario, the progressive decline in withdrawal reflex magnitude with repeated stimulation, a form of habituation, must arise centrally, at the interneuronal synapses within the polysynaptic withdrawal reflex circuit, which show well-described use-dependent synaptic depression with repetitive low-level activation. Alpha motor neuron cell bodies are the final common output rather than the site of this plastic change, and muscle spindle and Golgi tendon organ afferents are not part of the cutaneous withdrawal reflex circuit at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: habituation of a polysynaptic reflex through interneuronal synaptic depression is a classic example of simple, non-associative spinal cord plasticity worth distinguishing from peripheral receptor adaptation. COMLEX: recognizing that reflex habituation occurs centrally, not peripherally, is relevant to interpreting changes in reflex testing over a repeated examination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 4 — the full spinal reflex circuitry, tested here as a board-style circuit-integration vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl38_bloom_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the role of Ia, Ib, II, and cutaneous afferents in spinal motor control.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with untreated tertiary syphilis develops a wide-based, stomping gait that worsens markedly in the dark, along with globally absent deep tendon reflexes throughout both legs, though manual muscle strength testing remains entirely normal. Selective degeneration of which fiber type within the dorsal roots best explains both the absent reflexes and the gait disturbance together?","choices":{"A":"Type Ib afferents, tendon organ dorsal root fibers","B":"Type III afferents, nociceptive dorsal root fibers","C":"Type IV afferents, unmyelinated dorsal root fibers only","D":"Alpha motor neuron axons, ventral root fibers only","E":"Type Ia afferents, proprioceptive dorsal root fibers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tabes dorsalis selectively degenerates the large-diameter dorsal root fibers, principally type Ia afferents carrying proprioceptive input from the muscle spindle, producing both absent deep tendon reflexes, since Ia input drives the monosynaptic stretch reflex, and a sensory ataxic, stomping, dark-worsening gait from loss of proprioceptive feedback, while strength is preserved because the ventral root motor axons and alpha motor neurons themselves are untouched. Type Ib, III, and IV afferents are not the predominantly affected fiber type in tabes dorsalis, and ventral root motor axon involvement would instead produce weakness, which is absent here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tabes dorsalis, absent reflexes plus sensory ataxia with preserved strength from selective large-fiber dorsal root degeneration, is a classic tertiary syphilis presentation worth recognizing precisely. COMLEX: a positive Romberg sign in this patient would further confirm the proprioceptive, rather than cerebellar, basis for the gait disturbance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 5 — the Ia/Ib/II/cutaneous afferent framework, tested here as a board-style fiber-localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl38_bloom_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the role of Ia, Ib, II, and cutaneous afferents in spinal motor control.","batch":3,"stem":"A 14-year-old girl presents with progressive gait ataxia, absent deep tendon reflexes throughout, and a hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Genetic testing reveals a GAA trinucleotide repeat expansion causing degeneration of large sensory neurons within the dorsal root ganglia, sparing the cerebellum itself on imaging. Loss of which specific afferent fiber population best explains her absent reflexes despite normal muscle strength?","choices":{"A":"Type Ib afferents from dorsal root ganglion neurons","B":"Cerebellar Purkinje cell axons projecting to deep nuclei","C":"Type Ia afferents originating from dorsal root ganglion neurons","D":"Corticospinal tract axons originating from motor cortex","E":"Vestibular afferents originating from the semicircular canals"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This describes Friedreich ataxia, in which degeneration of large dorsal root ganglion neurons, principally those giving rise to type Ia afferents, eliminates the sensory limb of the monosynaptic stretch reflex, producing globally absent deep tendon reflexes despite preserved alpha motor neuron function and normal strength, while the ataxia itself reflects combined proprioceptive loss and degeneration of spinocerebellar tracts rather than the cerebellum proper, which appears normal on imaging in this vignette. Type Ib afferent loss would not primarily explain absent stretch reflexes, the cerebellum itself is explicitly spared here, and corticospinal or vestibular pathway involvement would not produce this specific reflex and cardiac combination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Friedreich ataxia's combination of absent reflexes from dorsal root ganglion Ia afferent loss, ataxia, and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is a classic trinucleotide repeat disease worth knowing as a unit. COMLEX: absent reflexes with preserved strength in a young patient with ataxia should prompt consideration of this diagnosis alongside the cardiac evaluation it requires.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 5 — the Ia/Ib/II/cutaneous afferent framework, tested here as a board-style fiber-localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"B":"Type Ib afferent loss would no","D":"Type Ib afferent loss would no","E":"Type Ib afferent loss would no"}},{"id":"sdl38_bloom_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the role of Ia, Ib, II, and cutaneous afferents in spinal motor control.","batch":3,"stem":"Two patients each have globally absent deep tendon reflexes in the legs. Patient one has a pure sensory neuronopathy destroying dorsal root ganglion cell bodies, with normal strength on exam. Patient two has anterior horn cell disease, with significant weakness, atrophy, and fasciculations on exam. Nerve conduction studies and EMG are performed on both. Which finding would most reliably distinguish patient one from patient two?","choices":{"A":"Abnormal sensory nerve action potentials only in patient two","B":"Abnormal sensory nerve action potentials only in patient one","C":"Fibrillation potentials on EMG only in patient one","D":"Normal compound muscle action potentials only in patient two","E":"Identical nerve conduction and EMG findings in both patients"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because patient one's lesion destroys the sensory neuron cell bodies giving rise to Ia and other sensory afferents, sensory nerve action potentials would be reduced or absent, while patient two's pure motor anterior horn cell disease leaves sensory neurons entirely intact, so sensory nerve action potentials remain normal in patient two but abnormal in patient one, the single most reliable distinguishing electrodiagnostic finding between an absent-reflex picture caused by sensory loss versus one caused by motor neuron loss. Fibrillation potentials would instead be expected in patient two, whose muscle is denervated, not patient one, and the two patients would not be expected to show identical or reversed findings given their fundamentally different underlying pathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: comparing sensory nerve action potentials is the key electrodiagnostic step for distinguishing a sensory neuronopathy from a pure motor neuron disease when both present with absent reflexes. COMLEX: this same reasoning, correlating exam findings with targeted electrodiagnostic testing, is the general approach to localizing any reflex abnormality on the exam.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 5 — the Ia/Ib/II/cutaneous afferent framework, tested here as a board-style fiber-localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"}],"39":[{"id":"sdl39_q01","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how spinal interneurons coordinate agonist-antagonist relationships and bilateral limb responses.","batch":1,"stem":"Which spinal circuit element allows a single Ia afferent to simultaneously excite the agonist alpha motor neuron and inhibit the antagonist alpha motor neuron?","choices":{"A":"Ib inhibitory interneuron","B":"Ia inhibitory interneuron","C":"Renshaw cell interneuron","D":"Propriospinal neuron","E":"Golgi tendon interneuron"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Ia afferent from a muscle spindle branches within the spinal cord: one branch synapses monosynaptically on the agonist alpha motor neuron, exciting it directly, while a second branch excites a dedicated Ia inhibitory interneuron that in turn inhibits the antagonist alpha motor neuron. This single afferent, dual-target arrangement is the cellular basis of reciprocal inhibition, allowing agonist contraction and antagonist relaxation to occur together automatically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ia inhibitory interneurons are distinct from Renshaw cells, which mediate recurrent inhibition of the same motor neuron pool via a collateral, not reciprocal inhibition of the antagonist. COMLEX: this circuit underlies smooth, coordinated single-joint movement tested in gait and reflex exams.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 4 — the reciprocal inhibition and reflex arc circuitry, now generalized to the full role of spinal interneurons in coordinating agonist-antagonist and bilateral limb relationships","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl39_q02","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how spinal interneurons coordinate agonist-antagonist relationships and bilateral limb responses.","batch":1,"stem":"Type Ib afferents from a Golgi tendon organ reach the anterior horn during a strong contraction of the triceps. How do these afferents reduce activity of the triceps alpha motor neurons?","choices":{"A":"They inhibit the triceps alpha motor neurons directly at a single synapse","B":"They excite interneurons that in turn inhibit the triceps alpha motor neurons","C":"They excite Renshaw cells through collaterals of the triceps motor axons","D":"They inhibit gamma motor neurons so that triceps spindles fall silent","E":"They excite Ia interneurons that are driven by the biceps spindles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ib fibers traverse the intermediate zone and form excitatory synapses with interneurons in the anterior horn, and these interneurons inhibit the alpha motor neurons of the muscle whose tendon organ was activated, so the circuit is disynaptic. Primary afferents are excitatory, so a direct one-synapse inhibition does not occur. Renshaw cells are driven by motor axon collaterals rather than by Ib afferents, Ia interneurons are driven by spindle afferents of the antagonist to produce reciprocal inhibition, and silencing gamma motor neurons is not how tendon organs act.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tendon organs are in series with muscle and sense tension; Ib afferent, excitatory synapse on an inhibitory interneuron, inhibition of the homonymous alpha motor neuron. COMLEX: This autogenic inhibition is the opposite of the spindle stretch reflex and underlies the give-way of the clasp-knife response.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — Golgi tendon organs as tension detectors with type Ib afferents","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Primary afferents are excitato"}},{"id":"sdl39_q03","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how spinal interneurons coordinate agonist-antagonist relationships and bilateral limb responses.","batch":1,"stem":"Which spinal neuron type links motor circuits across multiple spinal segments to coordinate bilateral limb responses, such as those needed for coordinated stepping?","choices":{"A":"Propriospinal neuron","B":"Ia inhibitory interneuron","C":"Renshaw cell interneuron","D":"Golgi tendon interneuron","E":"Postganglionic sympathetic neuron"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Propriospinal neurons have axons that travel within the spinal cord itself, connecting motor circuits at different rostrocaudal segments and across the midline, allowing coordination between upper and lower limb circuits and between the two sides of the body, functions essential for rhythmic, coordinated movements like walking. Ia inhibitory interneurons and Renshaw cells act locally within a single segment, and Golgi tendon interneurons and postganglionic sympathetic neurons are not part of this intersegmental coordinating system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: propriospinal connections are the anatomic substrate for spinal pattern generators involved in locomotion. COMLEX: intersegmental reflex spread, such as a withdrawal response involving multiple joints, reflects propriospinal circuitry.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 4 — the reciprocal inhibition and reflex arc circuitry, now generalized to the full role of spinal interneurons in coordinating agonist-antagonist and bilateral limb relationships","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl39_q04","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the major brainstem descending pathways that influence anterior horn neurons: vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, tectospinal, and rubrospinal systems.","batch":1,"stem":"Which descending tract originates from the lateral vestibular nucleus and excites motor neurons supplying antigravity extensor muscles?","choices":{"A":"Medial vestibulospinal tract","B":"Lateral reticulospinal tract","C":"Medial reticulospinal tract","D":"Lateral vestibulospinal tract","E":"Rubrospinal tract from the red nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The lateral vestibulospinal tract arises from the lateral vestibular nucleus, descends ipsilaterally through the length of the spinal cord in the anterior funiculus, and excites motor neurons supplying paravertebral and proximal limb extensors, the antigravity muscles that maintain posture and balance. The medial vestibulospinal tract instead projects only to cervical levels to control neck musculature, and the reticulospinal and rubrospinal tracts arise from different brainstem nuclei entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lateral vestibulospinal is the classic antigravity extensor pathway, tested alongside decerebrate rigidity vignettes. COMLEX: postural control assessment implicitly tests vestibulospinal integrity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 4 — the vestibular pathway traced from hair cells through the vestibular nuclei, now followed further down its descending projection to the spinal cord as the vestibulospinal tract","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The medial vestibulospinal tra"}},{"id":"sdl39_q05","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the major brainstem descending pathways that influence anterior horn neurons: vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, tectospinal, and rubrospinal systems.","batch":1,"stem":"Which brainstem descending pathway arises from the red nucleus and provides excitatory drive mainly to motor neurons supplying proximal limb flexors, primarily in the upper extremity?","choices":{"A":"Lateral vestibulospinal tract","B":"Medial reticulospinal tract","C":"Tectospinal tract","D":"Rubrospinal tract","E":"Lateral corticospinal tract"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rubrospinal tract originates from the magnocellular red nucleus, crosses in the ventral tegmental decussation, and descends contralaterally to excite motor neurons innervating proximal limb flexors, an influence that in humans is largely restricted to the upper extremity because the tract is vestigial and rarely extends caudal to the cervical enlargement. The vestibulospinal and reticulospinal tracts instead influence extensor tone, and the tectospinal tract mediates reflexive head and neck turning rather than limb flexion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rubrospinal influences upper extremity flexors only in humans, a commonly tested species-specific detail. COMLEX: decorticate posturing shows upper extremity flexion because the rubrospinal tract remains intact while cortical input is lost.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 4 — the vestibular pathway traced from hair cells through the vestibular nuclei, now followed further down its descending projection to the spinal cord as the vestibulospinal tract","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q06","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the major brainstem descending pathways that influence anterior horn neurons: vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, tectospinal, and rubrospinal systems.","batch":1,"stem":"Which descending pathway arises from the superior colliculus, crosses in the dorsal tegmental decussation, and mediates reflexive turning of the head and neck toward a sudden visual stimulus?","choices":{"A":"Rubrospinal tract","B":"Lateral vestibulospinal tract","C":"Tectospinal tract","D":"Medial reticulospinal tract","E":"Lateral reticulospinal tract"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The tectospinal tract originates in the superior colliculus, a midbrain structure that integrates visual and auditory input for orienting responses, crosses the midline in the dorsal tegmental decussation, and descends near the medial longitudinal fasciculus to influence cervical motor neurons that turn the head and neck toward a sudden stimulus. This is distinct from the rubrospinal tract, which influences limb flexors, and from the vestibulospinal and reticulospinal tracts, which mainly regulate posture and extensor tone rather than head-turning reflexes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the tectospinal tract connects the superior colliculus to reflexive head-orienting movements, a classically tested but often overlooked descending pathway. COMLEX: reflexive startle and orienting responses depend on tectospinal integrity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 4 — the vestibular pathway traced from hair cells through the vestibular nuclei, now followed further down its descending projection to the spinal cord as the vestibulospinal tract","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q07","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Differentiate lower motor neuron signs from upper motor neuron signs and connect them to lesion localization.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has weakness, spasticity, hyperreflexia, and a positive Babinski sign in the right leg with no muscle atrophy or fasciculations. Where is the lesion most likely located relative to the anterior horn cell?","choices":{"A":"Within the anterior horn cell body itself, not above it","B":"Distal to the anterior horn cell, in a peripheral nerve trunk","C":"Above the anterior horn cell, in an upper motor neuron pathway","D":"At the neuromuscular junction, between nerve and muscle","E":"Within the muscle fibers themselves, not the nerve"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Spasticity, hyperreflexia, and a positive Babinski sign, without atrophy or fasciculations, reflect loss of descending inhibitory control over an intact anterior horn cell and its axon, the hallmark of an upper motor neuron lesion located anywhere above the anterior horn, in the corticospinal tract, brainstem, or cortex. A lesion at or distal to the anterior horn cell, including a peripheral nerve, would instead produce flaccid weakness, hyporeflexia, atrophy, and fasciculations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UMN = 'everything up,' tone, reflexes, toes; the mirror image of the LMN pattern. COMLEX: a Babinski sign specifically implies UMN, not LMN, pathology and should never be attributed to a peripheral nerve lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — the anterior horn cell as the lower motor neuron final common pathway, now contrasted against the upper motor neuron pattern produced by lesions above it","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A lesion at or distal to the a"}},{"id":"sdl39_q08","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Differentiate lower motor neuron signs from upper motor neuron signs and connect them to lesion localization.","batch":1,"stem":"Which single finding, if present, would most strongly argue against a pure upper motor neuron lesion and instead suggest lower motor neuron involvement?","choices":{"A":"Visible fasciculations in the affected muscle","B":"A positive Babinski sign on the affected side","C":"A spastic increase in muscle tone throughout","D":"Brisk deep tendon reflexes on the affected side","E":"Clasp-knife resistance felt during passive stretch"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fasciculations reflect spontaneous firing of denervated or irritable individual motor units and require direct involvement of the lower motor neuron, its cell body or axon, something a pure upper motor neuron lesion cannot produce because the lower motor neuron remains intact and simply undergoes disuse-related change instead of denervation. A positive Babinski sign, spasticity, hyperreflexia, and clasp-knife rigidity are all classic upper motor neuron findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fasciculations are the single most specific bedside sign for LMN, not UMN, disease. COMLEX: EMG showing fibrillations and fasciculation potentials confirms denervation when fasciculations are subtle on exam.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — the anterior horn cell as the lower motor neuron final common pathway, now contrasted against the upper motor neuron pattern produced by lesions above it","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"A positive Babinski sign, spas","D":"A positive Babinski sign, spas"}},{"id":"sdl39_q09","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Differentiate lower motor neuron signs from upper motor neuron signs and connect them to lesion localization.","batch":1,"stem":"A lesion produces weakness with normal muscle bulk, brisk reflexes, and increased tone, but no sensory findings. Which additional finding would most specifically localize this to the lateral corticospinal tract rather than the anterior horn cell?","choices":{"A":"Fasciculations visible in the weak muscle group","B":"A positive Babinski sign on the affected side","C":"Atrophy of the weak muscle group over time","D":"Absent deep tendon reflexes on the affected side","E":"Flaccid tone felt during passive movement testing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A positive Babinski sign, extension of the great toe with fanning of the other toes upon plantar stimulation, is a specific marker of corticospinal tract dysfunction and is never a normal finding in an adult, distinguishing this upper motor neuron pattern from anterior horn cell, lower motor neuron, disease. Fasciculations, atrophy, absent reflexes, and flaccid tone are all LMN findings that would argue instead for anterior horn cell or peripheral involvement, which is not what is described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Babinski sign is a corticospinal tract-specific finding, normal only in infants (First Aid lists it among the primitive reflexes that disappear during the first 12 months). COMLEX: always check for Babinski when localizing an unexplained weakness pattern on the boards.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — the anterior horn cell as the lower motor neuron final common pathway, now contrasted against the upper motor neuron pattern produced by lesions above it","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fasciculations, atrophy, absen"}},{"id":"sdl39_q10","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Apply motor pathway anatomy to common clinical patterns: poliomyelitis, spinal muscular atrophy, ALS, spinal cord injury, vestibular dysfunction, decerebrate rigidity, and spasticity.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has both flaccid limb weakness with fasciculations and tongue atrophy, plus spastic weakness with hyperreflexia and pseudobulbar affect, but no sensory loss and no bowel or bladder dysfunction. Which diagnosis best fits this combined pattern?","choices":{"A":"Spinal muscular atrophy type 1","B":"Poliomyelitis, anterior horn cell disease","C":"Subacute combined degeneration","D":"Tabes dorsalis from neurosyphilis","E":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis uniquely combines both lower motor neuron degeneration, producing flaccid weakness, fasciculations, and bulbar atrophy, and upper motor neuron degeneration, producing spasticity, hyperreflexia, and pseudobulbar palsy with emotional lability, all without any sensory or bowel/bladder involvement. Spinal muscular atrophy and poliomyelitis produce pure LMN patterns only, while subacute combined degeneration and tabes dorsalis both prominently involve sensory pathways, which are spared here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ALS is 'combined UMN and LMN degeneration, no sensory loss,' a signature First Aid phrase; also called Lou Gehrig disease, treated with riluzole. COMLEX: pseudobulbar affect, emotional lability, is a distinguishing feature of the UMN component of ALS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 8 — the major spinal cord syndrome framework used for board-style vignettes, now applied specifically to anterior horn and descending motor pathway clinical patterns","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q11","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Apply motor pathway anatomy to common clinical patterns: poliomyelitis, spinal muscular atrophy, ALS, spinal cord injury, vestibular dysfunction, decerebrate rigidity, and spasticity.","batch":1,"stem":"A comatose patient following a large supratentorial hemorrhage develops bilateral upper and lower extremity extension, with the toes pointed inward and the neck extended. Which condition is being described?","choices":{"A":"Decorticate rigidity","B":"Flaccid paralysis","C":"Spinal shock","D":"Decerebrate rigidity","E":"Clasp-knife spasticity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Decerebrate rigidity, extension of both the upper and lower extremities with the neck extended, results from a lesion at or below the midbrain that interrupts cortical, corticorubral, and corticoreticular input while leaving the vestibulospinal system and the excitatory pontine reticulospinal system intact and unopposed, producing unopposed extensor hypertonus in all four limbs. Decorticate rigidity instead shows upper extremity flexion, from a more rostral lesion that spares the rubrospinal tract's flexor influence on the arms. Flaccid paralysis and spinal shock are states of lost tone and reflexes, and clasp-knife spasticity is a give-way to passive stretch, not a resting posture; none is extension of all four limbs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: decerebrate posturing extends all four limbs, a more caudal and more ominous lesion than decorticate posturing, which flexes the arms. COMLEX: progression from decorticate to decerebrate posturing signals rostral-to-caudal deterioration requiring urgent intervention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 8 — the major spinal cord syndrome framework used for board-style vignettes, now applied specifically to anterior horn and descending motor pathway clinical patterns","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q12","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Apply motor pathway anatomy to common clinical patterns: poliomyelitis, spinal muscular atrophy, ALS, spinal cord injury, vestibular dysfunction, decerebrate rigidity, and spasticity.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a known vestibular nerve schwannoma develops a wide-based, unsteady gait with a tendency to fall toward the side of the lesion, without any limb weakness. Which system explains this specific postural finding?","choices":{"A":"Vestibulospinal influence on antigravity extensor tone","B":"Corticospinal influence on distal limb strength alone","C":"Rubrospinal influence on proximal limb flexor tone only","D":"Tectospinal influence on reflexive head orientation","E":"Reticulospinal influence on ascending pain modulation pathways"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The vestibular apparatus and its descending lateral vestibulospinal projection normally provide asymmetric excitatory drive to antigravity extensor motor neurons to maintain upright posture and balance in response to head position and movement; a unilateral vestibular lesion disrupts this balance, producing a tendency to sway or fall toward the side of the lesion even though limb strength itself, which depends on the corticospinal tract, remains entirely normal. Reticulospinal axons do take part in pain modulation, but that is a descending function and would not make a patient fall toward one side.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vestibular dysfunction produces postural and balance deficits through the vestibulospinal system without weakness, distinguishing it from a corticospinal or LMN process. COMLEX: Romberg and tandem gait testing help localize vestibular versus proprioceptive causes of imbalance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 8 — the major spinal cord syndrome framework used for board-style vignettes, now applied specifically to anterior horn and descending motor pathway clinical patterns","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q13","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Use reflex abnormalities, tone, fasciculations, atrophy, and sensory findings to localize board-style motor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"A comatose patient with an expanding hemispheric hemorrhage initially shows flexion of the arms at the elbows with extension of the legs. Over the next hour, all four limbs become rigidly extended and the neck arches back. What does this change in posture most reliably indicate?","choices":{"A":"Recovery of cortical input to the red nucleus","B":"A new lesion confined to the lumbar anterior horn","C":"Spread of the lesion into the cerebellar anterior lobe","D":"Progression of damage caudally into the brainstem","E":"Resolution of mass effect on the opposite hemisphere"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Decorticate posturing, with arm flexion from the intact rubrospinal system and leg extension, reflects loss of cortical influence above the red nucleus; conversion to decerebrate posturing with extension of all limbs means the lesion has progressed caudally through the tentorial notch into the brainstem, an ominous sign that the medullary centers may soon fail. Recovery of cortical input or resolving mass effect would lessen abnormal posturing rather than worsen it. A lumbar anterior horn lesion would cause flaccid leg weakness, and anterior lobe cerebellar damage is not the expected route of a hemispheric mass.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Decorticate: lesion above the red nucleus, arms flexed, legs extended; decerebrate: lesion between the colliculi or below the red nucleus, all limbs extended. COMLEX: A shift from decorticate to decerebrate posturing during transtentorial herniation demands immediate intervention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 7 — rubrospinal, reticulospinal, and vestibulospinal tracts that shape each posture","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q14","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Use reflex abnormalities, tone, fasciculations, atrophy, and sensory findings to localize board-style motor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has bilateral lower extremity weakness with hyperreflexia below the lesion, flaccid weakness with hyporeflexia at the lesion level itself, and loss of pain and temperature sensation below the lesion, but preserved vibration and position sense throughout. Which vascular lesion best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Posterior spinal artery occlusion","B":"Complete transverse myelitis","C":"Central cord syndrome from syringomyelia","D":"Subacute combined degeneration","E":"Anterior spinal artery occlusion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Anterior spinal artery occlusion spares the posterior third of the cord, the dorsal columns supplied by the posterior spinal arteries, so vibration and position sense are preserved, while it infarcts the anterior two-thirds, producing UMN signs below the lesion from corticospinal tract loss, LMN signs at the lesion level from anterior horn infarction, and bilateral loss of pain and temperature below the lesion from spinothalamic tract loss. Posterior spinal artery occlusion would instead spare motor function and impair only dorsal column sensation. A complete transverse myelitis would also take out the dorsal columns, and subacute combined degeneration damages the dorsal columns (impaired position and vibration sense) along with the lateral corticospinal and spinocerebellar tracts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: anterior spinal artery syndrome spares dorsal columns, a frequently tested vascular pattern distinct from posterior spinal artery syndrome. COMLEX: aortic aneurysm repair is a classic precipitant of anterior spinal artery, watershed, infarction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 7 — dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes used for bedside lesion localization, now expanded with tone, fasciculation, and atrophy findings for full motor lesion localization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Posterior spinal artery occlus"}},{"id":"sdl39_q15","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Use reflex abnormalities, tone, fasciculations, atrophy, and sensory findings to localize board-style motor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient presents with ataxic gait, loss of vibration and position sense, absent deep tendon reflexes, and bilateral extensor plantar responses. Which combination of tract involvement best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Isolated anterior horn cell involvement without other tracts","B":"Isolated dorsal root ganglion involvement without other tracts","C":"Combined dorsal column, spinocerebellar, corticospinal involvement","D":"Isolated lateral corticospinal tract involvement without other tracts","E":"Isolated posterior root involvement without other tracts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This pattern, sensory ataxia and vibration/position loss from dorsal column and spinocerebellar tract involvement combined with a Babinski sign from corticospinal tract involvement, but with absent rather than brisk reflexes because peripheral or root-level afferent input is also disrupted, is characteristic of combined system disease processes such as subacute combined degeneration from B12 deficiency or Friedreich ataxia, both of which affect the dorsal columns, corticospinal tracts, and spinocerebellar tracts together rather than a single isolated tract or cell population.","boardPrep":"Step 1: combining a Babinski sign with absent ankle reflexes and no atrophy should prompt consideration of a combined dorsal column plus corticospinal process, not a single-tract lesion. COMLEX: B12 deficiency and Friedreich ataxia are the two classic combined-tract degenerations tested together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 7 — dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes used for bedside lesion localization, now expanded with tone, fasciculation, and atrophy findings for full motor lesion localization","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how spinal interneurons coordinate agonist-antagonist relationships and bilateral limb responses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old healthy volunteer participates in a reflex physiology study. When her biceps brachii is briskly stretched, researchers record simultaneous excitatory postsynaptic potentials in the biceps alpha motor neuron and inhibitory postsynaptic potentials in the triceps alpha motor neuron, both occurring within a few milliseconds of the stretch, using recording electrodes placed at the same spinal segment. Which single afferent fiber is responsible for generating both postsynaptic potentials?","choices":{"A":"A type Ib afferent originating from the biceps Golgi tendon organ","B":"A separate type II afferent from the triceps muscle spindle","C":"A cutaneous afferent from skin overlying the elbow joint","D":"A propriospinal neuron connecting adjacent cervical segments","E":"The same Ia afferent from the stretched biceps muscle spindle"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A single Ia afferent axon from the stretched biceps spindle bifurcates within the spinal cord: one branch synapses monosynaptically and excitatorily onto the biceps, agonist, alpha motor neuron, while a collateral branch excites a dedicated Ia inhibitory interneuron that in turn inhibits the triceps, antagonist, alpha motor neuron. Both postsynaptic potentials therefore originate from the same afferent fiber and occur nearly simultaneously because the excitatory pathway is monosynaptic and the inhibitory pathway adds only a single interneuron delay.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reciprocal inhibition from a single Ia afferent explains both halves of the stretch reflex response in one elegant circuit, a favorite integrative physiology figure question. COMLEX: this circuit is implicit in every normal deep tendon reflex elicited on exam.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 6 — a single Ia afferent generating both agonist excitation and antagonist inhibition","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl39_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how spinal interneurons coordinate agonist-antagonist relationships and bilateral limb responses.","batch":2,"stem":"An investigator selectively blocks Renshaw cell activity in an experimental spinal cord preparation using a pharmacologic antagonist, then electrically stimulates an alpha motor neuron pool. Compared to baseline, which change in motor neuron pool behavior is expected?","choices":{"A":"Loss of the monosynaptic stretch reflex within that same motor pool","B":"Increased, less regulated firing of the same motor neuron pool","C":"Loss of reciprocal inhibition acting on the antagonist pool","D":"Increased inhibition of the antagonist muscle pool","E":"No measurable change in motor neuron pool behavior"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Renshaw cells receive a recurrent collateral from the alpha motor neuron axon itself and feed back to inhibit that same motor neuron pool, recurrent inhibition, which normally limits and smooths ongoing motor neuron firing and helps prevent excessive, poorly regulated discharge. Blocking Renshaw cell activity removes this negative feedback loop, so the same motor neuron pool would fire more vigorously and less smoothly, without directly affecting the Ia-mediated monosynaptic stretch reflex or reciprocal inhibition of the antagonist, which depend on different interneurons entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Renshaw cell recurrent inhibition is a negative feedback loop onto the same pool that generated it, distinct from reciprocal inhibition of the antagonist. COMLEX: tetanus toxin blocks glycinergic Renshaw inhibition, producing the sustained rigid spasms characteristic of that disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 6 — loss of Renshaw cell recurrent inhibition on motor neuron pool regulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl39_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how spinal interneurons coordinate agonist-antagonist relationships and bilateral limb responses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old child is observed walking with well-coordinated, alternating flexion and extension of the left and right legs, each limb moving in the opposite phase of the other. Researchers propose that spinal circuits alone, independent of ongoing cortical input, can generate this basic alternating pattern. Which spinal neuron population is most directly responsible for coordinating this bilateral, alternating limb activity?","choices":{"A":"Propriospinal interneurons connecting lumbosacral segments bilaterally","B":"Ia inhibitory interneurons acting only within a single isolated limb","C":"Renshaw cells acting only on ipsilateral motor neuron pools locally","D":"Dorsal root ganglion cell bodies located individually at each spinal segment","E":"Postganglionic sympathetic neurons of the lumbar sympathetic chain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Propriospinal interneurons project axons within the spinal cord across multiple segments and across the midline, linking the lumbosacral circuits that drive each leg so that flexor activity on one side is coordinated with extensor activity on the other, the anatomic substrate proposed for spinal central pattern generators capable of producing basic alternating stepping movements even with reduced supraspinal input. Ia inhibitory interneurons and Renshaw cells act locally within a single limb's circuitry and cannot by themselves coordinate activity between the two legs. Postganglionic sympathetic neurons are autonomic efferents to glands and smooth and cardiac muscle, not part of the somatic stepping circuitry.","boardPrep":"Step 1: propriospinal connections are the anatomic basis proposed for spinal central pattern generators underlying locomotion, a concept increasingly tested in integrative physiology. COMLEX: preserved reflexive stepping-like movements below a complete spinal cord lesion reflect this intrinsic spinal circuitry.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 6 — propriospinal interneurons coordinating bilateral alternating limb movement","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl39_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the major brainstem descending pathways that influence anterior horn neurons: vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, tectospinal, and rubrospinal systems.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man sustains a complete transection of the brainstem at the midcollicular level following severe head trauma. On exam, all four limbs show extensor hypertonus. Both the vestibular nuclei and the medullary reticular formation lie caudal to this transection, yet only the vestibulospinal drive remains fully effective. Which feature of the vestibulospinal system best explains this difference?","choices":{"A":"The vestibulospinal tract originates rostral to the level of transection","B":"The vestibulospinal tract is more heavily myelinated than other tracts","C":"The vestibulospinal tract travels exclusively in the posterior funiculus","D":"The vestibulospinal system receives no direct projection from the cerebral cortex","E":"The vestibulospinal tract receives compensatory rubrospinal input"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The cerebral cortex has essentially no direct projections to the vestibular nuclei, so a lesion that isolates the brainstem and spinal cord from the cortex, as in a midcollicular transection producing decerebrate rigidity, leaves the vestibulospinal system's normal excitatory drive from the vestibular sensory apparatus and cerebellar fastigial nucleus entirely intact. The medullary reticulospinal system, by contrast, depends on cortical drive that the transection removes. The vestibulospinal tract arises caudal, not rostral, to the transection; its myelination and funicular position do not explain the difference; and the rubrospinal tract does not compensate for it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the vestibulospinal system's lack of cortical input explains why decerebrate rigidity features unopposed extensor hypertonus in all four limbs. COMLEX: this same principle explains why vestibular reflexes, like the vestibulo-ocular reflex, can persist in comatose patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 7 — vestibulospinal independence from cortical input explaining preserved function after decerebration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the major brainstem descending pathways that influence anterior horn neurons: vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, tectospinal, and rubrospinal systems.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with a large right hemispheric tumor develops flexion of the left arm at the elbow with extension of the left leg, while the right side remains unaffected. Imaging shows the lesion compresses cortical projections to the midbrain but does not extend into the brainstem itself. Which brainstem pathway explains the preserved left arm flexion despite loss of cortical input?","choices":{"A":"The rubrospinal tract, still activated by intact cerebellorubral input","B":"The lateral vestibulospinal tract, driven by intact vestibular input","C":"The medial reticulospinal tract, activated by intact pontine input","D":"The tectospinal tract, activated by intact visual input","E":"The lateral corticospinal tract, partially spared by the lesion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Decorticate posturing produces upper extremity flexion because the rubrospinal tract, which excites motor neurons supplying proximal limb flexors mainly in the upper extremity, continues to receive excitatory input from the cerebellar nuclei via cerebellorubral projections even when cortical input to the red nucleus, via corticorubral fibers, is lost. This is distinct from the extensor hypertonus produced by the vestibulospinal and reticulospinal systems, which explains the accompanying lower extremity extension in decorticate posturing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: decorticate posturing, arm flexion, leg extension, reflects intact rubrospinal flexor drive to the arms plus intact vestibulospinal and reticulospinal extensor drive to the legs. COMLEX: distinguishing decorticate from decerebrate posturing localizes the lesion level and helps predict trajectory.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 7 — rubrospinal-mediated arm flexion in decorticate posturing despite lost cortical input","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"This is distinct from the exte"}},{"id":"sdl39_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the major brainstem descending pathways that influence anterior horn neurons: vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, tectospinal, and rubrospinal systems.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with a severe traumatic brain injury develops bilateral extensor posturing of all four limbs. When the nursing staff performs endotracheal suctioning, a noxious stimulus, the extensor posturing visibly worsens. Which descending pathway activation best explains this noxious-stimulus-induced worsening?","choices":{"A":"The inhibitory medullary reticulospinal tract, driven by descending cortical input alone","B":"The lateral vestibulospinal tract, driven directly by nociceptive afferent input","C":"The rubrospinal tract, driven primarily by cerebellar nuclear input alone","D":"The tectospinal tract, driven by ascending auditory brainstem input signals","E":"The excitatory pontine reticulospinal tract, driven by ascending anterolateral input"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In decerebrate rigidity, the excitatory pontine reticulospinal tract retains its ascending nociceptive input from the anterolateral system, while the inhibitory medullary reticulospinal component has lost the excitatory cortical drive it needs to function; a noxious stimulus therefore selectively activates the still-functional excitatory pontine component without engaging the now-silent inhibitory medullary component, worsening the already unopposed extensor hypertonus. The vestibulospinal, rubrospinal, and tectospinal systems are not the pathways specifically implicated in this noxious-stimulus-driven exacerbation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: noxious stimuli worsening decerebrate rigidity through the pontine reticulospinal tract is a classic bedside teaching point in comatose patients. COMLEX: recognizing exacerbation with stimulation helps confirm decerebrate rather than decorticate posturing at the bedside.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 7 — pontine reticulospinal exacerbation of decerebrate rigidity by noxious stimuli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The vestibulospinal, rubrospin"}},{"id":"sdl39_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Differentiate lower motor neuron signs from upper motor neuron signs and connect them to lesion localization.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman presents with 3 months of progressive right hand weakness. Exam reveals atrophy and fasciculations of the right first dorsal interosseous muscle, absent triceps reflex on the right, and normal sensation throughout. Weeks later, her left leg becomes weak, with brisk patellar and Achilles reflexes and a positive Babinski sign on that side. Which statement best characterizes this combined presentation?","choices":{"A":"A single lower motor neuron lesion affecting multiple limbs simultaneously and to an equal degree","B":"A pure sensory lesion with no motor tract involvement","C":"A single upper motor neuron lesion affecting multiple limbs simultaneously and equally","D":"A peripheral polyneuropathy affecting all four limbs in a symmetric distribution","E":"Coexisting lower motor neuron findings in one limb and upper motor neuron findings in another"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient shows classic lower motor neuron findings, atrophy, fasciculations, and hyporeflexia, in her right arm alongside classic upper motor neuron findings, hyperreflexia and a Babinski sign, in her left leg, with sensation entirely preserved throughout; this simultaneous, mixed UMN-and-LMN pattern without sensory loss is the defining clinical signature of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, in which both the anterior horn cells and the corticospinal tracts degenerate together but not always symmetrically or synchronously across limbs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mixed UMN and LMN signs in different limbs, with no sensory loss, should immediately suggest ALS on the differential. COMLEX: early asymmetric presentation, as in this patient, is a common real-world pattern that can delay diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 8 — mixed upper and lower motor neuron findings across limbs pointing to ALS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Differentiate lower motor neuron signs from upper motor neuron signs and connect them to lesion localization.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man is found to have weakness, atrophy, and fasciculations in his right hand along with brisk reflexes and a positive Hoffmann sign in the same right arm. Which statement about this apparently contradictory single-limb finding is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"This combination is never physiologically possible and always suggests a factitious presentation","B":"The Hoffmann sign in this context should be disregarded as a normal anatomic variant","C":"Both LMN and UMN signs can coexist in the same limb when one disease affects both pathways","D":"LMN signs always predominate completely over UMN signs whenever both coexist in one limb","E":"This pattern can only occur if there are two entirely separate and unrelated lesions in that limb"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In diseases like ALS that simultaneously degenerate the anterior horn cell, lower motor neuron, and the corticospinal tract, upper motor neuron, within the same limb, it is entirely possible and expected to see LMN signs, atrophy and fasciculations, from denervation of the distal musculature alongside UMN signs, hyperreflexia and a Hoffmann sign, from loss of descending inhibition, both arising from the same underlying disease process rather than requiring two separate anatomic lesions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing that ALS can produce simultaneous UMN and LMN findings within a single limb is a key testable nuance beyond the simpler pure-UMN-versus-pure-LMN dichotomy. COMLEX: EMG can help confirm ongoing denervation even when reflexes appear paradoxically brisk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 8 — coexisting UMN and LMN signs within a single limb in combined degenerative disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Differentiate lower motor neuron signs from upper motor neuron signs and connect them to lesion localization.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man develops acute flaccid weakness of the right leg after an epidural abscess compresses the anterior horn at the L4 spinal level. Six weeks later, examination of that same leg shows persistent weakness, but the deep tendon reflexes below the lesion, previously absent, have become hyperactive, and a Babinski sign is now present. Which explanation best accounts for this evolving reflex pattern?","choices":{"A":"A second lesion has developed in the corticospinal tract above the anterior horn injury","B":"The original anterior horn cell injury has fully regenerated over the past six weeks","C":"Spinal shock from the anterior horn lesion is only now beginning, six weeks later","D":"Chronic LMN lesions eventually and naturally convert to a UMN reflex pattern over time","E":"The reflex change simply reflects normal healing of a purely LMN nerve injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A lesion confined to the anterior horn cell itself produces permanent LMN signs, flaccid weakness and hyporeflexia, at that segmental level, and this pattern does not spontaneously convert into hyperreflexia and a Babinski sign over time; the emergence of new UMN findings below the original lesion instead implies a second, separate process now affecting the corticospinal tract, such as an expanding abscess or a new compressive lesion higher in the cord, and should prompt urgent reevaluation rather than being attributed to healing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a change from LMN to UMN reflex findings over time is never simple healing and should always trigger a search for a new or expanding lesion. COMLEX: reassessing reflexes serially is an important part of monitoring a patient with a known spinal lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 8 — new upper motor neuron signs implying a second lesion above a prior anterior horn injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q25","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Apply motor pathway anatomy to common clinical patterns: poliomyelitis, spinal muscular atrophy, ALS, spinal cord injury, vestibular dysfunction, decerebrate rigidity, and spasticity.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-month-old previously healthy infant develops sudden onset of asymmetric leg weakness and fever after a several-day febrile gastrointestinal illness. Exam shows flaccid weakness of the left leg with absent reflexes and mild neck stiffness, but sensation is intact throughout, and cerebrospinal fluid shows a lymphocytic pleocytosis with normal glucose. Which infectious process best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Guillain-Barre syndrome from a preceding infection","B":"Bacterial meningitis with spinal cord abscess","C":"Herpes simplex encephalitis with spinal extension","D":"Enteroviral myositis without neural involvement","E":"Poliovirus infection of the anterior horn cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Poliovirus is transmitted fecal-orally, replicates in oropharyngeal and intestinal lymphoid tissue during the preceding gastrointestinal illness, and then spreads hematogenously to selectively destroy anterior horn cells, producing acute, often asymmetric flaccid weakness with hyporeflexia and preserved sensation, alongside the lymphocytic pleocytosis and normal glucose typical of the accompanying viral meningitis. Guillain-Barre syndrome typically produces more symmetric, ascending sensorimotor weakness rather than a purely motor, asymmetric pattern like this one. HSV encephalitis is a temporal lobe infection, not a cause of flaccid leg weakness, and a myositis without neural involvement would not explain the neck stiffness and lymphocytic pleocytosis of the accompanying viral meningitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: asymmetric acute flaccid paralysis with preserved sensation and viral meningitis features is the classic poliomyelitis presentation. COMLEX: distinguishing poliomyelitis, pure motor, from Guillain-Barre syndrome, sensorimotor, is a frequently tested pairing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 9 — poliovirus-induced acute flaccid paralysis distinguished from Guillain-Barre syndrome","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"HSV encephalitis is a temporal"}},{"id":"sdl39_q26","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Apply motor pathway anatomy to common clinical patterns: poliomyelitis, spinal muscular atrophy, ALS, spinal cord injury, vestibular dysfunction, decerebrate rigidity, and spasticity.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man involved in a high-speed motor vehicle collision sustains a complete spinal cord injury at T6. Immediately after the injury, exam shows flaccid paralysis and absent reflexes below the level of injury. Three weeks later, the same exam shows spastic paralysis with hyperreflexia and a Babinski sign below the same level. Which process best explains this evolution over the first three weeks?","choices":{"A":"Progressive ascending injury steadily extending the lesion level rostrally","B":"Delayed onset of a second, entirely separate anterior horn cell injury","C":"Resolution of spinal shock, unmasking the underlying upper motor neuron pattern","D":"Gradual regeneration of the previously transected corticospinal tract fibers over time","E":"Superimposed Guillain-Barre syndrome newly triggered by the recent trauma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Immediately after a severe spinal cord injury, spinal shock produces a temporary state of flaccid paralysis and areflexia below the lesion, even though the injury has interrupted upper motor neuron pathways, because segmental reflex circuits below the lesion are transiently suppressed. Over days to weeks, spinal shock resolves and the expected upper motor neuron pattern, spasticity, hyperreflexia, and a Babinski sign, emerges as the disconnected but structurally intact segmental reflex arcs below the lesion regain and then exceed their normal excitability, without any new injury or fiber regeneration. Guillain-Barre syndrome is a demyelinating peripheral neuropathy with depressed or absent reflexes, so it could not add hyperreflexia or a Babinski sign.","boardPrep":"Step 1: spinal shock temporarily masks the UMN pattern expected after a cord transection and must be distinguished from lower motor neuron injury. COMLEX: bulbocavernosus reflex return is used clinically to mark the end of spinal shock.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 9 — resolution of spinal shock unmasking upper motor neuron signs after cord injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q27","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Apply motor pathway anatomy to common clinical patterns: poliomyelitis, spinal muscular atrophy, ALS, spinal cord injury, vestibular dysfunction, decerebrate rigidity, and spasticity.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman with a family history of a similar condition presents with progressive gait ataxia, absent ankle reflexes, loss of vibration and position sense, a positive Babinski sign, kyphoscoliosis, and pes cavus. She is also found to have hypertrophic cardiomyopathy on echocardiogram. Genetic testing reveals a GAA trinucleotide repeat expansion. Which combination of spinal tracts best explains her neurologic findings?","choices":{"A":"Isolated degeneration of the anterior horn cells on both sides equally","B":"Isolated degeneration of the lateral corticospinal tracts only, with nothing else involved","C":"Combined dorsal column, spinocerebellar, and lateral corticospinal degeneration","D":"Isolated degeneration of the posterior roots and dorsal root ganglia alone","E":"Combined degeneration of the anterolateral system and dorsal columns alone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This presentation describes Friedreich ataxia, an autosomal recessive GAA trinucleotide repeat disorder affecting the frataxin gene that impairs mitochondrial function and produces combined degeneration of the dorsal columns, causing loss of vibration and position sense, the spinocerebellar tracts, causing ataxia, and the lateral corticospinal tracts, causing a Babinski sign, alongside dorsal root ganglion loss producing absent reflexes, together with the characteristic skeletal findings of kyphoscoliosis and pes cavus and the frequently fatal complication of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Friedreich ataxia combines dorsal column, spinocerebellar, and corticospinal degeneration with absent reflexes and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a heavily tested First Aid triad. COMLEX: the mnemonic 'Friedreich is fratastic' links frataxin, ataxia, and the staggering gait.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 9 — Friedreich ataxia combined tract degeneration and its systemic associations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q28","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Use reflex abnormalities, tone, fasciculations, atrophy, and sensory findings to localize board-style motor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man presents with weakness and numbness. Exam shows normal strength and reflexes in the arms, but in the legs there is weakness, hyperreflexia, a Babinski sign, and loss of vibration and position sense, with pain and temperature sensation fully intact throughout. Deep tendon reflexes in the legs are notably brisker than in the arms. Which spinal level and tract pattern best localizes this lesion?","choices":{"A":"A cervical-level lesion affecting only the anterior horn cells on both sides","B":"A thoracic lesion affecting the corticospinal tracts and dorsal columns","C":"A lumbosacral-level lesion affecting only the posterior nerve roots bilaterally","D":"A thoracic-level lesion affecting only the anterolateral system on both sides","E":"A cervical-level lesion affecting only the spinocerebellar tracts bilaterally"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Normal arm findings with leg-only UMN signs, hyperreflexia and Babinski, plus leg-only dorsal column loss, vibration and position sense, but fully preserved pain and temperature localizes the lesion to a thoracic level, below the cervical segments supplying the arms, affecting the lateral corticospinal tracts and dorsal columns bilaterally while sparing the anterolateral system; this pattern is classic for conditions like subacute combined degeneration or Friedreich ataxia rather than a lesion involving the spinothalamic tract or a purely peripheral process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: localizing by which limbs are spared versus involved, plus which sensory modality is spared, is the core skill tested in spinal cord localization vignettes. COMLEX: always map out arm-versus-leg involvement first before narrowing to a specific tract pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 10 — localizing a thoracic dorsal column and corticospinal lesion by arm-sparing, leg-involved pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q29","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Use reflex abnormalities, tone, fasciculations, atrophy, and sensory findings to localize board-style motor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman presents with new right-sided weakness. Exam shows increased tone, hyperreflexia, and a Babinski sign in the right arm and leg, with no muscle atrophy and no fasciculations anywhere, and sensation is entirely normal. Which additional finding, if present on repeat exam weeks later, would most strongly argue against a purely upper motor neuron process and instead suggest a superimposed lower motor neuron component?","choices":{"A":"Persistently brisk tendon reflexes in the right arm and leg","B":"A persistently positive Babinski sign on the right side","C":"Mildly increased tone that fluctuates throughout the day","D":"New atrophy and fasciculations appearing in the weak right arm","E":"Mild weakness that has remained stable in severity over time"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Atrophy and fasciculations require direct denervation of muscle from loss of the lower motor neuron itself, something a pure upper motor neuron lesion, which leaves the anterior horn cell and its axon intact, cannot produce; their new appearance on a limb that previously showed a pure UMN pattern would suggest either a second, coexisting lower motor neuron process or, in the right clinical context, a combined degenerative disease affecting both pathways. Persistently brisk reflexes, a persistent Babinski sign, fluctuating tone, and stable weakness are all consistent with an unchanged, pure UMN process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: new atrophy or fasciculations appearing in a previously pure-UMN limb should prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis, including ALS. COMLEX: serial exams that track for emerging LMN signs are an important part of longitudinal motor lesion evaluation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 10 — emerging atrophy and fasciculations signaling a superimposed lower motor neuron process","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q30","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Use reflex abnormalities, tone, fasciculations, atrophy, and sensory findings to localize board-style motor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man presents with a 8-month history of progressive weakness. Exam shows flaccid weakness, absent reflexes, and fasciculations in both arms, normal strength and reflexes in the trunk, and increased tone with hyperreflexia and a Babinski sign in both legs. Sensation is fully intact throughout. Which level and lesion pattern best explains this specific combination of findings?","choices":{"A":"A single lumbosacral root compression affecting both sides equally","B":"A diffuse peripheral polyneuropathy affecting all four limbs","C":"A cervical anterior horn process with corticospinal involvement below it","D":"An isolated thoracic dorsal column lesion affecting sensation only","E":"A pure cerebellar process affecting bilateral limb coordination only"},"correct":"C","explanation":"LMN signs, flaccid weakness, areflexia, and fasciculations confined to the arms, with UMN signs, spasticity, hyperreflexia, and a Babinski sign, confined to the legs, and fully preserved sensation throughout, localizes to a cervical-level process that directly destroys anterior horn cells at the cervical segments supplying the arms while simultaneously damaging the corticospinal tracts that then produce UMN signs in everything caudal to that level, the classic combined pattern seen in cervical-onset ALS rather than a peripheral nerve, dorsal column, or cerebellar process. A lumbosacral root lesion could produce neither arm findings nor UMN signs in the legs, because nerve roots carry LMN axons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LMN signs at the level of a lesion with UMN signs below it is a classic localizing pattern, seen here in cervical ALS and structurally analogous to anterior spinal artery syndrome. COMLEX: always separate 'at the level' findings from 'below the level' findings when localizing combined lesions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 10 — cervical-level anterior horn and corticospinal involvement producing arm LMN and leg UMN signs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_bloom_q01","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how spinal interneurons coordinate agonist-antagonist relationships and bilateral limb responses.","batch":3,"stem":"A researcher studies the flexor withdrawal reflex in an experimental spinal cord preparation. When one hindlimb is given a noxious stimulus, that limb flexes while the opposite hindlimb simultaneously extends to support body weight, and this crossed extension depends on interneurons whose axons cross the midline to reach the contralateral extensor motor neuron pool. Which interneuron population is most directly responsible for generating this crossed extensor response?","choices":{"A":"Ia inhibitory interneurons acting only within the same spinal side","B":"Renshaw cell interneurons acting only on the same ipsilateral motor pool","C":"Golgi tendon organ interneurons relaying tension information locally","D":"Commissural interneurons projecting across the spinal midline","E":"Dorsal root ganglion cell bodies located at each individual spinal segment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The crossed-extension reflex requires interneurons that cross the spinal midline to excite the contralateral extensor motor neuron pool at the same time the ipsilateral flexor withdrawal circuit is activated, a function specifically carried out by commissural interneurons, distinguishing this bilateral coordination from the purely ipsilateral, same-side circuitry of Ia inhibitory interneurons and Renshaw cells. Golgi tendon organ interneurons relay tension feedback rather than coordinating a crossed reflex, and dorsal root ganglion cell bodies are sensory relay stations, not the interneurons generating this specific bilateral motor output.","boardPrep":"Step 1: commissural interneurons are the specific circuit element that explains bilateral coordination in the crossed-extension reflex, worth separating from the purely ipsilateral reciprocal and recurrent inhibition circuits. COMLEX: this same crossed circuitry underlies the postural support seen clinically when one leg is suddenly withdrawn from a painful stimulus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 6 — spinal interneuron coordination of agonist-antagonist and bilateral responses, tested here as a board-style circuit-integration vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl39_bloom_q02","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how spinal interneurons coordinate agonist-antagonist relationships and bilateral limb responses.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with spasticity is started on a medication that acts as a GABA-B receptor agonist at presynaptic terminals of Ia afferents within the spinal cord, reducing excitatory neurotransmitter release from these terminals onto alpha motor neurons. Which effect best explains how this specific presynaptic mechanism reduces spasticity?","choices":{"A":"Increased postsynaptic Renshaw cell recurrent inhibitory output","B":"Reduced presynaptic Ia afferent neurotransmitter release onto alpha motor neurons","C":"Increased Ia inhibitory interneuron firing directed at the antagonist pool","D":"Direct postsynaptic blockade of acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction","E":"Increased Golgi tendon organ afferent firing threshold at the muscle tendon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This describes the mechanism of baclofen, which activates presynaptic GABA-B receptors on Ia afferent terminals to reduce calcium influx and neurotransmitter release, thereby directly dampening the excitatory monosynaptic drive onto the alpha motor neuron at its source rather than acting through the postsynaptic Renshaw cell, Ia inhibitory interneuron, neuromuscular junction, or Golgi tendon organ pathways, each of which represents a mechanistically distinct site of action not described in this scenario.","boardPrep":"Step 1: baclofen's presynaptic GABA-B mechanism on Ia afferent terminals is a distinct pharmacologic site from postsynaptic inhibitory interneuron circuits, worth knowing precisely for spasticity pharmacology questions. COMLEX: intrathecal baclofen pumps are used clinically for severe spasticity refractory to oral dosing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 6 — spinal interneuron coordination of agonist-antagonist and bilateral responses, tested here as a board-style circuit-integration vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl39_bloom_q03","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how spinal interneurons coordinate agonist-antagonist relationships and bilateral limb responses.","batch":3,"stem":"During patellar reflex testing, a patient is asked to interlock the fingers of both hands and pull them apart forcefully just before the reflex hammer strikes the tendon, and the resulting knee-jerk response becomes visibly stronger than when tested at rest. Which physiologic mechanism best explains this reflex reinforcement?","choices":{"A":"A lower firing threshold at the Ia afferent sensory ending itself","B":"Selectively decreased Renshaw cell inhibition of the quadriceps","C":"Facilitatory interneuronal input converging onto the leg alpha motor neuron pool","D":"Direct corticospinal tract activation of the quadriceps during hand pulling","E":"A temporary increase in Golgi tendon organ sensitivity during hand pulling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This reinforcement technique, the Jendrassik maneuver, works because the voluntary contraction of a distant muscle group generates facilitatory interneuronal input that converges onto and raises the excitability of the leg alpha motor neuron pool, making the same Ia afferent volley from the tendon tap produce a stronger reflex response, rather than through any change in the Ia afferent's own sensory threshold, a selective Renshaw effect, direct corticospinal drive to the quadriceps, or a Golgi tendon organ change, none of which explain this remote facilitation phenomenon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Jendrassik maneuver is a classic bedside reinforcement technique used when a deep tendon reflex is difficult to elicit at rest. COMLEX: this technique is routinely used during the neurologic exam when reflexes appear diminished or absent on first attempt.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 6 — spinal interneuron coordination of agonist-antagonist and bilateral responses, tested here as a board-style circuit-integration vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl39_bloom_q04","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the major brainstem descending pathways that influence anterior horn neurons: vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, tectospinal, and rubrospinal systems.","batch":3,"stem":"A physiologist studies a descending vestibular pathway and finds that it originates from the medial vestibular nucleus, projects bilaterally, and terminates only at cervical spinal levels to stabilize head and neck position during body movement, in contrast to a separate ipsilateral vestibular pathway that extends the full length of the cord to control limb extensor tone. Which tract is being described in this first, bilateral, cervical-restricted pathway?","choices":{"A":"Medial vestibulospinal tract","B":"Lateral vestibulospinal tract","C":"Lateral reticulospinal tract","D":"Rubrospinal tract","E":"Tectospinal tract"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The medial vestibulospinal tract arises from the medial vestibular nucleus, projects bilaterally, and is restricted to cervical spinal levels, where it coordinates head and neck stabilization, the vestibulocollic reflex, during body movement, a function distinct from the lateral vestibulospinal tract, which is ipsilateral, extends the full length of the cord, and controls antigravity extensor tone. The reticulospinal, rubrospinal, and tectospinal tracts arise from entirely different brainstem nuclei and do not match this specific bilateral, cervical-restricted vestibular projection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: separating the medial vestibulospinal tract, bilateral and cervical-only, from the lateral vestibulospinal tract, ipsilateral and full-length, is a frequently tested pairing distinction. COMLEX: the vestibulocollic reflex mediated by this tract is clinically relevant to head stabilization during gait and rapid movement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 7 — the vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, tectospinal, and rubrospinal systems, tested here as a board-style pathway-discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl39_bloom_q05","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the major brainstem descending pathways that influence anterior horn neurons: vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, tectospinal, and rubrospinal systems.","batch":3,"stem":"Just before a standing person voluntarily raises one arm to catch a thrown ball, researchers record activity in trunk and leg muscles that begins slightly before the arm movement itself, adjusting posture in anticipation of the upcoming shift in the body's center of gravity. Which descending system is most directly responsible for this anticipatory postural adjustment of the trunk and legs?","choices":{"A":"Lateral corticospinal tract, coordinating precise distal limb movement","B":"Reticulospinal tract, coordinating gross postural muscle activity","C":"Rubrospinal tract, coordinating proximal upper extremity flexion only","D":"Tectospinal tract, coordinating reflexive head orientation toward a stimulus","E":"Medial vestibulospinal tract, coordinating cervical head stabilization only"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The reticulospinal tract projects widely and bilaterally to influence axial and proximal postural musculature, allowing it to generate anticipatory, feedforward postural adjustments in the trunk and legs just before a voluntary limb movement disturbs balance, a coordinating role distinct from the lateral corticospinal tract's function in precise, planned distal limb movement itself. The rubrospinal, tectospinal, and medial vestibulospinal tracts are each restricted to more specific proximal-flexor, orienting, or cervical-only roles that do not explain this broad anticipatory postural response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the reticulospinal tract's role in feedforward postural adjustment, occurring before a planned voluntary movement, is a key integrative motor control concept beyond its role in basic tone regulation. COMLEX: impaired anticipatory postural adjustments contribute to fall risk in patients with reticulospinal pathway dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 7 — the vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, tectospinal, and rubrospinal systems, tested here as a board-style pathway-discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl39_bloom_q06","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the major brainstem descending pathways that influence anterior horn neurons: vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, tectospinal, and rubrospinal systems.","batch":3,"stem":"In a decerebrate animal with rigid extension of all limbs, the investigator cuts the posterior roots serving one hindlimb. The extensor hypertonus in that limb collapses immediately, although the descending reticulospinal and vestibulospinal axons are untouched. Which conclusion does this experiment support?","choices":{"A":"Descending drive reaches extensor alpha neurons directly","B":"Rigidity depends on intact cortical input to the cord","C":"Rigidity arises from the cerebellar anterior lobe","D":"Rigidity is maintained by Golgi tendon organ input","E":"Descending drive acts mainly through gamma motor neurons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cutting the posterior root removes the Ia afferents of the gamma loop, so gamma motor neurons can no longer indirectly drive extensor alpha motor neurons; the immediate collapse of hypertonus shows that the descending brainstem influence acts mainly on gamma motor neurons, which is why decerebrate rigidity is also called gamma rigidity. If the drive reached alpha motor neurons directly, root section would not abolish it. Cortical input is already removed by decerebration, removing the cerebellar anterior lobe increases rather than explains the rigidity, and Golgi tendon organ input inhibits rather than maintains tone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gamma loop: gamma motor neuron, intrafusal contraction, Ia firing, alpha motor neuron, extrafusal contraction; dorsal root section breaks the loop. COMLEX: After anterior lobe removal the extensor tone survives root section, indicating direct vestibulospinal drive to alpha motor neurons.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 2 — gamma motor neurons and intrafusal fibers that make up the gamma loop","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl39_bloom_q07","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Differentiate lower motor neuron signs from upper motor neuron signs and connect them to lesion localization.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient's ankle is passively and sharply dorsiflexed and then held in that position, producing several rhythmic, self-sustaining beats of alternating plantarflexion and dorsiflexion of the foot before the movement fades. Which underlying change best explains this finding, termed clonus?","choices":{"A":"Denervation of the gastrocnemius muscle producing spontaneous fiber contraction","B":"Loss of Golgi tendon organ afferent input removing protective autogenic inhibition","C":"Direct anterior horn cell injury producing disorganized motor unit firing","D":"Loss of descending inhibitory control producing a hyperexcitable stretch reflex arc","E":"Peripheral nerve demyelination producing conduction block at the ankle"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Clonus, rhythmic, self-sustaining oscillation of a muscle in response to a sustained stretch, reflects an upper motor neuron lesion that removes normal descending inhibitory control over the segmental stretch reflex arc, leaving it hyperexcitable enough that a single sustained stretch triggers repeated cycles of contraction and relaxation. Denervation, loss of Golgi tendon organ input, direct anterior horn cell injury, and peripheral nerve demyelination are all lower motor neuron or peripheral mechanisms that would instead produce weakness, fasciculations, or conduction block rather than this specific hyperexcitable UMN reflex oscillation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: clonus is a classic upper motor neuron sign reflecting loss of descending inhibition over the stretch reflex arc, frequently tested alongside spasticity and the Babinski sign. COMLEX: sustained clonus, more than a few beats, is considered pathologic and should prompt further UMN lesion workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 8 — the LMN-versus-UMN sign framework, tested here as a board-style additional-sign localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"A":"Denervation, loss of Golgi ten"}},{"id":"sdl39_bloom_q08","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Differentiate lower motor neuron signs from upper motor neuron signs and connect them to lesion localization.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has hyperreflexia and a positive Babinski sign in the legs. On additional exam, stroking the skin of the abdomen fails to elicit the normal reflexive contraction of the underlying abdominal muscles that is present when the same maneuver is performed above the level of the umbilicus. Which statement best explains this specific additional finding?","choices":{"A":"Superficial abdominal reflexes are lost only with a lower motor neuron lesion","B":"Superficial abdominal reflexes are unrelated to either upper or lower motor neuron status","C":"Superficial abdominal reflexes become exaggerated, not lost, with upper motor neuron lesions","D":"Superficial abdominal reflexes are lost below an upper motor neuron lesion level","E":"Superficial abdominal reflexes test only peripheral sensory nerve integrity, not motor pathways"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Unlike deep tendon reflexes, which become hyperactive with an upper motor neuron lesion, superficial cutaneous reflexes such as the abdominal reflex are lost below the level of a corticospinal tract lesion because their reflex arc requires intact descending facilitation to remain functional, making this a useful additional localizing sign, in this case a lesion at or above the thoracic segments supplying the lower abdominal reflex. A lower motor neuron lesion at the exact segmental level tested could also abolish a superficial reflex, but the combination described, hyperreflexia and Babinski in the legs, points specifically to an upper motor neuron process rather than a purely peripheral one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the superficial abdominal reflex being lost, rather than exaggerated, with an upper motor neuron lesion is a commonly missed exception to the general UMN hyperreflexia rule and is worth memorizing precisely. COMLEX: absent abdominal reflexes below a specific thoracic dermatome help localize the rostral extent of a spinal cord lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 8 — the LMN-versus-UMN sign framework, tested here as a board-style additional-sign localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl39_bloom_q09","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Differentiate lower motor neuron signs from upper motor neuron signs and connect them to lesion localization.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has a weak, atrophic tongue with visible fasciculations and difficulty swallowing, while a second patient has a spastic, slow-moving tongue with an exaggerated jaw jerk reflex and inappropriate, uncontrollable episodes of laughing and crying, but no tongue atrophy or fasciculations. Which statement best characterizes the difference between these two presentations?","choices":{"A":"Both presentations reflect the identical underlying lower motor neuron cranial nerve pathology throughout","B":"Both presentations reflect the identical underlying upper motor neuron corticobulbar pathology throughout","C":"The first patient has pseudobulbar palsy and the second has true bulbar palsy, reversed","D":"Neither presentation reflects a motor neuron pattern, both are purely sensory findings","E":"Bulbar palsy from cranial nerve nuclei loss versus pseudobulbar palsy from bilateral corticobulbar loss"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The first patient's atrophic, fasciculating tongue with dysphagia reflects bulbar palsy, direct lower motor neuron loss of the brainstem cranial nerve nuclei themselves, while the second patient's spastic tongue, exaggerated jaw jerk, and emotional lability reflect pseudobulbar palsy, an upper motor neuron pattern from bilateral corticobulbar tract loss above the brainstem nuclei, which are themselves intact and therefore show no atrophy or fasciculations. These two presentations are mirror-image UMN and LMN patterns applied to the cranial musculature rather than identical underlying pathology, and the diagnostic labels are not reversed between the two patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bulbar versus pseudobulbar palsy extends the LMN-versus-UMN framework to cranial nerve findings and is a classic ALS-related distinction, since ALS can produce either or both patterns. COMLEX: emotional lability, pseudobulbar affect, specifically points toward the UMN, corticobulbar, component rather than the LMN, bulbar, component.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 8 — the LMN-versus-UMN sign framework, tested here as a board-style additional-sign localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"C":"These two presentations are mi"}},{"id":"sdl39_bloom_q10","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Apply motor pathway anatomy to common clinical patterns: poliomyelitis, spinal muscular atrophy, ALS, spinal cord injury, vestibular dysfunction, decerebrate rigidity, and spasticity.","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman presents with several weeks of right eye pain worsened by movement and blurred vision, followed a month later by an electric shock-like sensation shooting down her spine when she flexes her neck. Exam now reveals leg weakness, hyperreflexia, and a positive Babinski sign. MRI shows multiple discrete white matter lesions. Which process best explains this combination of findings?","choices":{"A":"Multiple sclerosis, demyelination of the corticospinal tract and optic nerve","B":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, degeneration of upper and lower motor neurons","C":"Friedreich ataxia, combined dorsal column and corticospinal degeneration","D":"Anterior spinal artery occlusion, infarction of the anterior two-thirds of the cord","E":"Guillain-Barre syndrome, autoimmune demyelination of peripheral nerve roots"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This patient's optic neuritis, eye pain with vision loss, combined with a neck-flexion-induced electric shock sensation down the spine, Lhermitte sign, and later corticospinal tract signs from discrete, multifocal white matter lesions on MRI is classic for multiple sclerosis, an autoimmune demyelinating disease that produces upper motor neuron signs through demyelination rather than through motor neuron degeneration, arterial infarction, or peripheral nerve root involvement, which are the mechanisms underlying ALS, Friedreich ataxia, anterior spinal artery occlusion, and Guillain-Barre syndrome respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: optic neuritis plus Lhermitte sign plus multifocal white matter lesions is a classic multiple sclerosis triad, producing UMN signs through a demyelinating rather than degenerative or vascular mechanism. COMLEX: relapsing-remitting symptom timing, as seen here with distinct episodes weeks apart, is a hallmark feature supporting this diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 9 — motor pathway anatomy applied to named clinical patterns, tested here as a board-style diagnostic-pattern vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl39_bloom_q11","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Apply motor pathway anatomy to common clinical patterns: poliomyelitis, spinal muscular atrophy, ALS, spinal cord injury, vestibular dysfunction, decerebrate rigidity, and spasticity.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with long-standing cervical spondylosis falls forward and strikes his forehead, hyperextending his neck. He develops weakness that is markedly worse in both hands and arms than in his legs, along with a \"cape-like\" pattern of reduced pain and temperature sensation across his shoulders and upper arms, while leg strength and sensation are relatively preserved. Which anatomic principle best explains why the arms are disproportionately affected compared to the legs?","choices":{"A":"Cervical, upper extremity fibers lie more centrally within the affected tracts","B":"Lumbosacral, lower extremity fibers lie more centrally within the affected tracts","C":"The anterior horn cells supplying the arms are more vulnerable to hyperextension injury","D":"The corticospinal tract carries no fibers destined for the lower extremities","E":"The dorsal columns are entirely spared in every central cervical cord injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This presentation describes central cord syndrome, typically from a hyperextension injury in a patient with a pre-existing narrow cervical canal, in which the somatotopic organization of the corticospinal and spinothalamic tracts places cervical, upper extremity fibers more centrally and lumbosacral, lower extremity fibers more peripherally, so a centrally located injury preferentially damages the upper extremity fibers while relatively sparing the more peripherally located leg fibers. The remaining options either reverse this somatotopy incorrectly, misattribute the injury to the anterior horn cells, or make an inaccurate categorical claim about which fibers or columns are involved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: central cord syndrome's arms-worse-than-legs pattern is explained by somatotopic fiber lamination, a classic hyperextension-injury presentation in older patients with cervical spondylosis. COMLEX: this is the most common incomplete spinal cord injury syndrome and should be recognized from the mechanism and exam pattern together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 9 — motor pathway anatomy applied to named clinical patterns, tested here as a board-style diagnostic-pattern vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl39_bloom_q12","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Apply motor pathway anatomy to common clinical patterns: poliomyelitis, spinal muscular atrophy, ALS, spinal cord injury, vestibular dysfunction, decerebrate rigidity, and spasticity.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man is followed for eight years with slowly progressive leg stiffness, hyperreflexia, and bilateral Babinski signs. Despite the long disease course, he has never developed muscle atrophy, fasciculations, or any lower motor neuron findings on repeated exams, and electromyography remains normal. Which motor neuron disease variant, distinct from classic ALS, best fits this pure, isolated pattern?","choices":{"A":"Progressive muscular atrophy, isolated lower motor neuron degeneration","B":"Classic amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, combined upper and lower motor neuron degeneration","C":"Spinal muscular atrophy, isolated anterior horn cell degeneration","D":"Primary lateral sclerosis, isolated upper motor neuron degeneration","E":"Poliomyelitis, viral destruction of anterior horn cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Primary lateral sclerosis is a rare motor neuron disease variant that selectively degenerates upper motor neurons in the corticospinal tract without ever developing the lower motor neuron findings, atrophy, fasciculations, or abnormal EMG, that would instead be expected in classic ALS or in the purely lower motor neuron pattern of progressive muscular atrophy, spinal muscular atrophy, or poliomyelitis, all of which are excluded by this patient's persistently isolated, pure UMN presentation over many years.","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary lateral sclerosis is the pure-UMN counterpart to progressive muscular atrophy's pure-LMN pattern, with classic ALS representing the combined form, a three-way distinction worth knowing precisely. COMLEX: the many-year stability without LMN conversion, as in this patient, is itself a diagnostic clue favoring primary lateral sclerosis over early ALS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 9 — motor pathway anatomy applied to named clinical patterns, tested here as a board-style diagnostic-pattern vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl39_bloom_q13","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Use reflex abnormalities, tone, fasciculations, atrophy, and sensory findings to localize board-style motor lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 43-year-old woman has 6 months of drooping of the right eyelid late in the day, double vision, and trouble chewing and swallowing that improve with rest. Ptosis worsens during sustained upward gaze, pupils react normally, reflexes are normal, and there is no atrophy, fasciculation, or sensory loss. Where does this weakness localize?","choices":{"A":"Neuromuscular junction, postsynaptic membrane","B":"Anterior horn cells of the brainstem and cord","C":"Lateral corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts","D":"Peripheral nerve axons of the cranial nerves","E":"Oculomotor nucleus within the rostral midbrain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fatigable weakness that fluctuates over the day, begins in the ocular muscles in about two-thirds of patients, and spares pupils, reflexes, bulk, and sensation localizes to the neuromuscular junction; in myasthenia gravis, antibodies bind postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors and cause their lysis. Anterior horn cell disease brings atrophy and fasciculations, a corticospinal or corticobulbar lesion brings hyperreflexia and spasticity, and an axonal neuropathy brings reflex or sensory loss. An oculomotor nucleus lesion would not explain chewing and swallowing weakness and usually affects the pupil.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myasthenia gravis: autoantibodies to postsynaptic nicotinic ACh receptors; ocular onset, fatigability, normal pupils and reflexes. COMLEX: Respiratory muscle involvement is a myasthenic crisis that may need rapid airway support.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — fast ionotropic receptor signaling, as at the nicotinic neuromuscular junction","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"D":"Anterior horn cell disease bri"}},{"id":"sdl39_bloom_q14","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Use reflex abnormalities, tone, fasciculations, atrophy, and sensory findings to localize board-style motor lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has weakness and absent reflexes confined to a single myotome with sensory loss confined to the corresponding single dermatome on one side. A second patient has symmetric distal weakness in both feet, absent ankle reflexes bilaterally, and sensory loss in a stocking distribution over both feet. Which pattern of nerve involvement best distinguishes these two patients?","choices":{"A":"A single peripheral mononeuropathy versus an isolated anterior horn cell disease","B":"A single nerve root radiculopathy versus an isolated anterior horn cell disease","C":"A distal symmetric polyneuropathy versus an isolated corticospinal tract lesion","D":"A single peripheral mononeuropathy versus a proximal, not distal, myopathy pattern","E":"A single nerve root radiculopathy versus a distal symmetric polyneuropathy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The first patient's findings confined to one dermatome and myotome on one side reflect a single nerve root radiculopathy, while the second patient's bilateral, symmetric, distal, stocking-distribution findings reflect a distal symmetric polyneuropathy, a length-dependent process affecting the longest peripheral nerves first; this distinction, focal and unilateral versus diffuse and symmetric, is the key localizing principle, and the remaining answer options incorrectly substitute anterior horn cell, corticospinal, or myopathic processes that do not match either patient's described sensory and reflex findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguishing a focal single-root radiculopathy from a diffuse, length-dependent polyneuropathy by distribution pattern, dermatomal versus stocking-glove, is a core localization skill for the peripheral nervous system. COMLEX: reflex loss confined to one level suggests a root or focal nerve lesion, while diffuse distal reflex loss suggests a length-dependent neuropathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 10 — reflex, tone, fasciculation, and sensory findings used for lesion localization, tested here as a board-style comparative-localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl39_bloom_q15","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Use reflex abnormalities, tone, fasciculations, atrophy, and sensory findings to localize board-style motor lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has foot drop and sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot. On further exam, hip abduction strength, tested by resisting the examiner's hand while lying on one side, is also weak, even though the muscle responsible for hip abduction is not innervated by the peroneal nerve. Which localization best explains this combined pattern of findings?","choices":{"A":"Common peroneal nerve palsy at the fibular neck, sparing proximal hip muscles","B":"S1 nerve root radiculopathy, producing a different reflex and muscle pattern","C":"L5 nerve root radiculopathy, affecting multiple peripheral nerve distributions","D":"Femoral neuropathy, affecting hip flexion and knee extension","E":"Sciatic nerve palsy at the level of the sciatic notch, sparing hip abduction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because the L5 nerve root contributes fibers to both the peroneal-innervated muscles producing foot drop and, through an entirely separate peripheral nerve, the gluteus medius that abducts the hip, weakness in both distributions together localizes the lesion to the L5 nerve root itself rather than to an isolated peripheral nerve; a common peroneal nerve palsy would produce foot drop but spare hip abduction, since the peroneal nerve does not supply the gluteus medius, and S1 radiculopathy, femoral neuropathy, and sciatic nerve palsy each produce a different combination of muscle and sensory findings that does not match this vignette.","boardPrep":"Step 1: foot drop with weak hip abduction is the classic board distinction between L5 radiculopathy, root-level, and isolated common peroneal nerve palsy, nerve-level, since only the root supplies both muscle groups through separate nerves. COMLEX: checking hip abduction strength is a quick bedside maneuver to separate a root lesion from a more distal, isolated peroneal nerve injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 10 — reflex, tone, fasciculation, and sensory findings used for lesion localization, tested here as a board-style comparative-localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"}]};
